3900:
** παρά-πτωμα**, ατος, τό,
**false step, slip, blunder**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.10.6, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1251.14 (_plural_), 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 36.2.}}
**2** **defeat**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.100.}}
**3** **transgression, trespass**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Eze.14.13, +others, NT.Matt.6.14, NT.Gal.6.1, +others}}
**4** **error in amount of payments,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 5.91 (2nd c.BC).}}

3901:
** παραρρέω**,
_aorist_ -ερρύην (see. below): _perfect_ -ερρύηκα (see. below):— **flow beside, by**, or **past**, τὴν Νίνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.150; τὰς Πλαταιάς 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.2.31; παρὰ πόλιν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.20, etc.: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 6: proverbial, ὕδωρ παραρρέει, of those who promise to spare no effort, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 60, compare 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 109.2.}}
**II** **slip off** or **out**, εἴ τί μοι τόξων.. παρερρύηκεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 653}}; ὅτῳ μὴ παραρ ρυείη [ἡ χιών] whom it did not **slip off**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.4.11: _metaphorically_, πολλὰ ὑμῖν παρέρρει}} many points **escaped** you, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 781a; φιλοσοφίαν καὶ ῥητορικὴν παρερρυηκυίας}}**having disappeared from memory, Gp.Prooem.** {{Refs|4.}}
**II.2** of persons, π. τῶν φρενῶν **slip away from** one's senses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 357.6}}; also, **to be careless, neglect** advice, etc., υἱὲ μὴ παραρρυῇς {{LXX+NT|LXX.Pro.3.21; μήποτε παραρρυῶμεν NT.Heb.2.1.}}
**III** **run off**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 866a9}}; π. παρὰ (variant{πρὸς}) τὴν ἀρτηρίαν **slip** into the windpipe, {{Refs|[prev. author] “PA” 664b29.}}
**III.2** **slip in unawares** or **by stealth**, λόγοι παρερρυήκασι πρὸς ἡμᾶς ψευδεῖς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 13.16}}; παραρρυεὶς ἄνθρωπος εἰς τὸν νεών {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.969e; ὅθεν παρερρύηκεν ὁ τοιοῦτος λόγος 1st-2nd c.AD(?): Theon Rhetor “Progymnasmata - Rhet.” 6}}; εἴ τι ἐν τῷ τῆς ποιήσεως δρόμῳ παραρρυὲν λάθῃ any irregularity which **slips in**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hes.” 5.}}
**IV** φωναὶ σαθραὶ καὶ παρερρυηκυῖαι cracked and **unsteady**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Audibilibus” 804a32.}}

3902:
** παράσημ-ος**, ον,
(σῆμα) **marked amiss** or **falsely, counterfeit**, especially of money, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.213, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 3.86, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.65b: _metaphorically_, of men, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 518}}; δόξα π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1114 (Lyric poetry); π. ῥήτωρ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.242}}; δύναμις π. αἴνῳ power **falsely stamped** with praise, I. e. praised by a wrong standard, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 780 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** of words and phrases, **false, incorrect,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 11.144 (1st-2nd c.AD(?): Cerealius Epigrammaticus)}}; Ἀττικῶν παράσημος (to be read -σήμων) ἕν (i.e. βιβλίον) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἰδίων βιβλίων” 17}}; τὸ π. **eccentricity** of style, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 208.}}
**3** **marked by the side, noted**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1010d}}; π. τινί **marked, notorious for** a thing, {{Refs|[prev. work]823b, etc.}}; π. ἐπιτηδεύων τι **remarked as** studying it, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Brut.” 2}}; τὸ π. φεύγουσαι (of women) **conspicuousness**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.439.}}
**4** **indicative**, with _genitive_, τὸ π. ὄνομα τῆς πρὸς ὑμᾶς ἔχθρας {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 23.}}
**II** _adverb_ **-μως** **with false accent,** {{Refs|EM 191.34.}}
**II.2** **with a distinguishing prefix**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.12.3 codices}}

3904:
** παρασκευ-ή**, ἡ,
**preparation**, δείπνου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.82}}; παρασκευὴν σίτου παραγγείλας having ordered corn **to be prepared**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.25, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 369e; π. νεῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 190}}; ἐν τούτῳ παρασκευῆς ἦσαν in this state of **preparation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.17}}; **preparation, practice**, as of a speaker preparing his speech, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.6; ἡ π. τῆς πραγματείας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.26.5 (elsewhere προκατασκευή, which see)}}; λέγειν ἀπὸ παρασκευῆς, opposed to αὐτοσχεδίως, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor “De Figuris” 1.2}}; also, in a speech, **preparatory section**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Isaeo” 15 (_plural_); compare παρασκευάζω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 3 near the end}}
**b** with Preps., ἐκ παρασκευῆς **of set purpose, by arrangement**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.19, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.30}}; μάχη ἐγένετο ἐκ π. a **pitched** battle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.56; ἀπὸ παρασκευῆς [prev. author] 1.133; ἀπὸ π. οὐδεμιᾶς 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.22}}; δι᾽ ὀλίγης παρασκευῆς at short **notice**, offhand, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.8; τὸ ναυτικὸν ἐν π. ἦν [prev. author] 2.80}}; ἦσαν ἐν π. πολέμου were engaged in **preparing** for it (compare κατασκευή), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.14; ἐν παρασκευῇ εἶναι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1382b3; μετὰ παρασκευῆς πλείστης ἠδίκησεν [prev. author] “Rh.Al.” 1427a4; ἄνευ παρασκευῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 326a.}}
**2** **providing, procuring**, φίλων καὶ οὐσίας {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 361b}}; ὑγιείας σώματι π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 962a}}; **way** or **means of providing**, τίς.. τέχνη τῆς π. τοῦ μηδὲν ἀδικεῖσθα; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 510a}}; δύ᾽ εἶναι τὰς π. ἐπὶ τὸ θεραπεύειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 513d; in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 457, λευκὴν.. χροιὰν εἰς παρασκευὴν ἔχεις, ἐκ π. should be read.}}
**3** **intrigue, cabal**, for the purpose of gaining a verdict or carrying a measure, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 185, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.79, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.1, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.75, +others, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.32.}}
**II** **that which is prepared, equipage**, πλοῦτοί τε καὶ πᾶσα ἡ τοιαύτη π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 495a, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.14.}}
**II.2** frequently in military sense (see. above {{Refs|1.1)}}**armament**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.107, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.31, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 1.13, “HG” 5.2.23}}; ἵπποι καὶ ὅπλα καὶ ἡ ἄλλη π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.100, compare 5.17}}; γίγνεσθαι τὰς παρασκευὰς ἐποίης αI got the **armaments** ready for service, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.102}}; αἱ πρὸς πόλεμον π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1383b3.}}
**II.3** generally, **power, means**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1}}; **natural equipment**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.2.30, 2.19.30; φύσις καὶ π. [prev. work] 4.8.42, compare 1.6.37}}; of the **physical constitution** of a new-born infant, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.32}}; of mental faculties, **predisposition**, δύναμις καὶ π. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.6.3.}}
**III** among the Jews, **the day of Preparation**, before the sabbath of the Passover, {{NT|NT.Mark.15.42, NT.John.19.14, 31, etc.; ἡμέρα παρασκευῆς NT.Luke.23.54.}}

3905:
** παρατείνω**,
_future_ **-τενῶ**{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}: _perfect_ **-τέτᾰκα**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.832f}}:—**stretch out along, beside**, or **near**, χεῖρες παρατεταμέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 13; βραχίων παρὰ τὰς πλευρὰς παρατεταμένος [prev. author] “Art.” 1}}; **extend, deploy**, τὴν φάλαγγα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.48; π. ἔλυτρον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.185; παρετέτατο ἡ τάφρος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.15}}: —_passive_, **extend along** (see. below {{Refs|11.1)}}; **to be stretched at length, laid low**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 213.}}
**2**. **stretch on the rack, torture**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.135d: _metaphorically_, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.11}}:—_passive_, **to be tortured**, λιμῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 207b}}; **to be worn out**, with _participle_, παρετάθη μακρὰν σδὸν πορευθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.13.6}}; παρατέταμαι λιπαρὰ κάπτων I **am nigh dead** with eating dainties, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 506.1; γελῶντες.. ὀλίγου παρετάθησαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 303b; παραταθήσεται ὑπὸ σοῦ.. θαμὰ λέγοντος [prev. author] “Ly.” 204c}}; but πολιορκίᾳ παρατενεῖσθαι ἐς τοὔσχατον **will strain themselves** to the uttermost, **hold out** to the last, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.46.}}
**3**. **prolong, protract**, τοὺς λόγους {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1455b2}}; μῦθον{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 1451b38; ἐπὶ πλεῖον τὴν διάσκεψιν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Icar.” 29, compare “DMort.” 4.2; τὴν ἀκρόασιν [prev. author] “Im.” 13}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Am.” 4, etc.; ἐνεστῶτος τοῦ παρατεινομένου 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 253.3.}}
**3.b**. **delay**, τὴν ἀπόδοσιν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 237 viii 10 (2nd c.AD).}}
**4**. **apply** a figure to a straight line, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 87a: _absolutely_, [prev. author] “R.” 527a.}}
**5**. κοιλίαν π. **relax** the bowels, Philistio cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.115e.}}
**6**. **lengthen in pronunciation**, ὄνομα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Luct.” 13}}; **prolong** a sound, of echo, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Dom.” 3.}}
**II**. _intransitive_, **stretch** or **lie beside** or **along**, of a wall, a line of country, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.180: with _accusative_ loci, τὰ πρὸς τὴν ἑσπέρην φέροντα ὁ Καύκασος παρατείνει [prev. work] 203, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.8}}:—_passive_, παρατέταται ὄρος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.8, compare 4.38; ἡ δέ γ᾽ Εὔβοια.. ἡδὶ παρατέταται (with a pun on signification 1.1 in next line), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 212; also παρατείνειν παρὰ τὰ μέρη 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.31.5: with _dative_, π. τῷ κόλπῳ 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.2.2: so _metaphorically_, ψυχὴ μικρῷ σώματι -τείνουσα Demetrius Astrologus Lac. “Herc.” 1055.10.}}
**II.2**. **extend**, ἀπὸ τοῦ ἐντέρου κάτω π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 529a22}}; **extend over**, πάντας χρόνους καὶ τόπους {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.11 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}
**II.3**. of Time, **extend**, ἐνιαυτοῦ μῆκος π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 15.7.4; παρατείνοντος τοῦ πότου 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 8.5; πόλεμος π. εἰς ἔτη μ 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Συριακή” 48}}; **continue** one's life, **live**, ἕως.., μέχρι.., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.832f, 839e; ἐπὶ τρεῖς γενεάς 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Macr.” 3.}}
**II.4**. as aux. Verb, with _participle_, ποῖ παρατενεῖς δεδιὼς ταῦτ; how **long will you go on** fearing this ? {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 7.22, compare 5.26.}}

3906:
** παρατηρ-έω**,
**watch closely**, θεία φύσις -τηροῦσα τὰς τῶν ζῴν ἐξόδους {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.54U., compare 1st c.AD: Cebes Philosophus 9}}; of a general, π. τόπους {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.29.4}}; σφᾶς αὐτοὺς π. {{Refs|[prev. author] 11.9.9}}; **observe**, -τετηρηκότες μόνον (opposed to scientific explanation) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.248 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus: followed by an _interrogative_ clause, π. τινά, ὁπότερα.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.14.4; π. τίς ἔπταρεν 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 100.1}}: by a _participle_. π. [ὄρνιθα] ἀποδυόμενον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 620a8}}; with evil design, **lie in wait for, watch one's opportunity**, _absolutely_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1384b7; in argument, [prev. author] “Top.” 161a23}}; ἐνεδρεύειν καὶ π. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.3.2; π. καιροὺς εἰς κόλασιν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.43}} W.:—_middle_, παρετηροῦντο αὐτὸν οἱ Φαρισαῖοι {{NT|NT.Luke.6.7: _absolutely_, Vett. Val. 205.13}}:—_passive_, ὑπό τινος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Dicaearchus Geographus 1.16}}; **to be kept under ob servation**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 542.6.}}
**2**. **take care**, ὅπως μὴ.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.161}}; π. ἵνα.. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 53.}}
**3**. **observe carefully**, τὸ μέτριον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1405b33}}:—Med, **observe religiously**, ἡμέρας καὶ μῆνας {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Gal.4.10; τὴν τῶν σαββάτων ἡμέραν 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 14.10.25: generally, ὅσα προστάττουσιν [οἱ νόμοι] ἀκριβῶς π. 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 53.10.}}

3907:
** παρατήρ-ησις**, εως, ἡ,
**observation**, διειλημμένοι εἰς παρατήρησιν kept under **surveillance**, Aen.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Tact. 10.25; παρατηρήσεις ἄστρων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus 1.28, compare 5.31; π. παθέων ἀλλοτρίων “IG” 42(1).687.14 (Epid., 2nd c.AD)}}; ποιεῖσθαι τὴν π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.36 3b}}; μετὰ παρατηρήσεως so that **it can be observed, ** NT.Luke.17.20: in bad sense, **close observation**, to detect faults, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.22.8}}; ἐνέδρα καὶ π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.266b}}; **empirical observation**, opposed to λογισμός, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.127}}; so κατὰ ἱστορίαν ἢ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.40 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**2**. **observance** of rules, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 629.21.}}
**3**. **remark, note**, παρατηρήσεως ἄξια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 23.2, cf. Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1258}}; ψιλὴ π. bare **notice**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 41.8.}}

3908:
** παρατίθημι**,
_Doric dialect_ and _poetry_ **παρτίθημι** (late forms from παρατίθω {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.333, “Tab.Defix.Aud.” 26.27)}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ παρτιθεῖ, παρατιθεῖ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.192, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.73}}: _imperfect_ **-ετίθει**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 85, “Eq.” 1223:}}_aorist_ _active_ παρέθηκα, _middle_ παρεθέμην: _perfect_ παρατέθεικα: in _Attic dialect_ παράκειμαι generally serves as the _passive_:—**place beside**, πὰρ δὲ τίθει δίφρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.177, compare 182 (tmesis), “Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.167 (from Cyrene)}}, etc.; [εἰκόσι] κόσμον {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 90.40 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC).}}
**b**. frequently of meals, **set before, serve up**, σφιν δαῖτ᾽ ἀγαθὴν παραθήσομεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.810, compare 9.90 (tmesis); ἥ οἱ βρῶσίν τε πόσιν τε παρτιθεῖ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.192; πὰρ δ᾽ ἐτίθει σπλάγχνων μοίρας 20.260; [νῶτα βοὸς] γέρα πάρθεσαν αὐτῷ 4.66; νῦν οἱ παράθες ξεινήϊα καλά 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.408; ξείνιά τ᾽ εὖ παρέθηκεν 11.779, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.517 (tmesis); θεὰ παρέθηκε τράπεζαν 5.92: with _genitive_, τῷ νεκρῷ πάντων παρατιθεῖ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.73, compare 1.119 (_passive_)}}; παρετίθεσαν ἐπὶ τὴν τράπεζαν κρέα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.31}}; οἱ παρατιθέντες **the serving-men**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.8.20}}; τὰ παρατιθέμενα **meats set before one** (with or without βρώματα), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 2.1.30, 5.2.16: in Comedy texts, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 85, “Eq.” 52, 57, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristomenes Comicus 12, etc.}}; of a sacrificial meal, σκέλος τοῦ πράτου βοὸς παρθέντω τῷ θιῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).41.11 (Epid., 5th-4th c.BC).}}
**c**. of a mother, **put to the breast**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.105.}}
**2**. generally, **provide, furnish**, αἲ γὰρ ἐμοὶ.. θεοὶ δύναμιν παραθεῖεν (variant{περιθεῖεν}) oh that they **would place** power **at my disposal !**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.205}}; π.ἑκάστων τῶν σοφῶν ἀπογεύσασθαι, i. e. π. ἕκαστα τὰ σοφὰ ὥστε ἀπογεύσασθαι αὐτῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 157c; π. αὐτοῖς.. ἀναγιγνώσκειν.. ποιήματα [prev. author] “Prt.” 325e}}:—_middle_, **expose for sale**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 622b34.}}
**3**. **place upon**, στεφάνους παρέθηκε καρήατι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 577 (nisi to be read περίθηκε).}}
**4**. **lay before** one, **explain**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.14}}; π. ἔν τισι ὡς οὐ χρή.. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 2110.6 (4th c.AD)}}; **allege, produce**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 9.32; ὑποδείγματα 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.79 K.; παραβολὴν π. αὐ τοῖς NT.Matt.13.24}}:—_middle_, see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.}}
**5**. **put** or **provide side by side**, ὁμοῦ λύπας ἡδοναῖς π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 47a}}; παρατεθείσης τῆς ἀπολογίας (i.e. τῇ κατηγορίᾳ) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demades Orator 6}}; **set side by side, compare**, τινά τινι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 12.}}
**5.b**. Grammars, **place side by side, juxtapose** (opposed to συντίθημι **form a compound**), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 42.5, +others (_passive_).}}
**6**. **deposit**, ={παρακατατίθημι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 8.4 (assuming variant), see below 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 2.}}
**B**. _middle_, **set before oneself, have set before one**, ἐπὴν δαΐδας παραθεῖτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.105 codices, compare 19.150, 24.140; σκύφος παραθέσθαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 390; τράπεζαν Περσικήν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.130; σῖτον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.6.12}}; οἱ τὰ εὐτελέστερα παρατιθέμενοι those **who fare** less sumptuously, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hier.” 1.20}}; **have** meat **set before** others, ἠῶθεν δέ κεν ὔμμιν ὁδοιπόριον παραθείμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.506}}; **provide for oneself, supply oneself with**, παρετίθεντο τῶν ἀναγκαίων πρὸς τὸν πόλεμον, ὅσα.. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 26.}}
**B.2**. **deposit what belongs to one** in another's hands, **give in charge**, τοῦ παραθεμένου τὰ χρήματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 686}}. β; τὴν οὐσίαν ταῖς νήσοις π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 2.16; τῶν ἀβακείων ἃ παρεθέμεθα παρ᾽ αὐτῷ “PCair.Zen.” 71 (3rd c.BC),compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.17.10, “PGrenf.” 1.14.1 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}; **deposit** deeds or documents, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 237 iv 38 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; **give a person in charge to**, τινὶ ὀρφανόν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.8.22}}; **commend** or **commit** into another's hands, εἰς χεῖράς σου τὸ πνεῦμα {{NT|NT.Luke.23.46; τινὰς τῷ Κυρίῳ NT.Act.14.23, compare 20.32, NT.1Pet.4.19}}; **commend** by a letter of introduction, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGiss.” 88.5 (2nd c.AD).}}
**B.2.b**. **store up** in one's mind, ἅ τις ὁρᾷ π. παρ᾽ αὑτῷ {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.4.8.}}
**B.3**. **venture, stake, hazard**, σφὰς γὰρ παρθέμενοι κεφαλάς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.237; τοίτ᾽ ἀλόωνται ψυχὰς παρθέμενοι 3.74, compare 7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 12.18.}}
**B.4**. **apply** something **of one's own to** a purpose, **employ** it, ὄψιν ἐν τῷ διανοεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 65e.}}
**B.5**. **cite in one's own favour, cite as evidence** or **authority**, π. μῦθον, παράδειγμα, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Plt.” 275b, 279a}}; ἀντίγραφον [ἐπιστολῆς] {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“BGU” 1004.12 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἀποδείξεις Wilcken {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Chr.” 77.5 (2nd c.AD)}}; ψήφισμα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.833e, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator Chr. 17.10, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.479c, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 1.3, etc.}}; **mention**, ἔννοιάν τινος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 65.9}}; ἐκδόσεις π. **quote** editions, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pron.” 89.22}}: _absolutely_, **quote instances**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 52.7, +others}}:—rarely in _active_, λέξεις π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 37, see reading in [prev. author] “Comp.” 23.}}
**B.6**. **affix, apply a name**, τῷ χωρίῳ ὄνομα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 2.14.4.}}
**B.7**. **explain, allege**, Wilcken {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Chr.” 20 iii 12 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}

3909:
** παρατυγχάνω**,
_perfect_ -τέτευχα and -τετύχηκα (see. below): _aorist_ παρέτῠχον: — **happen to be near, be among**, παρετύγχανε μαρναμένοισιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.74}}; π. τῷ λόγῳ, τῷ πάθεϊ, **to be present at**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.236, 9.107; εἰς καιρόν γε παρατετύχηκεν ἡμῖν ἐν τοῖς λόγοις Πρόδικος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 340e; τῇ μάχῃ 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.70.7; οἱ -τετυχηκότες τοῖς κινδύνοις [prev. author] 12.28A. 5}}; but ὁ πλείστοις κινδύνοις -τετευχώς **who had met with**.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 12.27.8}}: with Preps., **visit**, εἰς κώμην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 76.11 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἐπὶ διάγνωσιν **put in an appearance** at.., Mitteis {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Chr.” 89.18 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2** _absolutely_, **happen to be present**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59, 6.108}}; of things, **offer, present itself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 38; παρατυχούσης τινὸς σωτηρίας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.19; ἕως ἄν τις παρατύχῃ διαφυγή [prev. author] 8.11; λαβόντας ὅ τι ἑκάστῳ παρέτυχεν ὅπλον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 474a.}}
**3** frequently in _participle_ παρατυχών, **whoever chanced to be by**, i. e. **the first comer, any chance person**, οὐκ ἐκ τοῦ παρατυχόντος πυνθανόμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.22; σὺν τοῖς π. ἱππόταις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.18}}; also τὸ παρατυγχάνον or παρατυχόν **whatever turns up** or **chances**, ποιεῖν τὸ παρατυγχάνον αὐτῷ to do **whatever circumstances required**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.Mag.” 9.1}}; πρὸς τὸ παρατυγχάνον as **circumstances** required, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.122; ἐν τῷ παρατυχόντι [prev. author] 5.38}}; ἀποκρίνασθαι ἐκ τοῦ παρατυχόντος answer **offhand**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.154a: παρατυχόν}}, _absolutely_, **it being in one's power, since it was in one's power** to do, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.76; ἐν καλῷ π. σφίσι ξυμβαλεῖν [prev. author] 5.60.}}

3910:
** παραυτίκᾰ**,
_adverb_ ={πάραυτα}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 767, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.89, 6.35, etc.; ἢ καὶ π. ἢ χρόνῳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 273}}; τὸ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.19, 7.137, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 833, etc.}}; ἐκ τοῦ π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 20}}; ἐν τῷ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 240b, etc.}}
**2** with _Substantives_, Ἅιδην τὸν π. ἐκφυγεῖν **present** death, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 13}}; ἡ π. λαμπρότης **momentary** splendour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.64; ἡ π. ἐλπίς [prev. author] 8.82; αἱ π. ἡδοναί 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.5.9, 8.1.32; τὸ π. ἡδύ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 239a.}}

3911:
** παραφέρω**,
_poetry_ **παρφέρω**, _future_ παροίσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1675 (Lyric poetry):}} —_passive_, _aorist 1_ παροισθέντι· παρενεχθέντι, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}:—**bring to** one's **side**, especially of meats, **serve, set before** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.119, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.6, etc.; π. ποτήρια 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 466; πάρφερε τὸν σκύφον 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 15}}; τὰς κεφαλὰς π. **exhibit** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.65; μάστιγάς τε καὶ κέντρα π. ἐς μέσον [prev. author] 3.130}}:—_passive_, **to be set on table, served**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.133; τοῦ ἀεὶ παραφερομένου ἀπογεύονται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 354b}}; τὰ π. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 26.}}
**2** **bring forward, allege**, **cite**, νόμον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.8, compare “PFlor.” 48.8 (3rd c.AD)}}; π. καινὰ καὶ παλαιὰ ἔργα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.26; λόγους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 981, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1675 (Lyric poetry); π. αὑτὸν ἐν σκώμματος μέρει 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.126}}, compare 132; πίστεις π. τοῦ μὴ.. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.27}}; μάρτυρα Eust.ad {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 306, compare “PAmh.” 2.81.12 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**3** **hand over**, ξύνθημα παρφέροντι ποιμέσιν λόχων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1140.}}
**4**_passive_, **come up, hasten along**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 534a3.}}
**II** **carry beside**, [λαμπάδας] ἵπποις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 724.}}
**III** **carry past** or **beyond**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 515a, etc.}}; π. τὴν χεῖρα **wave** the hand, of gesture in speaking, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.232}}; π. τὸν βραχίονα παρὰ τὰς πλευράς **swing** it in a vertical plane parallel to the sides, opposed to lifting the elbow outwards, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 12}}:—_passive_, **to be carried past** or **beyond**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.135; δρόμῳ παρενεχθέντας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 35, compare “Sull.” 29}}; πρὸς κοντὸν π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Dio” 25}}; τοῦ χειμῶνος παραφερομένου **while** it **was passing**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pel.” 10.}}
**III.2** **turn aside** or **away**, ἑκάστου π. τὴν ὄψιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.27}}; π. τοὺς ὑσσούς **put** them **aside**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 41}}; **put away, avert**, ποτήριον ἀπό τινος {{NT|NT.Mark.14.36}}; but also, **turn towards** an object, κάτω ὁρᾶν καὶ μηκέτι παρενεγκεῖν τὸν ὀφθαλμόν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMeretr.” 10.2; τὴν αὐτὴν αἴσθησιν παραφέρω πρὸς ἑκάτερον 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 414.}}
**III.3** _passive_, **move in a wrong direction**, of paralysed limbs, τὸ παραφερόμενον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1102b22}}; π. ἐν ταῖς χερσίν, of feigned madness, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.21.13}}; π. τοῖς σκέλεσι, of a drunken man, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.183}}; τὸ βλέμμα παρενήνεκται **is distorted**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.112B.}}
**III.4** **mislead, lead astray**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.41d:}} —_passive_, παραφέρεσθαι τῷ τέρποντι πρὸς τὸ βλάπτον{{Refs|[prev. work] 15d}}; **err, go wrong**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 38d, 60d; ἴσως μὲν ἀληθοῦς τινος ἐφαπτόμενοι, τάχα δ᾽ ἂν καὶ ἄλλοσε -φερόμενοι [prev. author] “Phdr.” 265b}}; παρενεχθείς (i.e. τῆς γνώμης) **mad**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 1.21.}}
**III.5** **change**, γνώμην **alter** the text of a decree, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 3.61}}; παρενεχθέντος τοῦ ὀνόματος{{Refs|[prev. work] 2.68; π. τὸ πεπρωμένον [prev. author] “Syr.” 58.}}
**IV** **sweep away**, of a river, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 28, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.35 (_passive_)}}; τοῦ χρόνου καθάπερ ῥεύματος ἕκαστα π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.432b}}:—_passive_, **to be carried away**, σέ, Βάκχε, φέρων ὑπὸ σοῦ τἄμπαλι παρφέρομαι {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.26 (6th c.AD: Marcus Argentarius Epigrammaticus).}}
**V** **let pass**, τὰς ὥρας παρηνέγκατε τῆς θυσίας Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.53}}; **let slip**, τὸ ῥηθέν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aratus” 43}}:— _passive_, **slip away, escape**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.24.}}
**VI** **overcome, excel**, τινά τινι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Charid.” 19.}}
**B** _intransitive_, **to be beyond** or **over**, ἡμερῶν ὀλίγων παρενεγκουσῶν, ἡμέρας οὐ πολλὰς παρενεγκούσας, a few days **over, more or less**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.20, 26.}}
**B.2** **differ, vary**, as dialects, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Xanthus Historicus 1}}; **to be altered**, παρενεγκόντος τοῦ ὀνόματος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Conon Historicus 46.4; παραφέροντα ἢ κατ᾽ ἄλλον τρόπον διαλλάττοντα 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 20}}; π. παρά τι **differ from**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 59.5; πρὸς τὴν ἀλήθειαν 4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.237 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}

3912:
** παραφρον-έω**,
**to be beside oneself, deranged**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.109, 3.34, 35, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 806, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 815, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nu.” S 44, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.9, etc.}}: _poetry_ **παραιφρ-** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.262 (variant).}}
**2** **to be delirious**, variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 10}}. παραφρόν-ησις, εως, ἡ, ={παραφροσύνη}, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Zech.12.4, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 18, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus}} _middle_ in {{Refs|“Rh.Mus.” 58.70.}}

3913:
** παραφρον-έω**,
**to be beside oneself, deranged**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.109, 3.34, 35, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 806, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 815, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nu.” S 44, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.9, etc.}}: _poetry_ **παραιφρ-** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.262 (variant).}}
**2** **to be delirious**, variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 10}}. παραφρόν-ησις, εως, ἡ, ={παραφροσύνη}, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Zech.12.4, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 18, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus}} _middle_ in {{Refs|“Rh.Mus.” 58.70.}}

3916:
** παραχρῆμα**,
_adverb_ for παρὰ τὸ χρῆμα (compare χρέος) **on the spot, forthwith**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.15, 7.150, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.17, etc.}}; πάλαι τε καὶ οἱ πλεῖστοι π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.75; εἰ καὶ μὴ π., ἀλλ᾽ ὀλίγον ὕστερον 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.64}}; ταχέως καὶ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 6}}; **at the moment**, π. τέρψασαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 6.23 (nisi divisim scribendum)}}; τὰ π. **the present**, opposed to τὰ μέλλοντα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.138}}; ἡ π. ἀνάγκη **present** necessity, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.17}}; τὸ π. περιχαρές{{Refs|[prev. work] 51; τὸ π. περιδεές [prev. author] 8.1}}; τὸ π. ἡδύ **immediate** pleasure, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356a; αἱ π. ἡδοναί 5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista 58: with the _Article_, τὸ π. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.11, etc.}}; ἐκ τοῦ π. λέγειν to speak **offhand**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 399d, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.1; ἐκ τοῦ π. στρατεύεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.11; αἱ ἐκ τοῦ π. ἡδοναί [prev. author] “Mem.” 2.1.20}}; λέγειν ἀπὸ τοῦ π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 1.1.30}}; ἐν τῷ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.73, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 455a, etc.; ἐς τὸ π. ἀκούειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.22; ἀσθενεῖς εἰς τὸ π. γίγνονται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 646c.—More frequently in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}} and _Attic dialect_ Prose than in later writers (hence explained by {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus παραχρῆμα· παραυτίκα.., εὐθέως),compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 106.3, “SIG” 577.12 (Milet., 3rd-2nd c.BC), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 42; νόμοι ἐκ τοῦ π. κείμενοι 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator}} **ad Them.**262a; ἐκ τοῦ π. εἰπεῖν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.6e, compare 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 18.2}}; ἐν τῷ π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.407 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus:}} —hence Cobet treats ἄρτι, εὐθύς, εὐθέως, when joined with παραχρῆμα as glosses, π. ἄρτι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.4.14}}; εὐθέως π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.20}}; εὐθὺς π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.11, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.94, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 48.40.}}
**2** in later writers, with _genitive_, π. τῆς εὐεργεσίας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 11.130; π. τῆς διαβάσεως 4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.240 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, compare “Geoponica” 10.75.16.}}

3917:
** πάρδᾰλις**
or **πόρδᾰλις** (see. below), ἡ, _genitive_ εως, _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ ιος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.20, etc.}}; _dative_ ει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 347}}:— **leopard, Felis pardus**, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.103, 17.20, 21.573, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.457, “hymnus ad Venerem” 71, 7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus (?)in “PLit.Lond.” 53r. 3, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 500a28, “Phgn.” 809b36, 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 3.63, etc.—Acc. to 1st c.AD: Apion Grammaticus (cited in 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus “Lex.” see at {πόρδαλις}) πόρδαλις was the male, πάρδαλις the female,compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}]; but πόρδαλις was used of the animal, παρδαλέη of its skin, _accusative_ to {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Etymologicum Magnum” 652.38, 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.; 3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus wrote πάρδ- in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus has πάρδ- “Nu.” [same place], but πόρδ- “Lys.” 1015}}; πάρδ- is found in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 196e, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.5.2, 6.17.9, and always in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}}; πόρδ- in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 296.}}
**II** a ravenous sea-fish, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 9.49, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 1.368.}}
**III** ={πάρδαλος}{{Refs 5th c.AD+|11, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**IV** name of a **plaster**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 12.1.}}

3918:
** πάρειμι**
(εἰμί **sum**), _infinitive_ -εῖναι, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ παρέᾱσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.192, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.247}}; _Ionic dialect_ _subjunctive_ παρέω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.98}}; _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ παρέμμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.640}}, _participle_ παρεών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.475}}: _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ παρέην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.267 (tmesis)}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ παρῆας variant in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.497 (Scholia, “Legal icon Messanense”)}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ πάρεσαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.75}}; _Attic dialect_ _imperfect_ παρῆ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 523}}; in later Greek παρήμην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 2.25}}: _Epic dialect_ _future_ παρέσσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.393}}:—**to be by** or **present**, ὑμεῖς θεαί ἐστε πάρεστέ τε ἴστε τε πάντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.485, etc.: in tmesi, πὰρ δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἔην καὶ ἀοιδός 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.267}}; πάρα used for πάρεστι and πάρεισι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.98, 23.479, etc.}}: frequently in _participle_, ποίπνυον παρεόντε {{Refs|24.475; σημάντορος οὐ π. 15.325}}, etc.; ἀπεόντα νόῳ παρεόντα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 2.1, compare 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 34.}}
**2** **to be by** or **near** one, with _dative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.105; μήλοισι 4.640; π. τινὶ παροινοῦντι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.1.7; π. παρά τινι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1056}}; π. τινί **to be** his **guest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 131.}}
**3** **to be present in** or **at**, μάχῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.497; ἐν δαίτῃσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.217}}; δόμοις π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 805; τοῖς πράγμασιν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.2, etc.; ἐν λόγῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 513; ἐν ταῖς συνουσίαις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 335b; ἐπὶ τοῖς ἀγῶσι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.159.}}
**4** **to be present so as to help, stand by**, τινι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.472, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.393, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 235; πλησίον παρῆσθα κινδύνων ἐμοί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1159, etc.}}; especially of one accused, οἱ νῦν παρόντες αὐτῷ καὶ συνδικοῦντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.12, compare 24.159: in Medicine texts, of nurses, assistants, etc., 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 1.1, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus}} _middle_ cited in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 10.37.11.}}
**5** παρεῖναι εἰς.. **to have arrived** at, ἐς κοῖτον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.9; ἐς τὸν Ἰσθμὸν π. τινί [prev. author] 8.60. ; ἐς τὴν Λακεδαίμονα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88; εἰς τὴν ἐξέτασιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.11; Ὀλυμπίαζε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.8: with _accusative_ loci, πάρεισι.. Αἰτναῖον πάγον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 95, compare 106, “Ba.” 5; π. τινὶ ἐπὶ δεῖπνον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.118, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 131; π. ἐπὶ τὸ στράτευμα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.35}}; π. πρὸς τὴν κρίσιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 6.6.26; πρός τινα [prev. author] “Cyr.” 2.4.21; also π. ἐνταυθοῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 33d}}; see at {πάρειμι} (εἶμι) IV.2.
**6** π. ἐκ.. **to have come** from.., ἐκ ταύτης [τῆς πόλιος] π. ἐς τὴν Ἀσίην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.24; τοὐκ θεοῦ παρόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1540}}; Φίλιππος ἐκ Θρᾴκης π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.101; Θείβαθεν αὐληταὶ πάρα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 862.}}
**II** of things, **to be by**, i.e. **ready** or **at hand**, τά τε δμώεσσι πάρεστι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.80, etc.; πάρα ἔργα βόεσσιν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 454; οὐ γάρ οἱ πάρα νῆες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.559}}; εἴ μοι δύναμίς γε παρείη if power **were at** my **command**,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|2.62; ὅση δύναμίς γε πάρεστι 23.128; ὅ τι πάρεστι 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 62}}; τὰ παρεόντα what is **ready**, χαριζομένη παρεόντων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.140; ἡ τοῦ πλέονος ἐπιθυμίη τὸ παρεὸν ἀπόλλυσι 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 224, compare 191}}; ἐκ τῶν παρεουσέων αὐγέων the best light **available**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 3; ἐκ τῶν παρεόντων τὸ εὔπορον εὑρίσκειν [prev. author] “Art.” 78; εἰ τὰ δεσμὰ μὴ παρείη [prev. passage]}} of feelings, conditions, etc., φόβος βαρβάροις παρῆν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 391; θαῦμα παρῆν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 254; ἐν τοῖς τότε παρεοῦσι.. κακοῖσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.20, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 26; ὡς παρεσομένου σφι πολέμου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.20: in Philos., of qualities or predicates, παρείη γ᾽ ἂν αὐταῖς (i.e. θριξίν) λευκότης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 217d, compare 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.6.4}}; of Time, ὁ παρὼν νῦν χρόνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1293, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.93, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1457a18; ἡ νῦν π. ἡμέρα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 683c}}; ἡ ἱερὰ συμβουλὴ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.4}}; τὰ παρόντα (_Ionic dialect_ παρεόντα) **the present state of affairs**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.113, etc.; τὰ π. πρήγματα [prev. author] 6.100}}; opposed to τὰ γεγονότα, τὰ μέλλοντα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 186b: _sing._, τὸ παρόν (_Ionic dialect_ παρεόν), πρὸς τὸ π. βουλεύειν, τὸ π. θεραπεύειν, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.20, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 149 (Lyric poetry); πρὸς παρεόν 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 106: Adverbial phrases, τὸ παρόν}} **just now**, τὸ π. εἴπομεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 693b; τὰ παρόντα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 215 (Lyric poetry): in Prose, ἐκ τῶν π}}. according to **present circumstances**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.40, etc.; ἐν τῷ π., opposed to τὸ ἔπειτα, [prev. work] 63, etc.}}; ἐν τῷ νῦν π. καὶ ἐν τῷ ἔπειτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 67c}}; ἐν τῷ τότε π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.95; πρὸς τὸ παρόν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.94}}; ὡς πρὸς τὸ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.201; πρὸς τὸ π. αὐτίκα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40; πρὸς τὴν π. ὄψιν [prev. author] 2.88}}; ἐπὶ τοῦ π. for **the present,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 9(2).517.6 (Epist. Philipp.), 1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 2.2}}; ἐς and πρὸς τὰ π., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 1.13.5, 5.22.5.}}
**III** _impersonal_, πάρεστί μοι **it depends** on me, **is in** my **power** to do, with _infinitive_, τοιαῦθ᾽ ἑλέσθαι σοι πάρεστιν ἐξ ἐμοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 867, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 364, etc.}}: also _imperfect_ παρῆν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.20, 9.70: without _dative_, παρῆν.. κλύειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 401; πάρεστι χαίρειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 638; ὁρᾶν πάρεστιν 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 164, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.2, etc.}}
**III.2** _participle_ παρόν, _Ionic dialect_ παρεόν, **it being possible** or **easy, since it is allowed**, παρεὸν αὐτῷ βασιλέα γενέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.129, compare 6.72, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1098 (Lyric poetry), “fragment” 564.3, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.19.}}
**IV**. _participle_ _masculine_ παρών is frequently in Trag., at the end of a verse, to give vividness, ἄνδρ᾽ ἐνουθέτει παρών **to his face**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1156}}; τοὺς θανόντας οὐκ ἐᾶς θάπτειν π. you **come here** and forbid.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1131, compare 338, “El.” 300, “Tr.” 422; uncertain in Comedy texts, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 657.}}

3919:
** παρεισ-άγω** [ᾰγ],
_perfect_ παρεισῆχα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 32}}:—**lead in by one's side, bring forward, introduce**, of persons brought into a public assembly, τοὺς παῖδας τῶν ἐν τῷ πολέμῳ τετελευτηκότων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.82; τοὺς αἰχμαλώτους 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.63.2}}; **propose** a candidate for a succession, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 21.}}
**2** with a notion of secrecy, π. [τοὺς Γαλάτας] εἰς Ἔρυκα **introduce, admit** them into the city, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.7.8, compare 1.18.3.}}
**3** **introduce into** a poem or narrative, κινδύνους {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 142, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus}} [same place], etc.; τὸν Ἀννίβαν ἀμίμητόν τινα π. στρατηγόν **represent** him as.., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.47.7, compare 5.2.6, 1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 9:}} —_passive_ {{Refs|[prev. work] 20, +others}}
**4** **introduce** doctrines, customs, etc., τὰς ὑπὲρ τῶν ἐν Ἅιδου διαλήψεις εἰς τὰ πλήθη π. {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.56.12, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.96; ξένα π. δαιμόνια 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.328d; αἱρέσεις NT.2Pet.2.1}}:—_passive_, μουσικὴν παρεισῆχθαι τοῖς ἀνθρώποις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Ephorus Historicus 8J.}}

3921:
** παρεισ-δύνω**,
**slip in, penetrate**, τὸ ἔλαιον π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author = 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus] “Pr.” 881a7: _metaphorically_, εἰς τὰς γνώμας π. 4th c.BC: Demades Orator}} 3: _perfect_ - δέδῡκα, εἰς τὰς ἄλλας διαλέκτους {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 319.24}}:—also **παρεισδύω**, τὰ παρεισδύοντα τῶν διαλέκτων {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pron.” 4.23.}}
**II** _middle_ **παρεισδύομαι**, ἐς τὸ στόμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.86, compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.101, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.653; ἀλλοφυλίας.. κατὰ μικρὸν -δυομένης 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.48U.; εἰς τὴν πόλιν 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.12.1, etc.}}; [τὸ ὕδωρ] παρεισδυόμενον πνίγει {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 933a16}}; of a leech's bite, **penetrate into**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 2.6}}; of customs, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.216b, “Agis” 3, etc.}}

3922:
** παρεισ-έρχομαι**,
with _aorist_ and _perfect_ _active_, **come** or **go in beside**: in Medicine texts, of fingers or instruments, **to be inserted**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(1).323, 332}}: generally, **come in**, ὅπως.. τύχη παρεισέλθῃ {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 281p.351U.; παρεισελθόντες ὡς φίλιοι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.7.3, +others; νόμος παρεισῆλθεν ἵνα πλεονάσῃ τὸ παράπτωμα NT.Rom.5.20; π. ἄφνω πρὸς τὴν ἑστίαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 23}}; **to be introduced**, of a side-issue, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.749}}: with _infinitive_, π. κατασκοπῆσαι {{NT|NT.Gal.2.4.}}
**II** **occur, suggest itself**, of an idea, τινι {{Refs|Vett. Val. 357.9.}}

3924:
** παρεκτός**,
_adverb_ **besides** or **except for** a thing, with _genitive_, {{NT|NT.Matt.5.32, NT.Act.26.29.}}
**II** _absolutely_, χωρὶς τῶν π. besides things **external**, {{NT|NT.2Cor.11.28.}}

3925:
** παρεμ-βολή**, ἡ,
**insertion, interpolation**, ἑτέρων πραγμάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.205, compare 1.166 (_plural_), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.261 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**2** Grammars, **parenthesis**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor “De Figuris” 25, Tiberius Rhetor “de Figuris - Rhet.” 48.}}
**II** **drawing up in battle-order**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.32.6}}; in Tactics, **insertion of men in the ranks** (distinct from παρένταξις and παρεμπλοκή), {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 6.1, 10.17.}}
**II.b** **company** of soldiers, etc., {{LXX|LXX.Gen.32.2(3)}}; **host**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work] 33.8 (_plural_), 2nd c.BC: Ezekiel Poeta Judaeus “Exagoge - Mnemosyne” 81.}}
**II.2** **encampment**, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 57, Theophilus Comicus 9, 2nd c.BC: Crito Comicus 1, LXX.Exo.14.19, +others, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.74.5, +others, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 27}}: generally, **soldiers' quarters**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.29.1}}; **barracks,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Acts.21.34, compare “Ostr.” 901, +others (2nd c.AD)}}; name of an ἄμφοδον, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 2131.8 (3rd c.AD).}}
**III** ={παρεξειρεσία} (which see), {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.7.4 (sed to be read παραβολαί).}}
**IV** in boxing and wrestling, π. βάλλειν **trip** an adversary by a twist of the leg, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ocyp.” 60, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.638f (_plural_).}}

3926:
** παρεν-οχλέω**,
**cause** one **much annoyance**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 13, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1381 b15, “Mem.” 453a16; π. τινὶ περί τινος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.8.1: simply τινι LXX.Judg.14.17, +others, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.86W., NT.Act.15.19.}}
**2** with _accusative_, **annoy**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.37.3, “OGI” 139.16 (Egypt, 2nd c.BC), 5th c.AD: Hierocles Platonicus Philosophus “in Carmen Aureum - FPG” 8p.431M.}}:—_passive_, παρηνώχλησθε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.50; ὑπό τινος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.53.6}}; of disease, ὑπὸ νευρικῆς διαθέσεως {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 331.10 (Pergam., 2nd c.BC).}}

3928:
** παρέρχομαι**
(the other moods of the _present_, and the _imperfect_ (παρήρχοντο is found in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus “fragment” 6.15), as also the}}_future_, are borrowed from πάρειμι (εῖμι **ibo**), compare ἔρχομαι): _aorist_ παρῆλθον, _infinitive_ -ελθεῖν, more rarely **-ήλῠθον**[{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.85 (for παρενθεῖν, see at {παρέρπω} 11)}}]:—**go by, beside**, or **past, pass by**, of a ship, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.357; ἧος μέγα κῦμα παρῆλθεν 5.429}}; of birds, {{Refs|12.62}}; of persons, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1004, etc.}}; [παρῆλθεν ὁ κίνδυνος] ὥσπερ νέφος **passed away**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.188.}}
**2** of Time, **pass**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.86; παρεληλύθει τὰ Διονύσια 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.69}}; ὁ παρελθὼν χρόνος time **past**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1028 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ὁ π. ἄροτος the **past** season, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 69}}; π. ὁδοί wanderings **now gone by**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OC” 1397; οἱ παρεληλυθότες πόνοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 231b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.2; τῆς παρελθούσης νυκτός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310a}}; ἐν τῷ παρελθόντι χρόνῳ in time **past, of old**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.20, etc.}}; τὰ παρεληλυθότα **past events**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.191}}; τὸ παρελθόν, opposed to τὸ μέλλον, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 218a9}}; ὁ παρεληλυθώς (i.e. χρόνος) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Po.” 1457a18, compare “Cat.” 5a8, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 3.106.}}
**IIpass by, outstrip**, especially in speed, τινα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.345; ποσὶν μή τίς με παρέλθῃ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.230}}; π. ἐν δόλοισιν **surpass** in wiles,{{Refs 6th c.BC+|13.291; οὔ με δόλῳ παρελεύσεαι 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1285; δυνάμει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 906 (Lyric poetry); ἀναιδείᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 277}}; π. τῇ πρώτῃ στρατείᾳ **to be superior, have the advantage**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.129; τοὺς λόγους τἄργα παρέρχεται 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.3}}; τὸ ψυχρὸν τοῦτ᾽ ὄνομα, τὸ ἄχρι κόρου, παρελήλυθε has **outdone** that hackneyed phrase, 'to satiety', {{Refs|[prev. author] 19.187.}}
**II.2outwit, elude, 'give the go-by to'**, μὴ δὴ οὕτως.. κλέπτε νόῳ, ἐπεὶ οὐ παρελεύσεαι οὐδέ με πείσεις (unless in _signification_ see) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.132; οὐκ ἔστι Διὸς κλέψαι νόον οὐδὲ παρελθεῖν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 613; φυλακὰς.. ἐούσας οὐδὲν χαλεπὰς παρελθεῖν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.72; π. τὴν πεπρωμένην τύχην 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 695; τὴν ἐν τῷ ὅλῳ ψυχήν 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.7.11; τὰς αἰτίας καὶ τὰς διαβολάς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.7.}}
**III** **pass on and come to** a place, **arrive at**, ἐς τὰ δίκαια {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 216; εἰς τὴν δυναστείαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.24; εἰς τὴν οὐσίαν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Gall.” 12; ἐπὶ τὰ πράγματα [prev. author] “DMort.” 12.4.}}
**III.2passin**, ἐς τὴν αὐλήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.77, 5.92}}.; ἔσω or εἴσω π. **go into** a house, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 849, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1337, etc.; ἔσω θυρῶνος [prev. author] “OT” 1241; εἴσω παρὰ τοὺς γηγενεῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 853: with _accusative_, π. δόμους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1137, “Hipp.” 108}}; of an army, π. εἰς τὴν πόλιν βίᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.5.11; π. εἴσω Πυλῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.35.}}
**III.3** _metaphorically_, εἰς παροιμίαν παρῆλθε τὸ πρᾶγμα **passed** into a proverb, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 593}}; εἰς τὴν τραγικὴν.. ὀψὲ π. [ἡ ὑπόκρισις] {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1403b23.}}
**IVpass without heeding**, τεὸν βωμόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.239}}; **disregard, slight** θεούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 231; νόμους 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.37}}; **pass over, omit**, οὐδὲν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 637, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 278e, etc.}}
**IV.2** **overstep, transgress**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.12, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.52.}}
**Vpass unnoticed, escape the notice of**(see. above {{Refs 6th c.BC+|11.2), mostly of things, πολλά με καὶ συνιέντα π. 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 419; οὐδέ μ᾽ ὄμματος φρουρὰν παρῆλθε τόνδε μὴ λεύσσειν στόλον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 226; τουτὶ γὰρ αὖ μικροῦ παρῆλθέ μ᾽ εἰπεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.110: _absolutely_, ὡς μὴ παρέλθωσ᾽ αἱ κόραι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 902.}}
**VIcome forward** to speak, ἐς τὸν δῆμον π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.45; εἰς τὴν ἐκκλησίαν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.95: frequently _absolutely_, ταῦτα ἔλεγε παρελθὼν ὁ Ἀριστείδης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.81; ὀλίγων ἕνεκα καὐτὴ παρῆλθον ῥημάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 443, compare “Av.” 1612}}; παρελθὼν ἔλεξε τοιάδε, π. εἶπε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.59, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 10; ὁ βουλόμενος παρελθὼν ἐλεγξάτω 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.14.}}
**VII** _perfect_ παρελήλυθα, ={πάρειμι}, **adsum**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.86.}}

3929:
** πάρεσις**, εως, ἡ,
**letting go, dismissal**, τινὸς ἐκ Συρακουσῶν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus}}“Comp.Dion. Brut.” 2; **release**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.37.}}
**IIslackening** of strength, **paralysis**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 4.45; παραπληγίη π. ἁφῆς καὶ κινήσιος 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.7, compare 2.5, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.652e.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, ἡ ἀπὸ τῆς ὕλης π. {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 440.}}
**IIIremission** of debts, χρημάτων π. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 81.1}}; of sins, {{NT|NT.Rom.3.25.}}
**IV** **neglect**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Βασιλική” 13.}}

3930:
** παρέχω**,
_future_ παρέξω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.317, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48, παρασχήτω [prev. author] 6.86, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.71, 15.248:}}_perfect_ παρέσχηκα: _aorist_ παρέσχον, _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ παρασχέμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.147; _imperative_ παράσχες 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 842 (παράσχε is _falsa lectio_)}}; _poetry_ παρέσχεθον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 639}}, _infinitive_ παρασχεθεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 321}}; _Aeolic dialect_ παρέσκεθον {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Oxy.” 1788 “fragment” 15}} ii 11; παρεχέσκετο is _falsa lectio_ for{παρεκέσκετο} in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.521. [In 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.113, πᾱρέχη.]}}
**A** _active_, **hand over**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.556}}; **furnish, supply**, φάος πάντεσσι παρέξω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.317; δῶρα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.147; especially in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”, ἱερήϊα, βρῶσίν τε πόσιν τε, σῖτον, 14.250, 15.490, 18.360: _absolutely_, ἐγὼ δ᾽ εὖ πᾶσι παρέξω}} I **will provide** for all,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|8.39; π. νέας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.83, 7.21; τεταρτημόριον τοῦ μισθώματος [prev. author] 2.180; χρήματα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48}}; ἀργύριον, ποίμνια, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.39.69,45.4; αἱ δὲ Συράκουσαι σῦς.. παρέχουσιν 5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 63.9}}; πληρώμαθ᾽ ἡ πόλις παρέχει the state **finds** men to man the ships, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.155, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.43.}}
**2** of natural objects, **yield, produce**, θάλασσα π. ἰχθῦς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.113}}; [σίδηρον] παρέξει (i.e. σόλος) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.835.}}
**3** of incorporeal things, **afford, cause**, φιλότητα, ἀρετήν, γέλω τε καὶ εὐφροσύνην,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|3.354, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.133, 20.8; ὀνίαις 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 88; π. εἰράναν τισί 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.23; ὕμνον [prev. author] “N.” 6.33; αἶσαν [prev. author] “O.” 6.102; Σάρδεσιπένθος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 322}}; τύχην, φρίκην, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 53, 1306 (_anapaest meter_)}}; χάριν, εὔνοιαν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 1498 (lvr.), “Tr.” 708}}; ὄχλον, πρήγματα π., {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86, +others (see. πρᾶγμα); πόνον 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 19, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.177; ἔργον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 523; π. εὔνοιαν εἴς τινα 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.76}}; αἴσθησιν παρέχει τινός **enables** one to observe a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.50}}; but αἴσθησιν π., _absolutely_, it **causes** remark, is perceived, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.22, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.13}}; πενία ἀνάγκῃ τὴν τόλμαν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.45}}; ὑφειμένου δόξαν π., ={ὑφειμένῳ ἐοικέναι}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.131a.}}
**II** **present** or **offer** for a purpose,
**II.1** with _infinitive_, [ὄϊες] παρέχουσι.. γάλα θῆσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.89; π. τὸ σῶμα τύπτειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 441; τὸ στράτευμα π. τισὶ διαφθεῖραι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.50}}(without_infinitive_, πτήξας δέμας παρεῖχε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 210): with _reflexive_ _pronoun_, ἐμαυτόν σοι ἐμμελετᾶν π}}. I **give** myself **up** to you to practise upon, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 228e; π. ἐμαυτὸν ἐρωτᾶν [prev. author] “Ap.” 33b, compare “Prt.” 312c; π. ἑαυτοὺς τοῖς ἄρχουσι χρῆσθαι ἤν τι δέωνται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.9}}: rarely with a _participle_, π. ἑαυτὸν δεδησόμενον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 35.}}
**II.2** **give oneself up, submit oneself**, ἑαυτόν being omitted, π. [ἑωυτοὺς] διαφθαρῆναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.17}}; πατεῖν παρεῖχετῷ θέλοντι [ἑαυτόν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1146, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 422; τοῖς ἰατροῖς παρέχουσι.. ἀποτέμνειν καὶ ἀποκάειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.54, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 456b}}; τῷ λόγῳ ὥσπερ ἰατρῷ παρέχων ἀποκρίνου{{Refs|[prev. work] 475d, compare “Tht.” 191a; ἕτοιμός εἰμί σοι παρέχειν ἀποκρινόμενος [prev. author] “Prt.” 348a}}; especially of a woman, obscene meaning, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 162, 227, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMeretr.” 5.4, etc. (in full, π. ἑαυτήν [prev. author] “DMar.” 13.1, 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.78).}}
**II.3** with _reflexive_ _pronoun_ and a predicative, **show, exhibit** oneself so and so, π. ἐμαυτὸν ὅσιον καὶ δίκαιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.2}}; σπάνιον σεαυτὸν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 3d}}; σαυτὸν σοφιστὴν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Prt.” 312a; ἑαυτὸν π. εὐπειθῆ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.22}}; μέτριον ἐμαυτὸν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.1; τοιοῦτον πολίτην 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.1; π.ἐν τῷ μέσῳ ἐμαυτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.46; δέμασἀ κέντητον παρέχων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.21.}}
**III** **allow, grant**, σιγὴν παρασχὼν κλῦθί μου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1115: with}}_infinitive_, ἐπεὶ παρέσχες ἀντιφωνῆσαι **did'st allow** me to..,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1114; π. αὐτοὺς δικαστὰς.. γίγνεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.37: _absolutely_ in imperative, πάρεχε}} **make way**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 308, “Cyc.” 203, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1326, “Av.” 1720 (all Lyric poetry); πάρεχ᾽ ἐκποδών [prev. author] “V.” 949.}}
**III.2** _impersonal_, παρέχει τινί with _infinitive_, **it is allowed, in one's power** to do so and so, παρεῖχε ἄν σφι εὐδαιμονέειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.170, compare 3.73, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).76; ὑμῖν οὐ παρασχήσει ἀμύνασθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.86; σωφρονεῖν παρεῖχέ σοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1080: _neuter_}}_participle_ used _absolutely_, παρέχον **it being in one's power, since one can**, like{ἐξόν, παρόν, παρέχον [ὑμῖν] ἄρχειν} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.49}}; also εὖ, καλῶς παρασχόν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.120, 5.14; κάλλιον π. [prev. work] 60.}}
**IV** **produce** a person **on demand**, ἐς τὸ κοινόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.4.38}}; εἰς τὴν βουλήν, εἰς ἀγοράν (to be read αὔριον), εἰς κρίσιν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.23, 23.9, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.117, compare “PHib.” 1.168 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**V** with a _predicate_ added, **make** so and so, τὴν διέξοδόν οἱ ἀσφαλέα π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.4; π. τινὰς βελτίους 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.136, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 274e, 277a}}: with _participle_, π. ξυμμάχους τὰς σπονδὰς δεχομένους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.35, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 21.4}}; κοινὴν τὴν πόλιν π. **offer** it as a common resort, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.52}}; γῆν ἄσυλον καὶ δόμους ἐχεγγύους π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 388, etc.}}
**B** _middle_ παρέχομαι, _future_ **-έξομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.20, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 23.8, etc.; also παρασχήσομαι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.24, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.8}}: _aorist 2_ παρεσχόμην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 3.18, 19}}: _perfect_ _passive_ (in _middle_ sense) παρέσχημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.11, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.49, 36.35}}: frequently used much like _active_, without any _reflexive_ sense:
**B.1** **supply of oneself** or **from one's own means**, νέας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.8, 15, +others; δαπάνην οἰκηΐην [prev. author] 8.17}}; π. ὅπλα **furnish** a suit of armour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.22.11, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.97}}; οἱ τὰ τιμήματα παρεχόμενοι the tax-**paying** citizens, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 39.6}}; μηδεμίαν δύναμιν π. εἰς τὴν στρατιάν **supply** no contingent **of one's own** to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.2.10; frequently with ἑαυτόν, εὔνουν καὶ πρόθυμον ἑαυτὸν παρέχεται “SIG” 333.11 (Samos, 4th-3rd c.BC), compare 620.6 (Tenos, 2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**B.2** of natural objects, **furnish, present, exhibit**, [ποταμὸς] κροκοδείλους π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.44; π. λίμνην ὁ Πόντος.. οὐ πολλῷ τεῳ ἐλάσσω ἑωυτοῦ [prev. work] 86, compare 46, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 81d.}}
**B.3** of works, ἓν ἔργον πολλὸν μέγιστον π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.93.}}
**B.4** of incorporeal things, **display on one's own part**, πᾶσαν προθυμίην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.6, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.11; πᾶν τὸ πρόθυμον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.85, compare 61; εὔνοιαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.10}}; χρείας Decrees cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.84.}}
**B.II** in Law, παρέχεσθαί τινας μάρτυρας, π. τεκμήρια, **bring forward** witnesses or proofs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 19d, “Prm.” 128b, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.11, compare 5.20,22, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 23.8, etc.}}; π. ἐκμαρτυρίαν, μαρτυρίαν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator [same places]}}
**B.III** **produce as one's own**, ἄρχοντα παρέχεσθαί τινα **acknowledge as one's** general, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.61, 62, 67}}; Ἀθηναῖοι ἀρχαιότατον ἔθνος παρεχόμενοι **presenting themselves as**.., {{Refs|[prev. work] 161}}; π.πόλιν μεγίστην, of an ambassador, **represent** a city **in one's own person**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.64, compare 85.}}
**B.IV** **offer, promise**, ἀψευδέα μαντήϊα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.174}}; ἔστιν ἃ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.36}}; **put forward**, τὸ εὐπρεπὲς τῆς δίκης {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.39.}}
**B.V** **render** so and so **for** or **towards oneself**, θεὸν παρασχέσθαι εὐμενῆ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 55; δυσμενεστέρους π. τοὺς ἀνθρώπους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317b, compare “R.” 432a, “Lg.” 809d}}; see above {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus V.}}
**B.VI** Arith., **make up, amount to**, ἐνιαυτοὶ.. παρέχονται ἡμέρας.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.28.}}

3931:
** παρηγορ-ία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, **exhortation, persuasion**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.1281 (_plural_): _metaphorically_, χρίματος.. ἀδόλοισι παρηγορίαις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 95 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ἴση παρηγορία, ={ἰσηγορία}, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 1.17b.}}
**2** **surname**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 4.8.3 (sed to be read προσηγ-).}}
**II** **consolation**, τοῦ πένθους {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cimon” 4, compare “Per.” 34}}; υἱοῖο for his loss, {{Refs|“IG” 7.2544 (from Thebes)}}; ὁδευόντων π., of the moon, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 6.}}
**II.2** **assuagement**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Diocl.fragment 142, etc.; τοῦ παροξυσμοῦ 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “χρονίων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.3.}}

3933:
** παρθένος**,
_Laconian dialect_ **παρσένος** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1263 (Lyric poetry)}}. ἡ, **maiden, girl**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.127, etc.}}; αἱ ἄθλιαι π. ἐμαί my unhappy **girls**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1462, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1302; also γυνὴ παρθένος 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 514}}; π. κόρα, of the Sphinx, uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1730 (Lyric poetry)}}; θυγάτηρ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.9}}; of Persephone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1342 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 804}}; **virgin**, opposed to γυνή, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tr.” 148, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 27.65.}}
**2** of unmarried women who are not virgins, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.514, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.34, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1219, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 530.}}
**3** Παρθένος, ἡ, **the Virgin Goddess**, as a title of Athena at Athens, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.11.10, 10.34.8}} (hence of an _Attic dialect_ coin bearing her head, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 675)}}; of Artemis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 17}}; of the Tauric Iphigenia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.103}}; of an unnamed goddess, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 46.3 (Halic., 5th c.BC), “IG” 12.108.48,54 (Neapolis in Thrace)}}; αἱ ἱεραὶ π., of the Vestal Virgins, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.69, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.89e, etc.}}; αἱ Ἑστιάδες π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cic.” 19}}; simply, αἱ π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.66.}}
**4** the constellation {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Virgo”, 4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus cited in 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.2.5, 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 97, etc.}}
**5** ={κόρη} {{Refs|111}}**pupil**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|X.cited in Longin. 4.4, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.7.}}
**II** as _adjective_, **maiden, chaste**, παρθένον ψυχὴν ἔχων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1006, compare 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 33}}; μίτρη π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“epigram Gr.” 319: _metaphorically_, π. πηγή 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 613.}}
**III** as _masculine_, παρθένος, ὁ, **unmarried man,** {{NT|NT.Rev. 14.4.}}
**IV** π. γῆ **Samian earth** {{Refs|compare (παρθένιος 111), “PMag.Berol.” 2.57.}}

3935:
** παρίημι**,
_future_ παρήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.161, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1193}}: _aorist 1_ παρῆκα {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 570}}: _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _aorist 2_ παρεῖσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.44}}; _participle_ παρείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 732:}}_perfect_ παρεῖκα (see. below); παρῆκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 5.3.6}}:—_passive_, _aorist 1_ παρείθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.868}}; _infinitive_ παρεθῆναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.105}}: _aorist 2_ παρείμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1666}}: _perfect_ παρεῖμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 852}}:—**let fall at the side, let fall**, πὰρ δ᾽ ἴεισι τὰ πτέρα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 16; τὴν χεῖρα παρεικώς 4th-3rd c.BC: Clearchus Historicus 25; παρεῖσ᾽ ἐμαυτήν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 819; π. ἀπ᾽ ὀμμάτων πέπλον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1203 (Lyric poetry); τὸ μάργον τῆς γνάθου [prev. author] “Cyc.” 310:}} —_passive_, ἡ δὲ παρείθη μήρινθος ποτὶ γαῖαν it **hung down** to earth, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.868.}}
**II** **pass by, pass over**, πᾶν ἔθνος καταστρεφόμενος καὶ οὐδὲν παριείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.177; π. κλύδων᾽ ἔφιππον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 732, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.263; π. τι ἄρρητον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 754a}}:—_passive_, περὶ μὲν τούτου παρείσθω {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.59.3.}}
**II.2** **pass unnoticed, disregard**, τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.86, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.14, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 291, “Ch.” 925, 1032, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1193, etc.; τὰ παθήματα.. παρεῖσ᾽ ἐάσω [prev. author] “OC” 363}}:—_passive_, παίδων πόθος παρεῖτο {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 545; μηδαμῇ παρεθῆναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.105}}: with _infinitive_, **omit** to do, παρέντα τοῦ μὲν τὸ φρόνιμον ἐγκωμιάζειν, τοῦ δὲ τὸ ἄφρον ψέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 235e, compare “PCair.Zen.” 369.2 (3rd c.BC), 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de communi mathematica scientia” 1}}: with a _negative_ repeated, μὴ παρῇς τὸ μὴ οὐ φράσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 283}}: with _participle_, οὐ παρίει σείων ὁ θεός {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 3.5.9: _absolutely_, αἰ δέ κα παρῇ “Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.169 (from Cyrene)}}:—_middle_, **neglect**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 778 (Lyric poetry); τὸν δῆμον 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 51.5.}}
**II.3** of Time, **let pass**, τὸν χειμῶνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.77; ἕνδεκα ἡμέρας [prev. author] 7.183; νύκτα μέσην [prev. author] 8.9; τὸν καιρόν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.27, etc.}}
**III** **relax**, τοὺς τερθρίους παρίει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 440}}; οἶνος παρίησι **weakens**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 9.86}}; **remit**, γόον, πόθον, χόλον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 111, “Tr.” 650, “IA” [1609]}}; **give up**, μελέτας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.85}}; τὸν φελλόν **give up the use of.**., Thphr. [prev. cited]:—_passive_, **to be relaxed, weakened**, κόπου δ᾽ ὕπο.. παρεῖται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 635; κόπῳ παρεῖμαι [prev. author] “Ph.” 852; παρειμένος νόσῳ [prev. author] “Or.” 881; ὕπνῳ [prev. author] “Cyc.” 591; γήρᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 931d; σώμασι παρειμέναι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 683; παρειμένα μέρη τοῦ σώματος 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.73, compare 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.7, etc.; καὶ δὴ παρεῖται σῶμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1070; τῷ λίαν παρειμένῳ [prev. author] “Or.” 210; τὰ σώματα παρειμένοι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.105; ὥστε καὶ τοῦ σώ ματός τι παρεθῆναι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 68.33.}}
**III.2** τοῦ ποδὸς παρίει **slack away** the sheet, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 436: so perhaps _metaphorically_, τοῦ μετρίου παρείς}} **letting go one's hold of** moderation, i. e. giving it up, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1212 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III.3** **remit** punishment, τιμωρίαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 9 (_passive_)}}; **pardon**, τὴν συμφοράν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 699:}} —_passive_, ἐποίησεν παρεθῆμεν (_Doric dialect_ for παρεθῆναι) secured our **release** from the obligation, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).66.47 (Epid., 1st c.BC)}}: with _genitive_, παρεῖσθαι τὰς πόλεις τῶν τόκων {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“OGI” 444.15 (Ilium, 1st c.BC).}}
**IV** **yield, give up**, νίκην τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.103, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 943; τυραννίδα τινί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 523; αὑτοὺς κυμάτων δρομήμασιν [prev. author] “Tr.” 693; π. τινὶ τὴν ἀρχήν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.23, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1285b15; οὐδὲ δεῖν δυνάμενον ἄρχειν παριέναι τῷ πλησίον [prev. work] 1325a37}}; **leave** a thing to another, σοὶ παρεὶς τάδε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 132; Ζεὺς τὰ μικρὰ.. ἄλλοις δαίμοσιν παρεὶς ἐᾷ “Tragica Adespota” 353}}:—_middle_, **give up**, συμμαχίαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1446b28}}; **resign**, στρατηγίαν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 39.23, etc.:}} —_passive_, [γῆ] παρειμένη **left** in private ownership, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.53.5 (3rd c.BC).}}
**IV.2** **permit, allow**, with _dative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, ἄλλῳ δὲ παρήσομεν οὐδενὶ ναυαρχέειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.161, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1482, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 341, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1336b29: with}}_subjunctive_, πάρες ὑπερβῶ **suffer** me to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 308 (_anapaest meter_): _absolutely_ (the _infinitive_ being understood), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 591, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 340, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 199c, etc.}}; μὴ παρῇς σαυτοῦ βροτοῖς ὄνειδος **do** not **allow** them to have cause to reproach thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 967}}; παρῆκεν, ὥστε βραχέα μοι δεῖσθαι φράσαι **has allowed** that there should be but little for me to say, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 570.}}
**V** **allow to pass, admit**, οὐδεὶς ὅστις οὐ παρήσει [ἡμέας] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.72, compare 4.146}}; π. ἐς τὴν Ἑλλάδα τοὺς βαρβάρους, τὸν Μαρδόνιον ἐπὶ τὴν Ἑ., {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.15, 9.1}}; Ἄδραστον εἰς γῆν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 468; λόγον π. εἰς τὸ φρουρίον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 561b}}; μὴ παρίωμεν εἰς τὴν ψυχήν **let us** not **admit** [the thought], {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 90e:}} —_middle_, βαρβάρους εἰς τὰς ἀκροπόλεις παρεῖνται **have admitted** them into **their very** citadels, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.15}}; of innkeepers, **admit**, τοὺς καταλύτας ἡμιασσαρίου {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.15.6.}}
**VI** _middle_, **obtain the leave of** a magistrate, παρέμενος τοὺς ἄρχοντας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 742b, compare 951a.}}
**VI.2** **beg to be excused** or **let off** something, οὐδέν σου παρίεμαι I **ask** no **quarter**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 341b}}; οὐκ ἂν παρείμην οἷσι μὴ δοκῶ φρονεῖν I **ask** no **favour** of them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1666}}; so παριέμεσθα καί φαμεν κακῶς φρονεῖν I **ask pardon**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 892; τοῦτο ὑμῶν δέομαι καὶ παρίεμαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 17c.}}

3936:
** παρίστημι**,
**A** causal in _present_, _imperfect_, _future_, and _aorist 1_; later _perfect_ παρέστᾰκα in same sense, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 5.196 (2nd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.94.7, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.273, etc.}}
**A.I** **cause to stand, place beside**, π. τοὺς ἱππεῖς ἐφ᾽ ἑκάτερον τὸ κέρας {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.72.9, compare 3.113.8}}; παραστήσας τὰ ὅπλα **having brought** his arms **into view**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.175}}; π. τινὰ φυλάττειν **set** one **near** a thing to guard it, variant in {{Refs|[prev. author] 49.35; π. σορὸν σορῷ “Anatolian Studies” p.204 (from Termessus).}}
**A.II** **set before the mind, present**, ὑπόθεσιν.. οὐ χὶ τὴν οὖσαν παριστάντες ὑμῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.1}}; τοῦτο π. τοὺς θεοὺς ὑμῖν that they may **put** this **into** your **minds**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.1; τὸ δεινὸν π. τοῖς ἀκούουσιν [prev. author] 21.72}}; π. ἐλπίδας, ὁτιοῦν τῶν δεινοτάτων, {{Refs|[prev. author] 19.333, 21.15}}; **arouse, inspire**, οὐ γὰρ ἡ πληγὴ παρέστησε τὴν ὀργὴν ἀλλ᾽ ἡ ἀτιμία {{Refs|[prev. work]72}}; π. φόβον καὶ ἀπορίαν ταῖς πόλεσι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.94.7}}; π. ὁ κίνδυνος διαλογισμόν, μὴ.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.159: so τοῦτο π. ὑμῖν γνῶναι}} **prompt** you to that decision, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.8}}; π. τινὶ θαρρεῖν **give** one confidence, variant in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.174}}; π. τινί with _infinitive_, **put** it **into his head** to.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 9.14.6}}; also π. τινὶ ὅτι or ὡς.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 13.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 600c.}}
**A.II.2** **dispose** a person, πρὸς μελαγχολίας {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.28 W., compare “Mus.” p.73 K.; also Ἀθηναίους ἄλλα παρέστησεν ὡς ἥρωα τιμᾶν Θησέα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 35}}:—also in _passive_, V. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus V. 1.}}
**A.II.3** of a Poet, **represent, describe**, τὸν Νέστορα παρέστησε [ὁ ποιητὴς] πείθοντα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ τοῦ καθ᾽ Ὅμηρον ἀγαθοῦ βασιλέως” p.65 O., compare 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.110f, 4.133b; δι᾽ ἐτυμολογίας 1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 1:}} —_passive_, παριστάσθω ὅτι.. **let it be stated** that.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.310.}}
**A.II.4** **furnish, supply, deliver,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 790.10 (3rd c.BC), “PTeb.” 5.196 (2nd c.BC), “Abh. Berl.Akad.” 1925(5).31 (from Cyrene).}}
**A.II.5** **make good, prove, show**, τι πολλοῖς τεκμηρίοις {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.51, cf. NT.Act.24.13; καθάπερ προϊόντες -στήσομεν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.85 W., compare “Mus.” p.37 K.}}
**A.II.6** with _accusative_ _person_, **present, offer**, ἑαυτοὺς τῷ θεῷ, ἑαυτοὺς δούλους εἰς ὑπακοήν, {{NT|NT.Rom.6.13, 16.}}
**A.II.6.b** **commend**, βρῶμα ἡμᾶς οὐ παραστήσει τῷ θεῷ {{NT|NT.1Cor.8.8.}}
**A.II.7** **render**, [ἡ πίσσα] τὸν οἶνον ἔὔποτον παρίστησι ταχέως {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.676c.}}
**A.II.8** in later Greek, as in _middle_ (V. C. 1), **produce** in court, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 759.22 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.:—_passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Sammelb.” 4512.82 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**A.III** **set side by side, compare**, [πόλεις] μικρὰς μεγάλαις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.40.}} — The use of these _active_ tenses occurs in Pl.[same place], but first becomes common in Oratt.
**B** _passive_, with _aorist_ 2, _perfect_ and _pluperfect_ _active_, _intransitive_:
**B.I** **stand by, beside**, or **near**, θέων δέ οἱ ἄγχι παρέστη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.442, compare 483; ἀμφίπολος δ᾽ ἄρα οἱ κεδνὴ ἑκάτερθε παρέστη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.335, compare 8.218, 18.183}}; ἑξείης πάντεσσι παρίστασαι, of a beggar,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|17.450; οὐδ᾽ ἄρα οἵ τις ἀνουτητί γε παρέστη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.371}}; ζωγράφῳ παρεστηκυῖα, of a painter's model, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.2: frequently in}}_participle_ παραστάς with a Verb, εἶπε παραστάς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.60; οὖτα π. 20.472}}; παρασταθείς, variant for{κατασταθείς}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 365.}}
**B.I.2** **stand by**, i.e. **help, defend**, τινι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.279, etc.; Τρωσὶ παρεστάμεναι καὶ ἀμύνειν 21.231, compare 15.255; Ὀδυσῆϊ π. ἠδ᾽ ἐπαρήγει 23.783, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 439, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.87, etc.; π. τινὶ χερσί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1384}}; βοηθοὶ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.19; οὐ παρέστη οὐδ᾽ ἐβοήθησεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.64.}}
**B.II** more frequently in past tenses, **to have come**, δεῦρο παρέστης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.405}}; **to be at hand**, νῆες δ᾽ ἐκ Λήμνοιο παρέστασαν {{Refs|7.467, etc.}}
**B.II.2** of events, **to be near, be at hand**, ἀλλά τοι ἤδη ἄγχι παρέστηκεν θάνατος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|16.853; κακὴ Διὸς αἶσα παρέστη ἡμῖν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.52, compare 16.280}}: in _future_ _middle_, σοὶ..παραστήσεσθαι ἔμελλεν μοῖρ᾽ ὀλοή {{Refs 4th c.BC+|24.28; ἐάν του καιρὸς ἢ χρεία παραστῇ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.101}}, compare 73: frequently in _perfect_, παρέστηχ᾽ ὡς ἔοικ᾽ ἀγὼν μέγας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 229, compare “Med.” 331}}; in _participle_, τὸ χρῶμα τὸ παρεστηκός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 399; ὁ νῦν παρεστηκὼς ἡμῖν λόγος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 962d}}: in _Attic dialect_ form παρεστώς, ῶσα, ός, th=s parestw/shs no/sou {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 734}}; τοῦ π. θέρους{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1340; τὰς παρεστώσας τύχας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” [1024]}}; τὰ παρεστῶτα **present circumstances**, τὰ λῷστα, κράτιστα τῶν π., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1053, “Pr.” 218; πρὸς τὸ παρεστός 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 564; πρὸς τὸ παριστάμενον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 9.1.}}
**B.III** **come to the side of** another, **come over to** his **opinion**, παραστῆναι ἐς τῶν Περσέων τὴν γνώμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.99}}: _absolutely_, **come to terms, surrender, submit**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.13, 5.65, 6.140; οἱ πολέμιοι παραστήσονται [prev. author] 3.155; τῷ πολέμῳ παραστῆναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.15, compare “Etymologicum Magnum” 653.2.}}
**B.IV** **happen** to one, τῷ δὴ λέγουσι.. θῶμα μέγιστον παραστῆναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.23; τὸ φρονεῖν ἀλλοῖα παρίσταται 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 108}}; especially **come into one's head, occur** to one, τὼς νόος ἀνθρώποισι παριστᾶται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 16.2; δόξα μοι παρεστάθη ναοὺς ἱκέσθαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 911}}; δόξα π. τινὶ ὥστε.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 66b}}; σοὶ τοῦτο παρέστηκεν, ὡς.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 233c}}; π. θαῦμα, γνώμη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.2, 24 (assuming variant); ἔκπληξις παρέστη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.96: _impersonal_, παρίσταταί μοι}} **it occurs** to me; τῷ οὐ παραστήσεται.. τεθνάναι βούλεσθαι to whom **it will** not **occur** to wish for death, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.46: followed by ὡς, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.61, 95, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.62, etc.}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.17; οὐχὶ παρίσταταί μοι ταὐτὰ γιγνώσκειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.1}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 21.12, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 58e}}; _participle_, τὸ παριστάμενον **that which comes into one's head, a thought**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cont.” 13}}; ἐκ τοῦ π. λέγειν speak **offhand**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 9, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.295.}}
**B.V** **to be disposed**, πρὸς τὰς πράξεις {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.71 K.; εἰρηνικῶς παρεστώτων [prev. author] “Hom.” p.45 O.}}
**B.V.2** **collect oneself**, παραστῆναι πρὸς τὸν κίνδυνον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.43}}; τῷ θυμῷ παραστάς{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 99; π. πρὸς τὴν ἀπολογίαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 19; παρεστηκότες ταῖς γνώμαις 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - FHG” 161 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}
**B.V.3** _metaphorically_, οἶνος παρίσταται the wine **improves, becomes fit for drinking**, opposed to ἐξίσταται, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.14.10, compare 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.8.}}
**B.V.3.b** of a crop, **to be ripe**, ὅταν ὁ πρώϊμος σπόρος παραστῇ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 56.68 (Egypt, 3rd c.BC)}}; so probably ἡ γῆ παρέστηκεν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PLille” 8.5 (3rd c.BC).}}
**B.VI** παρεστηκέναι φρενῶν **to be beside oneself, lose one's** wits, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.53.6; π. ταῖς διανοίαις [prev. author] 14.5.7, etc.}}; ἐπὶ τοσοῦτον π. {{Refs|[prev. author] 22.8.13; compare παρεξίστημι 11.}}
**B.VI.2** **to be passionately devoted to**, ἵπποισι παρεστεῶτες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 17.}}
**B.VII** _absolutely_, παρεστηκός, ={παρόν}, **since it was in their power, since the opportunity offered**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.133.}}
**C** Some tenses of _middle_, _present_ and _imperfect_ sometimes, _future_ and _aorist_ I almost always (for exceptions, see above {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11.2, III, iv), are used in causal sense:}}
**C.I** **set by one's side, bring forward, produce**, π. ἱερεῖα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.22}}; especially in a court of justice, τοὺς παῖδας παραστησάμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.35}}; παιδία παραστήσεται (of a culprit) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.99; ταῦτα παραστησάμενος [prev. work]187; μάρτυρας παρίστανται 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.13, etc.}}; παραστήσασθαί τινα **produce** him **as witness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.9, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.28, etc.; π. τινὰ εἰς κρίσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 555b.}}
**C.I.2** **commend**, τινί τινα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 15.7.3.}}
**C.II** **bring to one's side, bring over by force, bring to terms**, ἀέκοντας παραστήσασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.80; π. βία 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 916; π. πολιορκίᾳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.98; πολιορκοῦντας π. ὁμολογίᾳ [prev. work] 29: _absolutely_, π. τινά, π. πόλιν, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.45, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.124, etc.; τοὺς οἰκοῦντας τὴν Ἀττικὴν π. εἰς φορὰν δασμοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 706b.}}
**C.II.2** generally, **dispose for one's own views** or **purposes**, τινὰ παραστήσασθαι οὕτως ὥστε.. so **to dispose** a person that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.136; ἑαυτοὺς πρὸς τὴν μάχην 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.109.9}}; **dispose, induce** a person, πρὸς τὸ κοινωνεῖν {{Refs|[prev. author] 29.3.5}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Chio Epistolographus “Ep.” 3.}}

3938:
** πάροδος**, ἡ,
**B** **way by** or **past, passage**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.21, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 294b26}}; π. καὶ τροπαὶ τῶν ἄστρων{{Refs 6th c.AD+|[prev. work] 296b4, compare 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis de Caelo commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 507.24}}; π. τοῦ χρόνου **passage, lapse** of time, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 44.}}
**B.b** Astrology texts, **rotation** of chronocratory, Vett.{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Val. 37.9, compare 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 109.}}
**B.2** **going by** or **past, passing, entrance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.82}}; ἐν τῇ π. as they **passed by**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.126, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.68.8}}; κατὰ τὴν π. {{Refs|[prev. author] 21.46.12}}; ἐκ π., ἐν π., **by the way, cursorily**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 306b27, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.245 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.16; π. τινὶ ἐπὶ τὰς ὕστερον πράξεις διδόναι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.345c}}; τὴν π. ἵν᾽ ἔχῃς τῶν θυρῶν εὐνουστέραν **entrance** by the door, {{Refs|Dionysius Epigrammaticus Comedy texts 3.17.}}
**B.II** **narrow entrance** or **approach, mountain-pass**, as Thermopylae, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.30, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.7.4, etc.}}; λαβεῖν τὰς π. take the **pass**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.20, compare 9.32, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.334 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; opposed to δίοδοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.6.}}
**B.II.b** especially **side-entrance** on the stage, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Semus 20, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.126.}}
**B.II.c** **gallery** or **passage-way** surmounting fortifications, {{Refs|“IG” 22.463.49,52,1672.110.}}
**B.III** **coming forward, appearance**, especially before the assembly, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 3.29, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.205b, etc.}}
**B.III.2** **first entrance** of a chorus in the orchestra, which was made **from the side wings**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1123a23, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.108; ὥσπερ δράματος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.805d.}}
**B.III.2.b** **first song** sung by the chorus **after its entrance**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1452b22, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.785a.}}
**B.III.3** **use of stage for** an artist's **performance**, ἡ π. εὗρεν δραχμήν {{Refs|“Michel” 908, 909 (lasos).}}
**B.III.4** **public recitation,** {{Refs|Anthologia Graeca 11.422 (Antioch.).}}
**B.IV** in a ship, **gangway, passage along the deck**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 43: _metaphorically_, π. καὶ ἐπιβάθρα τοῦ συγγράφειν 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 3 “Praef.”}}

3939:
** παροικ-έω**,
**dwell beside**, with _accusative_, ἀπὸ Κνίδου μέχρι Σινώπης τὴν Ἀσίαν π. **dwell along the coasts of** Asia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.162}}: with _dative_, **live near**, πόλει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.71; ταῖς πυραμίσι “OGI” 666.13 (1st c.AD)}}; **dwell among**, τισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.93: _absolutely_, [prev. author] 6.82}}; of places, **lie near**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 1.5.}}
**II** **live in** a place **as** πάροικος, οἱ παροικοῦντες ξένοι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.47, compare “SIG” 709.9 (Chersonesus, 2nd c.BC)}}; ὡς ἐπὶ ξένης {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.416}}; **sojourn in**, Ἱερουσαλήμ {{NT|NT.Luke.24.18.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, τὸν ἀνθ ρώπινον βίον παρῳκηκότες {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Morte - Papiri Ercolanesi” 38.}}

3941:
** πάροικος**, ον,
**dwelling beside** or **near, neighbouring**, with _genitive_, Κάδμου πάροικοι.. δόμων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1155; [πόλεις]π.Ρῃκίων ἐπαύλων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 869 (Lyric poetry): with _dative_, ποταμῷ παροίκους Ἅλυϊ Diog.Trag. 1.7, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.113: _absolutely_, ἡ π. πηλαμύς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 503}}; **neighbour**, οὐκ ἀσίνης π. {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 80}}; Ἀττικὸς π., proverbial of a restless neighbour, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1395a18, 4th-3rd c.BC: Duris Epigrammaticus 96J.}}
**2** π. πόλεμος war **with neighbours**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.235.}}
**II** **foreign, alien**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.15.13, +others; σπέρμα π. ἐν γῇ ἀλλοτρίᾳ NT.Act.7.6}}: as _substantive_, **sojourner** in another's house, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.82}}: generally, **alien, stranger**, {{LXX|LXX.Lev.22.10}}: in later Greek, ={μέτοικος}, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 398.37 (Cos, 3rd c.BC), “IG” 7.2712.64 (Acraeph.), “OGI” 1338.12,20, +others(Pergam., 2nd c.BC), etc.}}; = **colonus,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-6th c.AD: Codex Justinianus 1.34.1 (_plural_).}}

3942:
** παροιμ-ία**, ἡ,
**proverb, maxim, saw**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 264, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 664, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 528, etc.}}; κατὰ τὴν π. as **the saying** goes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 222b; τὸ κατὰ τὴν π. λεγόμενον [prev. author] “Sph.” 261c}}; καθάπερ ἡ π.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 174.3}}: in _plural_, of the Proverbs of Solomon, LXX
**2** **figure, comparison,** {{NT|NT.John.10.6, +others}}
**3** **digression, incidental remark**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.61, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}

3943:
** πάροιν-ος**, ον,
={παροινικός}, Pratin.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Lyric poetry 1.8. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 4.8, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 146, etc.}}; μάχαι π. {{Refs|“Anacreontea” 40.12}};τὸ σὸν π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 444}}. _adverb_ -νως {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 6.21.}}
**II** ={παροίνιος}{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|11, ὄρχησις 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 14.629e.}}

3944:
** παροίχομαι**,
_future_ -οιχήσομαι: _perfect_ παρῴχηκα, _Ionic dialect_ παροίχωκα, and in later writers παρῴχημαι, {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Act.14.16, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 8.12.3; also in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.1}}, but with variant{παροιχομένων}:—**to have passed by**, παρῴχετο γηθόσυνος κῆρ. **he passed on, went on his way**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.272.}}
**2** of Time, **to be past** παροίχωκεν (variant{παρῴχηκεν}) δὲ πλέων νύξ {{Refs|10.252}}; ἡ παροιχομένη νύξ the **by-gone** night, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.209, 9.58; ὁ π. χρόνος [prev. author] 2.14; Ὀλύμπια παροιχώκεε [prev. author] 8.72}}; παροιχόμενοι ἀνέρες men ** of by-gone times**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.29 (uncertain reading); δεῖμα παροιχόμενον [prev. author] “I.” 8(7).12; τὰ παροιχόμενα κακά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.4.17; τὰ παροιχόμενα}}**the past,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.90.15, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.120, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.1}}; also, **the aforesaid**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 14}}; τοὔστρακον παροίχεται the danger of ostracism ** has gone by**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 71.}}
**3**Grammars, ὁ παρῳχημένος [χρόνος] **past** tense, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 123.17, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1081c}}; παρῳχημέναι φωναί forms **of past tenses**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 272.5.}}
**IIto be gone, dead**, δείματι with fright, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 738 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὅσον παροίχῃ how **art thou fallen**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 995 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III**c._genitive_, **shrink from**, νείκους τοῦδε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 452}}; later, **neglect**, τῶν πραγμάτων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 288.2 (2nd c.AD).}}

3946:
** παρόμοι-ος**, ον
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.80}}; _feminine_ η {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.183}}; α {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 616a19, variant in 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.192}}: - **closely resembling**, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.73, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.132}}; τινος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Cyranus 6.}}
**2**_absolutely_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.99, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.80}}; παρόμοιόν ἐστιν, ὅπερ καὶ.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.11}}; π.ποιεῖν ὡσπερανεί τις.. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.4d.}}
**3**of numbers, **nearly equal**, π. τοῖς Ἕλλησι τὸν ἀριθμόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.4.13}}. _adverb_ -ως {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Respiratione” 478b30.}}
**4**Grammars, **employing assonance**: π., τό, ={παρομοίωσις}, {{Refs|Rutilius Lupus Rhetor 2.12}}; κῶλα π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 22, 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 25.}}

3947:
** παροξ-ύνω**,
_perfect_ _infinitive_ παρωξυκέναι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.1.3, but παρωξυγκέναι 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 11.6.7}}:—_passive_, _perfect_ παρώξυμμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 4.8, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 276}}:—**urge, spur on, stimulate**, τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.5, etc.; [τινὰ] πρὸς τὰ καλά [prev. author] “Mem.” 3.3.13; ὲπὶ τὸν πόλεμον 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.3, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 54G.}}; τὰ ζεύγη πρὸς τὸ ἔργον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 577b31}}; τινα with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.37; κινδυνεύειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.3}}; opposed to ἀποτρέπω, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.37.}}
**2** **provoke, irritate**, πατρὸς μὴ π. φρένας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 674}}; ξὺν κατηγορίᾳ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.84}}:—_passive_, **to be provoked**, τινι at a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.99; διά τινα [prev. author] 6.56; ἐπί τινι “OGI” 48.15 (Egypt, 3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.7.5; πρός τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.6, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.2; πρὸς ἀλλήλους 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1302a39}}; ὑπό τινος Lys. [prev. cited]; κατὰ τῶν πολιτῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 31: with _dative_, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 87 (assuming variant), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 10.11: with}}_infinitive_, τίς οὐκ ἂν παροξυνθείη πολεμεῖ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.101}}:—_passive_, of sicknesses, **growvirulent**, π. οἱ πυρετοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 6.}}
**3** **make** an application **stronger**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.710.}}
**II** ={παροξυτονέω}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.485a}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 189.28, +others, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).167, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 7.323c.}}
**III** _intransitive_, **hasten.** {{Refs|Peripl.M.Rubr. 20.}}

3948:
** παροξ-υσμός**, ὁ,
**irritation, exasperation**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.14, LXX.Jer.39(32).37, NT.Act.15.39}}; π. ἀγάπης **provoking **or **exciting to..,** {{NT|NT.Heb.10.24.}}
**2** **severe fit of a disease, paroxysm**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 1.11, 12, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(2).387, etc.}}

3949:
** παροργ-ίζω**,
_future_ **-ιῶ**{{LXX|LXX.Deu.32.21}}:—**provoke to anger**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 34.1, LXX.3Ki.16.13,33, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.682, NT.Eph.6.4.}}
**II** _passive_, **to be** or **be made angry**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.16.6, probably in 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 7.2.1}}; τι πρός τινας see reading in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 26.17.}}

3951:
Included with: ** παροτρ-υντικός**, ή, όν,
**fit for inciting**, εἰς μάχην {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1169.55. παροτρ-ύνω}}, **urge on**, with _infinitive_, πὰρ θυμὸς ὀτρύνει φάμεν {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.38, cf. NT.Act.13.50, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 7.6.1, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 35.}}
**2** in Medicine texts, **displace** the uterus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.138.}}

3952:
** παρουσ-ία**, ἡ,
(πάρειμι) **presence**, of persons, δεσπότου, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 169, etc.}}; ἀνδρῶν π., ={ἄνδρες οἱ παρόντες}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 606}}; πόλις μείζων τῆς ἡμετέρας π., ={ἡμῶν τῶν παρόντων}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.86}}; παρουσίαν μὲν οἶσθα.. φίλων, ὡς οὔτις ἡμῖν ἐστιν, i.e. that we have no friends **present** to assist us, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 948}}; of things, κακῶν [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 227, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1049; ἀγαθῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 497e: _absolutely_, παρουσίαν ἔχειν, ={παρεῖναι}, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 540; τὰ τῆς τύχης.. κοινὰς [ἔχει] τὰς παρουσίας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 39; αὐτὸ τὸ ἀγαθὸν [αἴτιον] τῇ π. τοῖς ἄλλοις τοῦ ἀγαθὰ εἶναι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Eudemia” 1217b5, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 100d, etc.}}]
**2** **arrival**, ἡμῶν κοινόπουν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1104, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 209, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.128; εἰς Ἰταλίαν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.45}}; especially **visit** of a royal or official personage, βασιλέως, etc., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 48.14 (2nd c.BC), “IPE” 12.32 A 85 (Olbia, 3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; of a god, {{Refs|“IG” 42(1).122.34(Epid.).}}
**3** **occasion**, variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1251.}}
**4** π. τισὶ ποιεῖσθαι entertain them on their official **visits**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 139.9 (Philae, 2nd c.BC).}}
**5** in **NT, the Advent,** {{NT|NT.Matt.24.27, +others}}
**6** Astrology texts, **situation** of a planet at a point on the zodiac, ἤτοι κατὰ παρουσίαν ἢ κατὰ συμμαρτυρίαν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 49.26.}}
**II** **substance, property**, ὡς.. ἔχομεν παρουσίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 177, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 471; π. χρημάτων 5th c.BC: Crates Comicus 16.}}
**II.2** **contribution** in money, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Scholia 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Phal.” 1.3 (_plural_).}}

3953:
** παροψ-ίς**, ίδος, ἡ,
(ὄψον) ={παρόψημα}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 147, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 187, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.4: _metaphorically_, τῶν κακῶν παροψίδες}} **fresh tastes** of misery, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus 2.}}
**II** **dish on which such meats are served**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 60, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 86, 4th c.BC: Archestratus Epicus “fragment” 6, NT.Matt.23.25, 1st c.AD: Juvenalis, D. Junius Poeta Latinus 3.142, 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.74 (παραψ-codices,compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus)}}. (The second use is condemned by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 153.)}}

3954:
** παρρησί-α**, ἡ,
(πᾶς, ῥῆσις) **outspokenness, frankness, freedom of speech**, claimed by the Athenians as their privilege, ἐλεύθεροι παρρησίᾳ θάλλοντες οἰκοῖεν πόλιν κλεινῶν Ἀθηνῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 422, compare “Ion” 672; παρρησίᾳ φράζειν [prev. author] “Ba.” 668}}; ἔχειν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 391; οὔσης παρρησίας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 541; διδόναι π. τισί 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.28; ἐλευθερίας ἡ πόλις μεστὴ καὶ π. γίγνεται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 557b; τἀληθῆ μετὰ παρρησίας ἐρῶ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.31; τὴν ὑπὲρ τῶν δικαίων π. ἀποδόμενος 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 2.1}}; δημοκρατίας οὔσης οὐκ ἔστι π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.14; π. καὶ ἰσηγορία καὶ δημοκρατία 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.38.6}}; περὶ παρρησίας, title of work by Philodemus.
**2** in bad sense, **licence of tongue**, ἡ εἰς τοὺς θεοὺς π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.40, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 240e, 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 1.16.8.}}
**3** **freedom of action**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 2.7}}; π. ζωῆς καὶ θανάτου **power** of life and death, {{Refs|Vett. Val. 6.3, +others}}; **licence, permission**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 1.1.1}}; παρρησίᾳ ἐκτέμνεται τὸ δέρμα **without fear**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 15.8}}; ἤγαγον ὑμᾶς μετὰ παρρησίας **openly**, {{LXX|LXX.Lev.26.13.}}
**4** **liberality, lavishness**, κεκόσμηκε τὸν αὑτοῦ βίον τῇ καλλίστῃ π. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 323.10(Pergam., 2nd c.BC)}}; ἐπὶ τῇ.. τῶν καμάτων καὶ πάσης ἐπιμελείας παρρησίᾳ {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“IG” 5(1).547 (Sparta, 3rd c.AD)}}; = **copia,** {{Refs|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}

3956:
** πᾶς**, πᾶσα, πᾶν,
**A**_Aeolic dialect_ **παῖς**, παῖσα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 13.8, 21.2, 20a. 14, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 12.6, 25.8; Cretan dialect, Thess., 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} _feminine_ **πάνσα** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“GDI” 4976 (from Gortyn), “IG” 9(2).234.2 (Pharsalus, 3rd c.BC), 5(2).343.16 (Orchom. 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, 4th c.BC): _genitive_ παντός, πάσης, παντός: _genitive_ _plural_ _masculine_ and _neuter_ πάντων, _feminine_ πᾱσῶν, _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ πᾱσέων, _Epic dialect_ also πᾱσάων [σᾱ] 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.107: _dative_ _plural_ _masculine_ and _neuter_ πᾶσι}}, _Epic dialect_ and Delph. πάντεσσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.246, “IG” 22.1126.22,44}}; also _Locrian dialect_ πάντεσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.8 (5th c.BC); Delph. πάντεσι “SIG” 452.5 (3rd c.BC)}}; πάντοις {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“GDI” 2652 (Delph., 2nd c.BC), “Tab.Defix.Aud.” 75.8: πᾶν}} as _accusative_ _masculine_ in {{LXX+7th c.BC+|LXX, π. ἄνδρα, οἰκέτην, οἶκον, LXX.1Ki.11.8, LXX.Exo.12.44, LXX.Jer.13.11. [_Doric dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ πάν [ᾰ] Hdn.Gr. 2.12, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.85, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in 1787 “fragment” 3 ii 5, +others, and _Attic dialect_ in compounds, as ἅπᾰν, πάμπᾰν}}, etc. (but in compounds sometimes long in _Attic dialect_, {{Refs|“Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 416)}}.]—Coll. _pronoun_, when used of a number, **all**; when of one only, **the whole**; of the several persons in a number, **every.**
**A.I** in _plural_, **all**, πάντες τε θεοὶ πᾶσαί τε θέαιναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.5, etc.}}; πάντες ὅσοι.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.11, etc.}}; πάντας ᾧ ἂνπεριτυγχάνῃ, for ὅσοις ἄν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 566d}}: also with the _Article_, see.below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**A.I.2** strengthened by _adverbs_, ἅμα πάντες **all** together, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.253, etc.; πάντες ἅμα 1.495 (in Prose commonly ἅπαντες}}, but not always, see {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.23, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.10, etc.)}}: with a _collective_ noun, ἅμα πᾶς ὁ δῆμος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.14; πάντες ὁμῶς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.98; ὁμοῦ πάντες 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 715; πάντα μάλα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.115, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.216, etc.; πάντες ὁμοίως 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.85, etc.}}
**A.I.3** with _superlative_,πάντες ἄριστοι **all the** noblest, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.3, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.272, etc.}}
**A.I.4** **consisting** or **composed wholly of**, i.e. **nothing but, only**, φρουρούμενος ὑπὸ πάντων πολεμίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 579b; ἐκ πασῶν δυνάμεων συνεστώς “Corpus Hermeticum” 13.2}}; see 11.2.
**A.II** _singular_, **all, the whole**, πᾶς δ᾽ ἄρα χαλκῷ λάμπε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.65, compare 13.191}}; πᾶσα ὕλη **all the** wood, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 511, compare “Th.” 695, etc.}};πᾶσα ἀληθείη **all the** truth, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.407, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.507}}; τὴν φάτνην ἐοῦσαν χαλκέην πᾶσαν **all** of bronze, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.70}}; ἦν ἡ μάχη καρτερὰ καὶ ἐν χερσὶ πᾶσα, ἦν γὰρ τὸ χωρίον πρόσαντες πᾶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.43, etc.}}; πᾶν κράτος **the whole** power, **sovereign** power, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 142 (Lyric poetry); τὸ πᾶν κράτος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.35; μετὰ πάσης ἀδείας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.305; πᾶσα ἀνάγκη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 240a}}; πᾶσαι δ᾽ ὠΐγνυντο πύλαι, πᾶσαι γὰρ ἐπῴχατο [πύλαι], the **whole** gate was open (shut), i.e. the gate was **wide** open, **quite** shut, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.809, 12.340, as explained by 3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus}}; see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**A.II.2** as in {{Refs|1.4, with attraction, ὁ πάντ᾽ ἄναλκις οὗτος, ἡ πᾶσα βλάβη who is}}**nought but** mischief, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 301, compare “Ph.” 622, 927.}}
**A.III** **every**, οἱ δ᾽ ἄλκιμον ἦτορ ἔχοντες.. πᾶς πέτεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.265, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.313, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 972, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1131, 1135}}; ἄκουε πᾶς, ={ἀκούετε πάντες}, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 372; πᾶς χώρει [prev. author] “Pax” 555: with _partitive_ _genitive_, παντὶ βροτῶν (variant{βροτῷ}) 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.100; πᾶς τοῦτό γ᾽ Ἑλλήνων θροεῖ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 597; τῶν ἀνθρώπων πᾶς 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 3.70; also πᾶς ἀνήρ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1366, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1125, etc.; πᾶσα ἀνθρώπου ψυχή 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 249e}}]: with the _Article_, see infr. B; πᾶς τις **every single** one, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 621, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.50, 3.79, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 28, etc.; πᾶς τις βροτῶν [prev. author] “El.” 984, compare “OC” 25, etc.}}; πᾶς ὅστις.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 1413}}; πᾶν ὅσον.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 787, etc.}}
**A.III.2** less frequently, **any one**, τὸ μὲν ἐπιτιμᾶν.. φήσαιτις ἂν.. παντὸς εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.16; παντὸς ἀνδρός [ἐστι] γνῶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 532e; χαλεπόν τι καὶ οὐχὶ παντός [prev. author] Alcaeus1.129a}}; παντὸς ἀκούοντος.. when **any one** hears.., {{NT|NT.Matt.13.19}}; ἀμήχανον δὲ παντὸς ἀνδρὸς ἐκμαθεῖν ψυχήν **any** man's soul, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 175}}; πάντων ἀποστερεῖσθαι λυπηρόν to be deprived of **anything**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.5; compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 111.2, VI.}}
**B** with the _Article_, in the sense of **all, the whole**, when the _substantive_ is to be strongly specified, πᾶς being put either before the _Article_ or after the _substantive_, πᾶσαν τὴν δύναμιν **all his** force, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.214; τὰ ἀγαθὰ πάντα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.20 (assuming variant)}}: with abstract Nouns and others which require the _Article_, πάντα τὰ μέλλοντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 101; πᾶσαν τὴν ἀλήθειαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.87}}; τὰ τῆς πόλεως π. **all** the affairs of state, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.48, etc.: emphatically, τὰς νέας τὰς πάσας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.59.}}
**B.II** πᾶς is put between the _Article_ and _substantive_, to denote totality (V. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11), ὁ πᾶς ἀριθμός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 339; τὴν πᾶσαν ἵππον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80; τὸ πᾶν πλῆθος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.93}}; οἱ πάντες ἄνθρωποι **absolutely all**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.7, etc.}}; so πᾶν the _neuter_ with the _Article_ itself becomes a _substantive_, τὸ πᾶν **the whole**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 275, 456, etc., see below 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator IV}}; τὰ πάντα **the whole**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eu.” 415}}; τοῖς πᾶσιν **in all points**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.64, 5.28}}; οἱ πάντες **all of them**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80}}; but also, **the community**, opposed to οἱ ὀλίγοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.86}}; ἡ μὲν [τάξις] πάντα ἕν, ἡ δὲ πάντα ὅλον, ἡ δὲ πάντα πᾶν **all things** as a unity, as a totality, as an **integral sum**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 206.}}
**C** with Numerals to mark an exact number, ἐννέα πάντες **full** nine, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.258, compare 24.60; ἐννέα πάντ᾽ ἔτεα 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 803; δέκα πάντα τάλαντα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.247, etc.; but κτήνεα τὰ θύσιμα πάντα τρισχίλιαἔθυσε 3, 000}} **of all kinds**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.50}}; τὸν ἀρχιτέκτονα.. ἐδωρήσατο πᾶσι δέκα with ten presents **of all kinds**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.88; Παυσανίῃ πάντα δέκα ἐξαιρέθη [prev. author] 9.81; τὰ πάντα μυρία [prev. author] 3.74; πάντα θύειν ἑκατόν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 170; πάντα χίλια ἔθυεν 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 2.60.}}
**C.II** with the _Article_, **in all**, οἱ πάντες.. εἷς καὶ ἐνενήκοντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.70, compare 1.214, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.60, 3.85, etc.; τριήρεις.. τὰς πάσας ἐς διακοσίας [prev. author] 1.100, compare 7.1; ἐν εἴκοσι ταῖς πάσαις ἡμέραις 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 1.11.5.}}
**D** Special Usages: in _dative_ _plural_ _masculine_ πᾶσι, **with** or **in the judgement of all**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.285, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1446; ὁ πᾶσι κλεινός [prev. author] “OT” 8; κράτιστον πᾶσιν Οἰδίπου κάρα [prev. work] 40.}}
**D.2** _feminine_ _plural_, ἔδοξε πάσαις (i.e. ταῖς ψήφοις) carried **unanimously,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12(3).168.14 (Astypalaea, 2nd-1st c.BC),compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Bis Acc.” 18,22.}}
**D.II** _neuter_ _plural_ πάντα **all kinds of things**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, frequently in phrase δαίδαλα πάντα, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.60, +others; οἰωνοῖσι πᾶσι 1.5.}}
**D.II.2** πάντα γιγνόμενος becoming **all things**, i. e. assuming every shape, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.417.}}
**D.II.3** πάντα εἶναί τινι to be **everything** to one, ἦν οἱ.. τὰ πάντα ἡ Κυνώ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.122}}; ἦσάν οἱ πάντα —ἅπαντα codices) αἱ Συρήκουσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.156; Εὔβοια αὐτοῖς πάντα ἦν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.95; πάντ᾽ ἐκεῖνος ἦν αὐτοῖς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.43}}; π. ἦν Ἀλέξανδρος (i.e. ὑμῖν) {{Refs|[prev. author] 23.120}}; π. εἶναι ἔν τισι to be **all in all** among them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.157.}}
**D.II.4** πάντα as _adverb_ for πάντως, **in all points, entirely, wholly**, π. νοήμονες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.209; π. γὰρ οὐ κακός εἰμι 8.214; ὁ πάντ᾽ ἄναλκις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 301; τῷ πάντ᾽ ἀγαθῷ [prev. author] “Aj.” 1415 (_anapaest meter_); τὸν πάντ᾽ ἄριστον [prev. author] “OC” 1458; πάντ᾽ ἐπιστήμης πλέων [prev. author] “Ant.” 721 (hence παντάγαθος, παντάριστος, etc. as _compound_ words)}}; τὰ πολλὰ π. **almost throughout**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.67, compare 1.203, 2.35}}; but τὰ π. **in every way, by all means, altogether**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.97; οἰόμενοι τὰ π. νικᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.1; ὁ τὰ π. φιλαίτατος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.98}}; also ἐς τὰ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.81}}; κατὰ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. passage], 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 30d.}}
**D.III** _neuter_ _singular_, τὸ πᾶν **the whole** (V. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11), περὶ τοῦ π. δρόμον θέειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.74; πολλοῦ γε καὶ τοῦ π. ἐλλείπω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 961; τοῦ π. ἡμαρτηκέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 235e}}; ἄξιοι τοῦ π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sph.” 216c}}; τὸ πᾶν as _adverb_, **completely, altogether**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 781 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1009, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 959a, etc.}} (but, **for all that, nevertheless**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 188.27)}}: with _negative_, **at all**, οὐκ ἠξίωσαν οὐδὲ προσβλέψαι τὸ πᾶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 217, etc.}}; also πᾶν alone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, etc.}}
**D.III.b** in Philos., τὸ πᾶν **the universe**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 13, 4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 268a11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 28c, 30b, etc.}}; including τὸ κενόν (opposed to τὸ ὅλον), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Placita Philosophorum” 2.1.7; also, 4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}} name for **ten**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “in Nicomachi arithmeticam introductionem” p.118P., “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 59.}}
**D.III.c** τῷ παντί **in every point, altogether**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.12, etc.}}
**D.III.d** τὸ π., ={μολυβδόχαλκος}, Ps.- {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus Alch. p.56 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, Maria cited in Zos.Alch.p.192 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**D.III.2** πᾶν **anything**, πᾶν μᾶλλον ἢ στρατιήν οἱ ἐδίδου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.162}}; εἴη δ᾽ ἂν πᾶν **anything** is possible, {{Refs|[prev. work] 195}}; πᾶν ποιῶν by **any** means **whatever**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 39a (also πᾶν ποιεῖν καὶ λέγειν [prev. passage]}}; πᾶν ποιεῖν ὥστε.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phd.” 114c), compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).48; πᾶν ἂν ἔπραξαν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.16: more frequently in _plural_, πάντα ποιῶν [prev. author] 12.84, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.2}}; π. ποιεῖν ὅπως.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.4.21; πάντα τολμῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 761; compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 111.2.}}
**D.III.3** ἐπὶ πᾶν **on the whole, in general**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 875d; ὡς ἐπὶ πᾶν εἰπεῖν [prev. author] “Euthd.” 279e, etc.; τὸ πρὸς ἅπαν ξυνετὸν ἐπὶ πᾶν ἀργόν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82.}}
**D.III.4** παντὸς μᾶλλον more than **anything**, i. e. **above all**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 49b, “Prt.” 344b, “Grg.” 527b; π. μᾶλλον οὐ [prev. author] “Phdr.” 228d: in answers, π. γε μᾶλλον}} **quite so**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 67b.}}
**D.IV** with Preps., εἰς πᾶν προελήλυθε μοχθηρίας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.3}}; ἐς τὸ πᾶν **altogether**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 682 (Lyric poetry), “Eu.” 52, 83}}; ἐν παντὶ ἀθυμίας εἶναι to be in **utter** despair, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.55: more frequently ἐν παντὶ εἶναι, ἐν παντὶ κακοῦ εἶναι}}, to be in **great** danger or fear, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 194a, “R.” 579b; ἐν π. γενέσθαι [prev. author] “Euthd.” 301a; ἐμ παντὶ ἐοῦσα “IG” 42(1).122.27 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; ἐν παντὶ εἶναι μή.. to be **in great fear** lest.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.29; ἐς πᾶν κακοῦ ἀπίκατο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.118; ἐς πᾶν ἀφικέσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.1.12; εἰς πᾶν ἐλθεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.13}}; ἐπὶ πᾶσιν in **all things**, καιρὸς δ᾽ ἐπὶ π. ἄριστος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 694}}; but also, **finally**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.11.1, +others}}; περὶ παντὸς ποιεῖσθαι esteem above **all**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.26, “An.” 1.9.16}}; πρὸ παντὸς εὔχεσθαι wish above **all**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 239e}}; διὰ παντός (i.e. χρόνου) **for ever, continually**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 705 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.38, etc.}}; also, **altogether**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 407d; διὰ πάντων [prev. author] “Sph.” 254b}}; ὁ κατὰ πάντων λόγος the **common** formula, {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.2186}}; ἡ κ. π. τελετή {{Refs|[prev. work]1596, “PMag.Lond.” 121.872}}; μέχρι παντός **for ever**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.6.18; εἰς τὸ πᾶν ἀεί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 684; ἐς τὸ πᾶν χρόνου [prev. author] “Eu.” 670.}}
**D.V** διὰ πασῶν (i.e. χορδῶν), see at {διαπασῶν}.
**D.VI** οὐ πᾶς not **any**, i.e. **none**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.142(143).2, NT.Luke.1.37, NT.Gal.2.16, +others}}; ἄνευ πάσης ταραχῆς without **any** disturbance, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.87.}}

3957:
** πάσχα**, τό,
_indeclinable_, the Hebrew “Passover” (from **pāsa[hudot]** 'pass over') or **Paschal feast**, {{LXX|LXX.Exo.12.48, etc.}}
**2** **paschal supper,** {{NT|NT.Matt.26.17, 19, +others}}
**3** **paschal lamb**, θύειν τὸ π. {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.12.21, +others; τὸ π. ἐτύθη Χριστός NT.1Cor.5.7.}}

3958:
** πάσχω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.297, etc.}}: _imperfect_ ἔπασχον {{Refs|17.375, etc.}}: _future_ πείσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.134, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ παισεῖται {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Abh.Berl.Akad.” 1925(5).21 (Cyrene, 3rd c.BC)}}: _aorist_ ἔπᾰθον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.492, etc.}}: _perfect_ πέπονθα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.6, etc.:}}_pluperfect_ ἐπεπόνθειν{{Refs|[prev. work] 92, etc.}}; _Attic dialect_ ἐπεπόνθη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 198c}} (all the above tenses in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, _present_ and _aorist_ only in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus). —Rarer forms, _2nd pers._ _plural_}}_perfect_ πέπασθε (so {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.99, πέποσθε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.53}}; _feminine_ _perfect_ _participle_ πεπᾰθυῖα {{Refs|17.555}}; _Doric dialect_ _perfect_ πέποσχα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Stesichorus Lyricus 89, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 11, “PCair.Zen.” 482.18 (3rd c.BC)}}:—**have** something **done to one, suffer**, opposed to **do**, ὅσσ᾽ ἔρξαν τ᾽ ἔπαθόν τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.490; ῥέζοντά τι καὶ παθεῖν ἔοικεν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.32}}; δρᾶν καὶ πάσχειν, see at {δρά; πολλὰ μὲν.. πείσεσθαι}, πολλὰ δὲ ποιήσειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.89, etc.: hence used as}}_passive_ of ποιέω compare {{Refs 4th c.BC+|(4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 2a4, “Metaphorical” 1017a26, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.6.8, etc.), π. τι ὑπό τινος}} **to be treated** so and so by another, **suffer** it at his hands, ἃ πάσχοντες ὑφ᾽ ἑτέρων ὀργίζεσθε, ταῦτα τοὺς ἄλλους μὴ ποιεῖτε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.61, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.44, 124, +others; ἐξ ἐμοῦ μὲν ἔπαθες οἷα φῂς παθεῖν, δρᾷς δ᾽ οὐδὲν ἡμᾶς εὖ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 252; οἷα πρὸς θεῶν πάσχω θεός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 92, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.36.}}
**II** **to have** something **happen** to one, **to be** or **come to be in a state** or **case**, καί τι ἔφη γελοῖον παθεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174e; ὅπερ οἱ τὰς ἐγχέλεις θηρώμενοι πέπονθας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 846, compare “V.” 946}}; ὁρᾶτε μὴ ταὐτὸ πάθητε τῷ ἵππῳ see that **it be** not **with** you as with the horse in the fable, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1393b20, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 488a}}; παραπλήσιον π. ὥσπερ ἂν εἰ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.27}}; ὁμοιότατον πεπονθέναι ὥσπερ ἂν εἴ τις.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 98c.}}
**II.2** of the influence of **passion** or **feeling, to be affected** in a certain way, **be** (or **come to be) in a** certain **state of mind**, οἱ Καμαριναῖοι ἐπεπόνθεσαν τοιόνδε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88, compare 1.80, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.56; ὅ τι μὲν ὑμεῖς πεπόνθατε ὑπὸ τῶν ἐμῶν κατηγόρων οὐκ οἶδα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 17a, compare 21c, 22c, Alcaeus1.118b, “Smp.” 198c; π. τι πρός τινας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.42, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 485b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.11, 8.15, etc.; τι ἔς τινας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.11: sometimes with}} _adjective_, ὑϊκὸν πάσχει **he is** swinishly **disposed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.30: _absolutely_, ὁ πάσχων}} **the man of feeling** or **impulse**, ὁ μὴ πάσχων **the** un**impassioned man**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Magna Moralia” 1203b21.}}
**II.3** of things, πεπόνθασι.. αἱ Ἰώνων ὁρταὶ τοῦτο this **is the case with**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.148}}; πάσχει δὲ ταὐτὸ τοῦτο καὶ τὰ κάρδαμα this **is just the way with**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 234; οἷον τὰ γράμματα πεπονθότ᾽ ἂν εἴη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 253a}}; ὁμοίως π. τῷ Νείλῳ **to be in** the same **case** with.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.20.}}
**II.4** Grammars, of words, **to be subject to** certain **changes,** {{Refs|EM 200.11, 491.2, etc.}}; τὸ πεπονθός a **modified** form, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 137.16.}}
**II.4.b** **to be passive** in meaning, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Synt.” 244.6, +others}}
**III** frequently with _adverbs_, κακῶς πάσχειν or παθεῖν **to be in evil plight, unlucky**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.275, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.146, etc.}}; κακῶς π. ὑπό τινος **to be ill used, ill treated by**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1041 (_anapaest meter_); ἐκ Διὸς π. κακῶς [prev. work] 759 (but also κακὸν π. ὑ. τ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48): frequently with an}} _adjective_, κακά, αἰνά, λυγρὰ π{{Refs 8th c.BC+|., 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.99, 22.431, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.37; ἀνάρσια πρός τινος [prev. author] 5.89: frequently in Trag., π. δύσοιστα, τάλανα, ἀμήχανα, οἰκτρά, σχέτλια, ἀνάξια, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 789 (Lyric poetry), “Th.” 988 (uncertain), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 598, “Hec.” 321, “Andr.” 1180 (uncertain), “IA” 852: also in Prose, δεινά, βίαια π., 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 51.19, 21.1, etc.}}; πρέποντα πάσχειν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|Anti-pho 3.3.9: in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus also with}} _substantive_, ἄλγεα, κήδεα, πήματα, ἀεκήλια ἔργα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.297, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.555, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.886, 18.77}}: rarely in _Attic dialect_, πράγματ᾽ αἴσχιστ᾽ ἂν ἐπάθομεν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.17.}}
**III.b** εὖ πάσχειν **to be well off, in good case**, with _genitive_, τῶν αὑτοῦ (to be read ὧν αὐτοῦ, compare ὅς Possess.) κτεάνων εὖ πασχέμεν **to have the good of, enjoy** one's own, like{ἀπολαύω}, γεύομαι, etc., {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1009, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.32}}; εὖ πάσχειν **receive benefits**, opposed to εὖ δρᾶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 868, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.40, etc.; ἀνθ᾽ ὧν ἔπασχον εὖ.. χάριν δοῦναι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1489; τιμᾶσθαι.. ἐν τῇ μνήμῃ τῶν εὖ πεπονθότων 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.182; εὖ παθεῖν ὑπό τινων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 519d, etc.}}: also with an _adjective_, π. ἀγαθά {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.37; τι ἐσλόν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.89, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 22.5; τερπνόν τι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 521, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.83}}; χαρτά, ὅσια, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 618, “Hec.” 788}}; γλυκέα, χαρίεντα π., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 591, “Ec.” 794; δίκαια 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.10; φιλικὰ ὑπό τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.6.}}
**III.2** without _adverb_, with reference to **evil**, used for κακῶς or κακὰπ., μάλα πόλλ᾽ ἔπαθον καὶ πόλλ᾽ ἐμόγησα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.223, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.607; εἴ κεν μάλα πολλὰ πάθοι 22.220}}; ὁτιοῦν π. **suffer** anything whatever, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.133, etc.: _absolutely_, παθὼν δέ τε νήπιος ἔγνω}} **by hard experience**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 218, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 403}}; ὁ παθών **the injured parly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 730a, 878c:}} —Phrases: μή τι πάθῃς or πάθοι, lest **thou**, lest **he suffer** any **ill**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.596, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.567, compare 11.470, etc.; μή τι πάθωμεν 13.52: hence εἴ τι πάθοιμι or ἤν τι πάθω}}, as euphemism, if aught **were to happen** to me, i.e. if I were to **die**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Callinus Epicus 1.17, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.102, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1105, “V.” 385, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.51, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 8.10; ἂν οὗτός τι πάθῃ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.11; ἐάν τινα ἀνθρώπινα πάσχῃ “IG” 3.74.13; so εἴ τι πείσεται.. ἅδε γᾶ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 244 (Lyric poetry); ἤν τι ναῦς πάθῃ [prev. author] “IT” 755, cf. Syngr. cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.13.}}
**III.2.b** in Law, **suffer punishment, pay the penalty**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.30; π. ὡς ἱερόσυλος “SIG” 1016.7 (Iasos, 4th c.BC),cf. NT.1Pet.4.15}}; ὡς προδότης καὶ ἐπιβουλεύων τῷ δήμῳ πασχέτω τι Aen.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Tact. 11.9; τιμᾶν ὅ τι χρὴ παθεῖν.. ἢ ἀποτεῖσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 299a (-τίνειν codices),compare “Ap.” 36b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.9.5, “IG” 12.65.50, etc.}}
**III.3** τί πάθ; what **is to become of me?** ὤμοι ἐγώ, τί π.; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.404, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.465, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 216 (Lyric poetry), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.24}}; sometimes what (else) **am I to do?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 798}}; so especially τί γὰρ π.; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 614, “Supp.” 257, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1432, etc.}}; ὡμολόγηκα· τί γὰρ π.; I allow it—how **can I help it?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 302d, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.118.}}
**III.4** in _2nd pers._ _singular_, τί πάσχει; what'**s the matter with you?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 708, “Av.” 1044}}; τί χρῆμα πάσχει; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Nu.” 816}}: so in _aorist_ _participle_, τί παθώ; τί παθόντε λελάσμεθα θούριδος ἀλκῆ; what **possesses us that** we have forgotten.. ? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.313}}; but τί παθόντες γαῖαν ἔδυτ; what **befell you that** you died ? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.106}}; also οὐδὲν θαυμαστὸν ἔπαθεν.. πεισθείς **no wonder that** he was induced, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.7.}}
**III.5** **to be ill, suffer**, with _accusative_ of the part affected, π. τοὺς πόδας, τὴν πλευράν, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PSI” 4.293.23 (3rd c.AD), “PGen.” 56.27 (4th c.AD): _absolutely_ in}}_participle_, ὁ πάσχων, almost ={ὁ κάμνων}, the **patient, PMag. Par.**{{Refs|1.3017}}; μεταβαίνει ἀπὸ τῶν παθῶν ἐπὶ τοὺς πάσχοντας ἀνθρώπους {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.583, compare 15.501, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “de fasciis - Opera Hippocratis et Galeni” 45, +others}}
**IV** in later Stoic Philos., πάσχειν is **to be acted upon by outward objects, take impressions from**, them, opposed to ἀποπάσχω, mostly followed by ὅτι, **to be led to suppose that**.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.2.3, 1.18.1, etc.}}: also with _accusative_, **have experience of**, ἀρετήν, λόγον, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.449, 1.121.}} (Πṇθσκω, _future_ Πένθ-σομαι, compare πένθος.)

3960:
** πᾰτάσσω**,
_Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ πάτασσον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.216}}: _future_ **-άξω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 657, “Ra.” 646, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 371b9}}: _aorist_ ἐπάταξα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1199}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ἐπατάχθην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 3, 40, Ach. Tat. 7.4}}: _future_ παταχθήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Fug.” 14:}}_perfect_ πεπάταγμαι (ἐκ-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.327 (elsewhere 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has only _present_ and _imperfect_)}}: _Attic dialect_ and {{Refs|LXX mostly}}_future_ and _aorist_ _active_ (τύπτω and πλήσσω being used in other tenses):
**I** _intransitive_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **beat, knock**, θυμὸς ἐνὶ στήθεσσι πάτασσεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.216; πάτασσε δὲ θυμὸς ἑκάστου 23.370; ἐν δέ τέ οἱ κραδίη στέρνοισι πατάσσει 13.282, compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Cynegeticus” 15.}}
**II** with _accusative_ of the thing set in motion, **strike, smite**, [ξίφος] πάταξον εἰς ἄκρον πόδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 748}}; πρὸς κίονα νῶτον π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1007: with _accusative_ _cognate_, π. πληγήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 527c, compare “Lg.” 879e: _absolutely_, or with _accusative_ of person or thing struck, ὁ πατάξας}} **the man who struck the blow**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.3.4, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.92; ἐὰν μὲν τὸν ἄρχοντα πατάξῃ τις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.33}}; of a deadly blow, ἐὰν λίθος.. ἢ σίδηρος πατάξῃ {{Refs|[prev. author] 23.76}}; **sting**, of a bee, {{Refs|Ach. Tat. 2.7}}; of lightning, **strike**, Arist. [prev. cited]; π. τινὰ δορί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1463; πύξ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 548, compare “Eq.” 1130 (Lyric poetry): frequently in phrase πατάξαι θύραν}}, see at {θύρα}:—_passive_, ὁ μηρὸς πατασσέσθω {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Rh.Pr.” 19.}}
**II.b** **smite, slaughter**, {{LXX|LXX.Judg.9.43, +others}}: simply, **kill**,{{LXX|LXX.1Ki.17.9, +others}}
**II.c** **afflict, visit**, πατάξαι σε Κύριος παραπληξίᾳ{{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Deu.28.28, compare “IG” 12(9).1179.23 (Euboea, 2nd c.AD).}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, ἄτῃ πατάξαι θυμόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1097}}; πόθος καρδίαν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 54 (also μοι κραδίην ἐπάταξε ὅττι.. Thgn.[same place])}}; πατάξω.. μεγάλοις ποτηρίοις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 20; compare παίω 1.6.}}

3961:
** πᾰτ-έω**,
Delph. **βᾰτέω** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.292e}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **μάτημι** [ᾰ] {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 54}}: (πάτος):— **tread, walk**, π. ὁδοῖς σκολιαῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.85; πρὸς βωμόν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Ag.” 1298}}; ὑψοῦ π. **walk** on high, of a king, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.115; π. ἐπάνω ὄφεων NT.Luke.10.19}}:—_passive_, οἱ ἔχεις πατηθέντες {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 1.14.}}
**II** _transitive_, **tread on, tread**, πόας τέρεν ἄνθος μάτεισαι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica [same place]; πορφύρας πατεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 957; δωμάτων πύλας [prev. author] “Ch.” 732}}; χῶρος οὐχ ἁγνὸς πατεῖν holy ground, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 37}}; π. τὸν ἁδὺν οἶνον ἀπ᾽ ἀμπέλω **tread** grapes, Hybrias{{LXX+NT+2nd c.AD+|(“Scol.” 28.4); ληνούς LXX.Neh.13.15, cf. NT.Rev.19.15, 2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 5.12.1}}; also, **thresh** corn, π. τὰ θέρη {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PFlor.” 150.5 (3rd c.AD)}}; κριθὴν καλῶς πεπατημένην {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 988 (3rd c.AD)}}; π. ἐκ τοῦ χόρτου σπέρματα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PFlor.” 388.5 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.2** **walk in**, i.e. **dwell in, frequent**, Λῆμνον πατῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1060; γαῖαν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 18.20; π. πόντον 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 2.218; νῶτα ἁλός “Anthologia Graeca” 7.532 (6th-7th c.AD: Isidorus Hispalensis Grammaticus)}}; rarely of vehicles, τὰ μὴ πατέουσιν ἅμαξαι {{Refs|Callias Epigrammaticus “Aet. Oxy.” 2079.25: _metaphorically_, εὐνὰς ἀδελφοῦ π}}. **frequent**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1193; ἐμεῖο δέμνιον οὐκ ἐπάτησε 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 248}}; οὐδ᾽ Αἴσωπονπεπάτηκας **hast** not **thumbed** Aesop, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 471}}; τὸν Τεισίαν.. πεπάτηκας ἀκριβῶς **you have studied** him carefully, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 273a}}:—_passive_, **to be hackneyed**, τῇ ποιητικῇ πεπατῆσθαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Po.Herc.” 1676.10}}; πεπατημένος **well-worn, trite**, ῥήσεις, λόγοι, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.345, 444, compare 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus cited in 4th c.AD: Eusebius Caesariensis Scriptor Ecclesiasticus “Praeparatio Evangelica” 10.3; τὸ πεπατημένον 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 45.6.}}
**II.3** **tread under foot, trample on**, τινα {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1146, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 248a, etc.; βουλήν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 166; πόλιν NT.Rev.11.2: _absolutely_, πατοῦσι καὶ λακτίζουσι καὶ δάκνουσι 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.562: _metaphorically_, π. κλέος, τιμὰς τὰς θεῶν, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1357, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 745; τὰ τοῖν θεοῖν ψηφίσματα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 377}}:—and in _passive_, τὰ.. δίκαια.. λάγδην πατεῖται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 683, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 644 (Lyric poetry), “Eu.” 110.}}

3962:
** πᾰτήρ**, ὁ,
_genitive_ and _dative_ πατέρος, πατέρι in _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry, _Attic dialect_ πατρός, πατρί (which is also the commoner form in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus, and 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus)}}; _accusative_ always πατέρ; _vocative_ πάτερ: _plural_ πατέρες, πατέρας, πατέρων (πατρῶν only {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.687, 8.245)}}; _dative_ πατράσι [ᾰ] (cf. Sanskrit _locative_ _plural_ **pitṛ[snull]u**, no _dative_ _plural_ occurs in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus or 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus)}}, late _Epic dialect_ πατέρεσσι {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 10.40}}:— **father**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.98, etc.}}; πατρὸς πατήρ **grandfather**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|14.118, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.180, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.3.4, etc.; τοῦδε κεκλῆσθαι πατρός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 86; μητρὸς καλεῖσθαι παῖδα τοῦ π. παρόν [prev. work] 564}}; τὰ πρὸς πατρός **by the father's side**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.99, compare “SIG” 1015.7 (Halic.)}}, etc.; especially of God, the **father** of the Israelites, {{LXX|LXX.Deu.32.6, +others}}; **father** of men, {{NT|NT.Matt.6.8, +others}}; **father** of Jesus Christ, {{Refs|[prev. work] 7.21, +others}}
**II** especially as epithet of Zeus, πατὴρ Ζεύς, π. Κρονίδης, π. ἀνδρῶν τε θεῶν τε, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.235, 21.508, 1.544, +others}}; Ζεὺς π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 512, etc.; Ζεῦ πάτερ καὶ θεοί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 225}}; π. Οὐρανιδᾶν Ζ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.194; ὁ τῶν ἁπάντων Ζεὺς π. Ὀλύμπιος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 275, etc.}}
**III** respectful mode of addressing persons older than oneself, ξεὶνε πάτερ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.28, 48, 8.145, compare “POxy.” 1296.15 (3rd c.AD)}}, etc.; in addressing an elder brother, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 65.3 (2nd c.BC).}}
**IV** _metaphorically_, **father, author**, ἀοιδᾶν π... εὐαίνητος Ὀρφεύς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.176}}; Χρόνος ὁ πάντων π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “O.” 2.17, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 41a}}; τοῦ λόγου π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 177d, compare “Phdr.” 257b, etc.}}; ὁ π. τῶν φώτων, i.e. God, the **father** of the stars, {{NT|NT.Jam.1.17}}; οἱ π. τῶν ἀτόμων the **authors** of the atomic theory, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.246}}; of capital, τοῦ πατρὸς ἐκγόνους τόκους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 555e.}}
**V** title of a grade in the mysteries of Mithras, {{Refs|“IG” 14.1272, etc.}}
**VI** π. πατρίδος, = Latin _pater patriae_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 23, “BGU” 1074.1 (1st c.AD), “IG” 7.2713.33 (Acraeph., 1st c.AD), etc.; similarly, π. τῆς πόλεως [prev. work]5(1).1417.11 (from Methone).}}
**VII** in _plural_,
**VII.1** **forefathers**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.209, etc.}}; ἐξέτι πατρῶν **from our fathers' time**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.245; ἐκ πατέρων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.45.}}
**VII.2** **parents**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 21.17, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.40, “epigram Gr.” 227 (from Teos).}}
**VII.3** **parentnation**, opposed to colonists, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.51, 8.22, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 9}}. (Cf. Sanskrit_pitár_-, Latin _pater_, etc.)

3965:
** πατρι-ά**,
_Ionic dialect_ **πατρ-ιή**, ἡ, (πατήρ) **lineage, descent**, especially **by the father's side**, ἐγενεηλόγησε τὴν π. τὴν Κύρου {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.75, compare 2.143, NT.Luke.2.4.}}
**II** ={πάτρα} {{Refs|II.2}}**clan**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.200, “Michel” 195.1 (Elis, 5th c.BC), 995 A 26 (Delph., 5th c.BC).}}
**II.2** **family**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.12.3, +others, NT.Eph.3.15.}}
**III** in _plural_, = **patrum officia,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-6th c.AD: Codex Justinianus 1.5.14.}}

3966:
** πατρι-άρχης**, ου, ὁ,
(πατρι **father** or **chief of a race, patriarch**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.1Ch.27.22, NT.Act.2.29, 7.8, NT.Heb.7.4.}}
**II** **title borne by the Bishops** of Rome, Constantinople, Jerusalem, Antioch, and Alexandria, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 3.2, etc.}}:— _adjective_ **πατρι-αρχικός**, ή, όν, **of** or **belonging to him**, θρόνος{{Refs|[prev. work] 7 “Praef.” 1, compare “Codex Justinianus [4th-6th c.AD]” 1.5.12.22.}}

3967:
** πατρῐκός**, ή, όν,
(πατήρ) **derived from one's fathers, hereditary**, νόμοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 116; ἀρίς Callias Epigrammaticus Comedy texts 16; φίλος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 142; φίλοι “OGI” 227.9 (Didyma, 3rd c.BC)}}; βασιλεῖαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.13, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Pol.” 1285a19; ἁμαρτεῖν τοῦ π. τύπου 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 228; αἱ π. ἀρεταί 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.69; ξένος 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.11, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.6; ἐχθρός 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.40; φύσει τῆς πρὸς ὑμᾶς ἔχθρας αὐτοῖς ὑπαρχούσης πατρικῆς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.49}}; εἰς τὸ π., = **by right of inheritance,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 5.12 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II** ={πάτριος}, **of** or **belonging to one's father**, γᾶρυς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 65 (Lyric poetry); ὁ π. λόγος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 242a; ἡ π. πρόσταξις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1180a19}}; οἰκονομία π., opposed to δεσποτική and γαμική, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1253b10}}; ἐνευχόμενός σοι τοὺς π. θεούς the gods **of your father(s),** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 421.2 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἡ πατρική (i.e. οὐσία) **patrimony**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1304; π. οἰκία “PStrassb.” 99.4 (2nd c.BC)}}; τὰ π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.75 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus)}}; but τὰ π., also, **father's house**, {{LXX|LXX.Sir.42.10.}}
**II.2** **like a father, paternal**, π. γὰρ ἀρχὴ βούλεται ἡ βασιλεία εἶναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1160b26; π. καὶ συγγενικὴ αἵρεσις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.25.1}}; παρρησία π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.802f; π. θεός “OGI” 418 (Judaea, 1st c.AD)}}. _adverb_, τὰς κολάσεις πατρικῶς ποιεῖσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1315a21; ὁ θεὸς π. κηδόμενος τοῦ ἀνθρωπείου γένους 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.117d.}}
**II.3** Grammars, ἡ πατρική, ={ἡ γενική}, the **genitive**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 1.111 H.}}

3968:
** πατρίς**, ίδος,
_poetry_ _feminine_ of πάτριος, **of one's fathers**, πατρὶς αἶα, ἄρουρα, **one's fatherland, country**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.236, 20.193; π. γαῖα 24.322, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 1, 12, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 585}}; γῆ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 641, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 859 (_paratragoedia_)}}; π. πόλις the city **of one's sires**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).36.}}
**II** _substantive_, ={πάτρα}{{Refs 8th c.BC+|1, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.213, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.586, 9.34, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.140, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.69, etc.}}; κοινὴ π., i. e. **the nether world**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.113c: proverbial, π. γάρ ἐστι πᾶσ᾽ ἵν᾽ ἂν πράττῃ τις εὖ}}, **ubi bene, ibi patria**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1151}}; simply, **native town** or **village**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|“UPZ” 9.5 (2nd c.BC), NT.Mark.6.1, etc.: _plural_, ἐν ταῖς αὑτῶν π. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.296, compare 305, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 308a, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Epitaphius” 10.}}

3971:
** πατρῷος**, α, ον,
also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 210 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1147 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.; _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ **πατρώϊος**, η, ον, the only form used in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus, and 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}}; the former first in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 888, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.290}}, though both use the longer form, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 521, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.220}}; Thess. **πατρούεος** (which see): (πατήρ):— **of** or **from one's father, coming** or **inherited from him**, σκῆπτρον, ἔγχος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.46, 19.387; τέμενος, δῶμα, οἶκος, 20.391, 21.44, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 376}}; ξεῖνος πατρώϊός ἐσσι παλαιός my old **hereditary** friend, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.215; π. ἑταῖροι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.254, 17.69}}; γαῖα πατρωΐη one's **fatherland**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|13.188, 251; πατρῴα γῆ 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 888, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.290, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 67, etc.; π. οὖδας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 503; ἄστυ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1450}}; δῶμα, ἑστία, κοῖται, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1595, “Hec.” 22, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 194 (Lyric poetry)}}; πατρώϊα one's **father's goods, patrimony**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.80, 20.336, 22.61}}; τὰ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.26, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 819, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 27.11, variant in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1303b34; τὰ π. χρήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1658; θρόνος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 230, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 268, etc.}}; δοῦλοι π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.1; γέρεα [prev. author] 7.104; θυσίαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 3.30 codices; ἀρχή 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.6}}; π. δόξα **hereditary** glory, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 7.5.16 (but πατρῴα καὶ παππῴα δόξα}} **of our fathers** and grandfathers, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.73)}}; π. οἰκία, κλῆρος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.62, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 157e, “Lg.” 923d, etc.; οὐσία 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 45}}; ἡ εἰρήνη ἡ π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).68.13 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; ἔχων π. ἡμῶν ὑποθήκην {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 7339.6 (1st c.AD)}}; π. θεοί tutelary gods **of a family** or **people**, as Apollo at Athens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 933, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 302d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 55.3, “Sammelb.” 6262.5 (3rd c.AD)}}; Zeus among the Dorians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 162.3; πρὸς θεῶν π. καὶ μητρῴων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.21, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.69: _sing._, “Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.169 (from Cyrene)}}; Zeus was the θεὸς π. of Heracles, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 288, 753}}; of Orestes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 671}}; Ζεὺς π. was also the god **who protects parents' rights**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1468, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 881d, etc.}}
**II** ={πάτριος}, **of** or **belonging to one's father**, μῆλα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.136; π. πρὸς στάθμαν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.45}}; π. ἄεθλοι **imposed by him**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 4.220}}; but π. ἆθλος **of him**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 856 (Lyric poetry); π. γνώμη [prev. work] 640}}; π. φόνοι, πήματα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OC” 990, 1196; π. χέρες 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 210 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; τὰ πατρώϊα **the cause of one's father**, opposed to τὰ μητρώϊα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.53.}} —Grammars distinguished πατρῷος, as expressing **patrimonial possession**, from πάτριος as expressing hereditary manners, customs, institutions; see {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Ammonius Epigrammaticus “Diff.” s. see., “Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 297, 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus, etc.}}—The distinction holds in _Attic dialect_ Prose; but {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus use πατρώϊος}} only, and in all these senses; so also Trag. [πάτριος should be restored in all passages in Trag. where the _2nd pers._ syllable is made short in _anapaest meter_ and Lyric poetry, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 82, “Tr.” 162, “Ba.” 1368, “El.” 1315, Alcaeus249}}; but γῆς ἀπὸ πατρωΐης ends a pentameter in {{Refs|“IG” 12.978.]}}

3973:
** παύω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.67, etc.}}: _Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ παύεσκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.315, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 963 (Lyric poetry)}}: _future_ παύσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.207, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ παυσέμεν (κατα-) {{Refs|7.36:}}_aorist_ ἔπαυσα {{Refs|15.15, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ παῦσα {{Refs|17.602}}: _perfect_ πέπαυκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.70, 4th c.BC: Antisthenes Rhetor “Ὀδυσσεύς” 10}}:—_middle_ and _passive_, _Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ παυέσκετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.17}}: _future_ παύσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.198, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.56, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1040, “Ph.” 1424, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 93, etc.}}; πεπαύσομαι only {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 91, “Tr.” 587 (though held to be the true _Attic dialect_ form by Moer. p.293 P.)}}; παυσθήσομαι (variant παυθ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.81}}; later παήσομαι (ἀνα-) {{NT|NT.Rev.14.13}}: _aorist_ ἐπαυσάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.260}}; ἐπαύθην, _Epic dialect_ παύθην, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 533, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.91 (variant{παυσθῇ})}}], etc.; ἐπαύσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.94, etc.; later ἐπάην 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 2.141}} H.: _perfect_ πέπαυμαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.125, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 615, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.84, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 29, etc. (πεπάσθαι is _falsa lectio_ in Vett. Val. 359.31):}}
**I** causal, **make to end**,
**I.1** with _accusative_ only, **bring to an end, check**, sometimes of persons, ἵνα παύσομεν ἄγριον ἄνδρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.314, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 963 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 330}}; **stop** or **silence** by death, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.274, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 397}}:—_passive_ and _middle_, **take one's rest**, ἐνὶ κλισίῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.17, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.52, etc.}}; **cease, have done**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.295, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.103, etc.}}; of one singing or speaking, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|17.359, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.8.δ}}: generally, _middle_ denotes **willing**, _passive_ **forced, cessation.**
**I.1.b** mostly of things, **make an end of, stop, abate**, χόλον, μένος, νεῖκος πολέμοιο, ῥόον, ὀδύνας, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.67, 1.282, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.543, 5.451, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.528, etc.; μέριμναν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).13 (assuming variant)}}; λύπας ᾠδαῖς π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 197 (_anapaest meter_)}}, etc.; π. τόξον **let** the bow **rest**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.279; π. τοὺς γάμους 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 575; πόντου σάλον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1242}}; π. τὸν νόμον **annul** it, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Or.” 571}}; π. τὸν λόγον **close** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.6.7; τυραννίδα καταλύσαντα πεπαυκέναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.70}}; π. τείχη **raze** them, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 69.9}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.91, etc.}}
**I.2** with _accusative_ _person_ et _genitive_ _of things_, **hinder, keep back**, or **give** one **rest, from** a thing, π. Ἕκτορα μάχης, πόνοιο Ἀχιλῆα, Θάμυριν ἀοιδῆς, Πηνελόπειαν κλαυθμοῖο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.15, 21.137, 2.595, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.801}}; π. τινὰ ἀλκῆς, ἄλης, καμάτοιο, ὀδυνάων, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.250, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.342, 5.492, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.191; so π. χεῖρας πολέμοιο 21.294; ὀρχηθμοῖο πόδας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.298; π. τινὰ τῆς βοῆς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 798; τῆς ὕβρεως 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1259; τῆς λυγγός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 185d}}; τῆς ἁμαρτίας καὶ ἀμα θίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 784c; τῶν ἐπιθυμιῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.5}}; [τῆς νόσου] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).121.71 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; π. τινὰ τῆς βασιληΐης **depose** one **from** being king, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.123}}; τινὰ τῆς ἀρχῆς, τῆς στρατηγίας, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.6.3, “HG” 6.2.13; τῆς ἔξω ξυμμαχίας τινάς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.65; also π. τινὰ ἐκ κακῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 987; τινὰ ἀπὸ παιδαγωγῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 3.1; with _accusative_ unexpressed, αἴ κέ ποθι Ζεὺς.. παύσῃ ὀϊζύος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.35; φάρμαχ᾽ ἅ κεν παύσῃσι.. ὀδυνάων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.191}}:—_passive_ and _middle_, **rest** or **cease from** a thing, πολέμοιο, μάχης, ἔργων, πόνου, γόοιο, κλαυθμοῦ, ὀδυνάων, κλαγγῆς, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|21.432, 467, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.683, 24.384, 9.540, 17.7, 4.812, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.100, etc.}}; τῆς μάχης, τοῦ δρόμου, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.74, 4.124; γόων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1211; τῆς ὀργῆς 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.6; φιλανθρώπου τρόπου 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 11}}; παύεσθαι ἀρχῆς **to be deposed from**, or **reach the term of**, office, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.56, compare 6.66, “IG” 12.114.46; ἐκ μεγάλων ἀχέων παυσαίμεθ᾽ ἄν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1531 (Lyric poetry); ἐκ τρόχων πεπαυμένοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 46, compare “El.” 1108.}}
**I.3** with _present_ _participle_, **stop** a person **from**.., π. τινὰ ἀριστεύοντα **stop** him **from** doing bravely, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.506; τὸν ἄνδρα παῦσον ταῦτα ποιεῦντα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.23}}; γελῶντας ἐχθροὺς π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1295; παύσω δέ σ᾽ ὄντ᾽ ἄπαιδα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 717}}:—_passive_ and _middle_, **leave off doing**.., ὅθ᾽ ὕπνος ἕλοι, παύσαιτό τε νηπιαχεύων when **he stopped** playing, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.502, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 615, “Ag.” 1047, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.133, etc.}}; of things, ἄνεμος μὲν ἐπαύσατο.. θύων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.400: the}}_participle_ is frequently to be supplied, αἷμα, φλόξ, ἄνεμος ἐπαύσατο, the blood **stopped** [flowing], the fire [burning], the wind [blowing], {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.267, 23.228, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.168, etc.}}; so Ῥοδώπιος πέρι πέπαυμαι (i.e. λέγων) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.135, compare 7.10.}}
**I.4** less frequently with _infinitive_, **stop** a person **from**.., ἔμ᾽ ἔπαυσας ἐπὶ Τρώεσσι μάχεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.442; ῥαψῳδοὺς ἔπαυσε ἀγωνίζεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.67, compare 7.54: sometimes with μή}} inserted, θνητούς γ᾽ ἔπαυσα μὴ προδέρκεσθαι μόρον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 250; παύσας ὑμᾶς μὴ λίαν ἐξαπατᾶσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 634; also π. τὸ μὴ προσελθεῖν.. τὴν ὁλκάδα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.53; π. τοῦ.. εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 416c.}}
**I.4.b** _middle_ with _infinitive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Batrachomyomachia 193, “Anthologia Graeca” 6.21.8, and later Prose, as 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.216d.}}
**I.5** _middle_, **yield, give**, of timber, opposed to ἵστασθαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 5.6.3.}}
**II** _intransitive_ in _imperative_ παῦε, **cease, leave off** (παύου is rare, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 359, 4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 5.20, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Im.” 2), παῦε μάχης 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 449 codices, compare “hymnus ad Cererem” 351; παῦε γόοιο “epigram Gr.” 320.5 (from Thyatira): mostly _absolutely_, παῦε}} **stop! have done! be quiet!** παῦε, μὴ λέξῃς πέρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1275, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1208, “Ra.” 122, 269, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 228e; παῦε, παῦε, μὴ βόα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1504, compare “V.” 1194; also παῦε, παῦε τοῦ λόγου [prev. author] “Ra.” 580; παῦε, παῦ᾽ ὀρχούμενος [prev. author] “Pax” 326; παῦ᾽ ἐς κόρακας [prev. author] “Ach.” 864}}, where the other Verbs are _plural_; παῦ, _apocalyptic_ for παῦε, παῦ, μηδὲν ὄμνυ᾽ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 96, compare 2nd c.AD: Aelius Dionysius Grammaticus “fragment” 275, etc.}}: also _imperative_ _middle_, παῦσαι λέγουσα [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 706; παῦσαι φαρμακοπωλῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 28; π. μελῳδοῦσ᾽ “Comica Adespota” 601; π. δυσωνῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 224, compare 5th c.BC: Theopompus Comicus 62, {5-6th c.BC}: Philetaerus Comicus 6, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 213.1}}]; παύσασθε νοῦν ἔχοντες (to be read λέγοντες) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 482.1.}}

3975:
** παχύν-ω**,
_perfect_ _passive_ πεπάχυσμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 394a28, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “de gymnastica” 52, but πεπάχυμμαι 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.678, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.49.68}}:—**fatten**, τὰ σώματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 518c; βοῦν [prev. author] “R.” 343b; ἴππον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 12.20}}:—_passive_, **grow fat**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 5.44, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 791; δαιτὶ παχυνόμενος “Anthologia Graeca” 7.207 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus); παχυνθῇ ἡ ἀκρίς LXX.Eccl.12.5.}}
**b** **thicken, strengthen**, δεσμόν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 1.38.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **cause to wax fat, increase**, κότον π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 618}}:—_passive_, ὄλβος ἄγαν παχυνθείς {{Refs|[prev. author] “Th.” 771 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2.b** _metaphorically_, **make gross** or **dull**, τὰς ψυχὰς ὑπὸ πλης μονῆς π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.995d}}; βρώσεις τὸν νοῦν π. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista}}“V A” 1.8:— _passive_, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Isa.6.10; πεπαχυσμένος 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.21.1}}; **to be coarsened**, [ψυχὴ] παρὰ τὴν ἰδίαν φύσιν πεπαχυμμένη {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus [prev. cited]; περιβλήματα.. παχυνόμενα ὑπὸ τῆς γηίνης φύσεως 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus}}**in R.**1.119 K.
**II** _passive_, **become thick**, π. πρὸς τὸν ἥλιον, of the skull, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.12}}; of humours, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 19}}; of excrements, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Prog.” 11, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 735a36, +others}}; distinguished from πήγνυσθαι, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mete.” 383a11, b18}}; of fruit juices, **become concentrated**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.16.2}}; of oil, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “de gymnastica” 52.}}
**II.2** **to be dulled**, of the sun's light, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 35.}}

3976:
** πέδη**, ἡ,
(πέζα) **fetter**: in _plural_, **shackles**, ἀμφὶ δὲ ποσσὶ πέδας ἔβαλε χρυσείας, of horses, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.36}}; of men, τοῖς ἀδίκοις ἀμφιτίθησι πέδας {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 4.34, compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 539, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 6, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἥρως” 3, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.95}}; πεδέων ζεῦγος pair **of fetters**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.35}}; ἐν πέδαις (variant{ἐς πέδας}) δῆσαί τινα put one in **fetters**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.77; αἱ πέδαι, ἐν τῇσι ἐδεδέατο [prev. passage]; ἐν πέδαις δῆσαι, φυλάττειν, etc., 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 882b, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.181b, etc.: _metaphorically_, πέδαις ἀχαλκεύτοισι}}, of the robe in which Agamemnon was entangled, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 493; πέδας χειροῖν καὶ ποδοῖν [prev. work] 982}}: in _singular_, of the poisoned robe of Nessus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1057}}; π. Ἑλληνικαί, of the fortresses of Chalcis, Corinth, and Demetrias, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.11.5, etc.}}
**2** **anklet, bangle**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 320.11, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 81, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Lex.” 9.}}
**3** of fishing-**nets**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 670.5.}}
**II** **mode of breaking in a horse by riding him in a figure-of-eight course** (compare ἱπποπέδη), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 3.5, 7.13; π. ἑτερομήκης, κυκλοτερής, [prev. work] 14.}}

3977:
** πεδῐεινός**, ή, όν,
**flat, level**, χῶρος [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.198 (variant{πεδινός}); πεδιναὶ ὑποχωρήσεις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.34.8; τὰ πεδινά 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 880b28, 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 1.2, 1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 18, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Nicias” 26}}]: _comparative_ πεδιεινότερος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 704d; πεδινώτερος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.5.2.}}
**II** **of the plain, found on the plain**, opposed to ὄρειος, λαγώς [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyn.” 5.17. (πεδινός, variant{πεδεινός}); [δένδρα] πεδεινά 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.8.1, compare 3.11.2}}]; πεδινὸν ἄνθος, ={ἀργεμώνη}, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.177. (πεδιεινός may have become πεδῑνός (written also πεδεινός)}} about 2nd c.BC; πεδῐνός is uncertain, since πεδινώτερος may be _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “An.” [same place])}}

3978:
** πεζ-εύω**,
**go** or **travel on foot, walk**, ἐπὶ γαίας πόδα πεζεύων (where πόδα is _pleonastic_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 869 (_anapaest meter_); οὔτε ἄπουν οὔτε πεζεῦον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 669b7}}; π. περὶ τὴν τροφήν, of certain birds, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 593a25, compare “GA” 751b13.}}
**2** **go** or **travel by land**, opposed to going by sea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.5. [4], 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.29.11; π. μετὰ τῶν ἵππων [prev. author] 10.48.6}}; οἱ πεζεύοντες **land-forces**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1327b10}}; π.διὰ τῆς θαλάσσης, of Xerxes passing by his bridge over the Hellespont, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.89}}; π. τὴν θάλασσαν **pass** it **like dry land**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.8}}; simply, **march, pass through**, ἀνοδίας {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.257}}:— _passive_, ὁ Ἄθως πλείσθω καὶ ὁ Ἑλλήσποντος πεζευέσθω {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Rh.Pr.” 18}}; ἡ ἐκ Βρεντεσίου πεζευομένη ὁδός **by land**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.3.5}}; πεζεύεται _impersonal_, ταῖς ἁρμαμάξαις {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.1.14.}}

3979:
Included with: ** πεζός**, ή, όν,
(see. πούς):
**1** in Poets, especially _Epic dialect_,
**1.a** **on foot, walking**, πεζοί **fighters on foot**, opposed to those in chariots, πεζοί θ᾽ ἱππῆές τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.59, compare 5.13, 11.150}}; πλῆτο δὲ πᾶν πεδίον πεζῶν τε καὶ ἵππων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.436, compare 9.50.}}
**1.b** **on land, going by land**, opposed to sea-faring, especiallyin {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”; εἰ δ᾽ ἐθέλεις π., πάρα τοι δίφρος τε καὶ ἵπποι 3.324; οὐ μὲν γάρ τί σε πεζὸν ὀΐομαι ἐνθάδ᾽ ἱκέσθαι 1.173; ἔφθης πεζὸς ἰὼν ἢ ἐγὼ σὺν νηῒ μελαίνῃ 11.58, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.29}}; ἐν νηῒ θοῇ ἢ π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.438.}}
**2**in Prose, ὁ π. (with or without στρατός),
**2.a** sometimes **infantry**, opposed to cavalry (ἡ ἵππος), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80, 4.128; σὺν δυνάμει καὶ π. καὶ ἱππικῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.18}}; but,
**2.b** more frequently **land-force, army**, opposed to naval force, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.97, 6.95, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.47, 2.94, etc.}}; τὸ π. variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.81}}; στρατιὰ καὶ ναυτικὴ καὶ π. [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.33, compare 7.16 (and see at {πεζικός}); ἡ π. στρατιὰ καὶ τὸ ναυτικόν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.34, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 558 (Lyric poetry), 719, 728 (both troch.); οἱ μὲν ἐφ᾽ ἵππων, οἱ δ᾽ ἐπὶ ναῶν, πεζοί τε βάδην [prev. work] 19}}]; τὰ π. κράτιστοι strongest **by land**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.12; καὶ ναυσὶ καὶ πεζοῖσι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 622; π. μάχαισιν [prev. author] “Eq.” 567}}; ἡ π. μάχη battle **by land**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 707c}}; ἐν τοῖς ναυτικοῖς κινδύνοις, ὥσπερ ἐν τοῖς π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.91.}}
**3** of animals, **land**, opposed to birds and fishes, τὰ π. καὶ τὰ πτηνά **beasts** and birds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 207a, compare “Plt.” 264e}}; π. καὶ ἔνυδρον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 288a, compare “Lg.” 823b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 143b1, etc.; ἡ π. θήρα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 222b, compare “Lg.” 824a.}}
**II** _metaphorically_ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|compare (αὐτὰρ ἐγὼ Μουσέων πεζὸς ἔπειμι νομόν 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 4.1.9)}}, of language, **prosaic**, λόγοι π. **prose**{{Refs 1st c.BC+|(compare 111.3), 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 6, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 4.6.1}}; διὰ πεζῶν [λ.] {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.87 K.; λόγος “POxy.” 724.10 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἡ π. διάλεκτος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 3; ἡ π. λέξις [prev. work] 1; opposed to ἡ ἔμμετρος, [prev. work] 4}}; ἡ π. alone, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.2.6; τινὰ καὶ πεζὰ καὶ ἐν ἔπεσι ποιήματα 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 69.3}}; π. τις ποιητική, of bombastic prose, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 8}}; κομιδῇ πεζὸν καὶ χαμαιπετές{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 16, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.853c; τὰ ἄγαν π. καὶ κακόμετρα [ὀνόματα] [prev. work]747f; π. ὀνόματα, opposed to ποιητικά, 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 167.}}
**II.2** of verse, **unaccompanied by music**, καὶ πεζὰ καὶ φορμικτά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 16}}; πεζῷ γόῳ· ἄνευ αὐλοῦ ἢ λύρας, {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.; compare 111.2.}}
**II.3** **common, ordinary**, ἑταῖραι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 205; μόσχοι 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 169; π. αὐλητρίς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 155.}}
**III** _dative_ _feminine_ **πεζῇ** (i.e. ὁδῷ) as _adverb_,
**III.1** **on foot**, opposed to σὺν ἵππῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 5.5.}}
**III.1.b** more commonly, **by land**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.159, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.94, etc.}}; π. ἕπεσθαι to follow **by land**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.110, 115; στρατιὰν μέλλων π. πορεύσειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.132; π. πορεύεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.1; οὔτε π. οὔτε κατὰ θάλατταν [prev. work] 5.6.10}}; καὶ π. καὶ ναυμαχοῦντες **by land** and by sea, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.24.}}
**III.2** **without musical accompaniment**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(compare 11.2), παῦσαι μελῳδοῦσ᾽ ἀλλὰ π. μοι φράσον “Comica Adespota” 601, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 237a.}}
**III.3** regular _adverb_ πεζῶς **in prose**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.165 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see at {ἱστορῆσαι}.}}]
**IV** _comparative_ πεζότερος **more like a foot-journey**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.804d}}; **more like prose**, στίχοι π. τῇ συνθέσει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.252, etc.: _superlative_ πεζότατος, τὸ π. μόριον τῆς ψυχή,compare 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Timaeum commentarii” 3.317 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}

3982:
** πείθω**,
**persuade**, _imperfect_ ἔπειθον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.91, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry πεῖθον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|16.842, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 8.16}}: _future_ πείσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.345, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ πεισέμεν {{Refs|5.252}}: _aorist 1_ ἔπεισα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.80, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 84, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 304, etc. (8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} has only _optative_ πείσειε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.123)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ _participle_ πείσαις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.16}}: _aorist 2_ ἔπῐθον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “P.” 3.65 (poetry πίθον), 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.58 (poetry dual πιθέταν), 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 941, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 949, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.64, used by 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} only in _Epic dialect_ _reduplicate_ forms πεπίθωμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.112, πεπίθοιμι 23.40, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.14, πεπῐθεῖν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.184, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.536, πεπῐθών 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).72 (see. below), πεπιθοῦσα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.26 (_indicative_ not in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” or 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”, πέπιθον 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.964, πέπιθε “hymnus ad Apollinem” 275)}}: _perfect_ πέπεικα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 26.7, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.24, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.15}}:—_middle_ and _passive_ **πείθομαι**, **obey**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.79}}, etc.: _future_ πείσομαι{{Refs|[prev. work] 289}}, etc.: _aorist 2_ ἐπῐθόμην, _Epic dialect_ πιθόμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5.201, ἐπίθετο 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 73, ἐπίθοντο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.260, “IG” 22.29.14, reduplicate πεπίθοντο 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 135; _imperative_ πίθεο 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.59, πιθοῦ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 992, _plural_ πίθεσθε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 794}}; _subjunctive_ πίθωμαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.273, etc.; _optative_ πιθοίμην 4.93, etc. (reduplicate πεπίθοιτο 10.204)}}; _infinitive_ πιθέσθαι {{Refs|7.293, etc. (πεπιθέσθαι “Anthologia Graeca” 14.75)}}; _participle_ πιθόμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1226}}: _aorist 1_ _middle_ ἐπεισάμην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12(5).720.5 (Andros, 2nd c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.391 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, 4th c.BC: Sopater Comicus in Rhetores Graeci 8.150}} W.: _future_ _passive_ πεισθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 624, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 248e, etc.}}: _aorist 1_ ἐπείσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 593, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 526, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 866, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.29}}: _perfect_ πέπεισμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 697, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 578, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 328e}}; Thess. _perfect_ _infinitive_ πεπεῖστειν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 9(2).517.16 (Larissa, 3rd c.BC).}}
**II** _intransitive_ tenses of _active_, in _passive_ sense, _perfect_ 2 πέποιθα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.325, etc. (not frequently in Prose); _imperative_ πέπεισθι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 599 codices}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ _subjunctive_ πεποίθῃς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.524}}; _Epic dialect_ _1st pers._ _plural_ πεποίθομεν (for-ωμεν) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.335; _optative_ πεποιθοίη 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 940}}: _pluperfect_ ἐπεποίθειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.171}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐπεποίθεσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.88}}; _Epic dialect_ πεποίθεα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.434, 8.181}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ ἐπέπιθμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.341, 4.159: 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus uses}}_aorist 2_ _participle_ πιθών ={πιθόμενος}, {{Refs|“P.” 3.28, reduplicate πεπιθών “I.” 4(3).72.}}
**III** as if from **πῐθέω**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} has _future_ πῐθήσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.369}} (**obey**): _aorist_ _participle_ πῐθήσας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.398}} (**trust**),{{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 359, 671, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.109, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 618 (Lyric poetry), 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 735}}; _reduplicate_ _aorist_ _subjunctive_ πεπῐθήσω _transitive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.223}}:—also _Aeolic dialect_ **πίθημι**, _participle_ πίθεις {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 9.4.}}
**A** _active_, **prevail upon, persuade**, usually by fair means, τινα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.345, etc.}}; πεπιθεῖν φρένας Αἰακίδαο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 184; σοὶ δὲ φρένας ἄφρονι πεῖθε 16.842; τοῦ θυμὸν ἐνὶ στήθεσσιν ἔπειθον 9.587, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.258, 23.337; Ἕκτορι θυμὸν ἔπειθε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.78}}: with _accusative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, **persuade** one **to**..,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 223, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 724, etc.}}; π. τινὰς ὥστε δοῦναι, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.5, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.31, etc.}}; ὥστε μὴ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 901}}; later ἵνα.. {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Matt.27.20, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.181a}}; π. τινὰς ὡς χρὴ.., ὡς ἔστι.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 327c, 364b; π. τινὰ ἐς τὴν ὁμολογίαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.76; κοὐδείς γέ μ᾽ ἂν πείσειεν.. τὸ μὴ ἐλθεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 68}}; πείθω ἐμαυτόν I **persuade** myself, **am persuaded, believe**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.33, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.70, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 453b, etc.; also π. τι ὠφέλιμον ὄν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.17: frequently in}}_participle_, πείσας **by persuasion, by fair means**, opposed to ἐν δόλῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 102, compare 612}}; opposed to βίᾳ, {{Refs|“Tragica Adespota” 402}}; πόλιν πείσας **having obtained** the city's **consent**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1298; δᾶμον πείσαις λόγῳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.16}}; μὴ πείσας unless **by leave**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 844e; οὐ πείσαντες τὸν δῆμον 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.41}}; πείθοντες, opposed to βίᾳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.5.11}}; π. γυναῖκα, opposed to βιάζεσθαι, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 6.1.34}}; πέπεικε, opposed to ἠνάγκακε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hipparchus” 232b (but π. ἀνάγκῃ 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 62.16, compare πειθανάγκη)}}: with _neuter_ _pronoun_, **persuade** one to or of a thing, τοῦτό γε οὐκ ἔπειθε τοὺς Φωκαιέας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.163, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1064 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 399b, etc.; ἔπειθον οὐδέν᾽ οὐδέν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1212}}; μὴ πεῖθ᾽ ἃ μὴ δεῖ **do** not **attempt to persuade** me to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1442}}; also τοιάνδ᾽ ἔπειθε ῥῆσιν **addressed** them thus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 615.}}
**2** **prevail on by entreaty**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.219, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.363; τότε κέν μιν ἱλασσάμενοι πεπίθοιμεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.100; ὥς κέν μιν ἀρεσσάμενοι πεπίθωμεν 9.112, compare 181, 386, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 450; Ζηνὸς ἦτορ λιταῖς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.80, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 366a, “Ap.” 37d: with double _accusative_, τὸν φόρον ὑποτελῶ Ἀθηναίοισιν, ὃν ἂν πείθω Ἀθηναίους “IG” 12.39.27.}}
**II** in bad sense, **talk over, mislead**, ἐπεὶ οὐ παρελεύσεαι οὐδέ με πείσεις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.132, compare 6.360; ἔληθε δόλῳ καὶ ἔπειθεν Ἀχαιούς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.106, compare 14.123; πεπιθοῦσα θυέλλας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.26.}}
**II.2** π. τινὰ χρήμασι **bribe**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.134, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 21.10}}; π. ἐπὶ μισθῷ μισθῷ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.4, 9.33, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.96, etc.}} (_passive_, χρήμασι πεισθείς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.137): proverbial, δῶρα θεοὺς πείθει 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 272}}; πείθειν τινά alone, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.21, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.19, NT.Act.12.20.}}
**II.3** offood, **tempt**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Xenocrates Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 2.58.84.}}
**B** _passive_ and _middle_, **to be prevailed on, won over, persuaded**, _absolutely_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.201, etc.}}; _imperative_ frequently in Trag., πείθου **be persuaded**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 520, “El.” 1015, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 440}}; but πιθοῦ **comply**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1181, “El.” 1207}}: with _infinitive_, **to be persuaded** to do, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 624}}; πείθεσθέ μοι πρύτανιν ἑλέσθαι {{Refs|Pl. “Prt.” 338a}}; also πείθεσθαί τινι ὥστε.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.2}}; ὃ.. ὑμεῖς.. ἥκιστ᾽ ἂν ὀξέως πείθοισθε (i.e. πρᾶξαι) {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.34}}; ἑκὼν καὶ πεπεισμένος of one's own **free will,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|POxy. ivp 203 (4th c.AD)}}, etc.; τὰ μὲν παρ᾽ ἡμῶν ἴσθι σοι πεπεισμένα we are **won over** to you, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1170.}}
**B.2** πείθεσθαί τινι **listen to** one, **obey** him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.79, etc.}}; τοῖς ἐν τέλει βεβῶσι π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 67}}; τοῖς ἄρχουσι, τῷ νόμῳ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.8, “An.” 7.3.39; μᾶλλον τῷ θεῷ ἢ ὑμῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 29d: sometimes with double _dative_, ἔπεσι, μύθοισι π. τινί, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.150, 23.157: without _dative_ _person_, ἐπείθετο μύθῳ 1.33, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.177}}; γήραϊ πείθεσθαι **yield, succumb** to old age, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.645}}; στυγερῇ πειθώμεθα δαιτί **let us comply with** the custom of eating, sad though the meal be, {{Refs|[prev. work] 48; νῦν μὲν πειθώμεθα νυκτὶ μελαίνῃ, of leaving off the labours of the day, 8.502}}; ἀδίκοις ἔργμασι π. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 4.11, 13.12.}}
**B.2.b** with _adjective_ _neuter_, σημάντορι πάντα πιθέσθαι **obey** him **in** all things, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.21}}; ἅ τιν᾽ οὐ πείσεσθαι ὀΐω wherein I think some **will** not **obey**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.289, compare 4.93, 7.48, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.100, etc.; πάντ᾽ ἔγωγε πείσομαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 529; πείσομαι δ᾽ ἃ σοὶ δοκεῖ [prev. author] “Tr.” 1180; οὐ.. πείθομαι τὸ δρᾶν [prev. author] “Ph.” 1252; μύθοις.. πεισθεὶς ἀφανῆ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1288 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.3: rarely with Noun in _accusative_, χρήμασι πεισθῆναι [τὴν ἀναχώρησιν] 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.21 (assuming variant).}}
**B.3** with _genitive_, four times in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, πείθεσθαί τινος 1.126, 5.29, 33, 6.12, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 726, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.73; πείσθητί μευ 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.66}}; κείνου.. πιθοίατο _vulgar form_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.57.}}
**B.II** πείθεσθαί τινι **believe, trust in**, πείθεθ᾽ ἑταίρῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.45; οἰωνοῖσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.238; τεράεσσι θεῶν καὶ Ζηνὸς ἀρωγῇ 4.408; ἐνυπνίῳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.79; λεγομένοισι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.146, etc.}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **believe that**.., οὐ γάρ πω ἐπείθετο ὃν πατέρ᾽ εἶναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.192, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8, etc.: with _dative_ _person_ et}}_infinitive_, π. τινὶ μὴ εἶναι χρήματα, ={ὅτι χρήματα οὐκ ἔχει}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.8.3: with ὡς, οὐ πείσονται ὡς σὺ αὐτὸς οὐκ ἠθέλησας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 44c, compare “R.” 391b: with _neuter_}} _adjective_ or _pronoun_, τὰ περὶ Αἴγυπτον τοῖσι λέγουσι αὐτὰ π., οὐκ ἐπείθοντο τὰ ἐσαγγελθέντα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.12, 8.81; πείθεσθε τούτῳ ταῦτα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 592}}; ταῦτ᾽ ἐγώ σοι οὐ πείθομαι I **do** not **take** this **on your word**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 25e, compare “Phdr.” 235b: _absolutely_, ὡς ἐγὼ πείθομαι 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.34.}}
**B.II.b** π. τινὰ ὅπως.. **to believe of** him, that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1251.}}
**B.III** _perfect_ {{Refs|2 πέποιθα}}**trust, rely on**, with _dative_ _person_ vel _of things_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.325, etc.}} (not frequently in early Prose, as αὑτῷ πεποιθέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 248a)}}: with _dative_ et _infinitive_, οὔ πω χερσὶ πέποιθα ἄνδρ᾽ ἀπαμύνασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.71, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.96, etc.: with _dative_, οἷσι.. μαρναμένοισι πέποιθε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.98: later with}}_infinitive_ only, πέποιθα τοῦτ᾽ ἐπισπάσειν κλέος I **trust** to win this fame, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 769}}; αἰχμήν.. μᾶλλον θεοῦ σέβειν πεποιθώς **daring to**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 530: once in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, χρήμασι ἐπεποίθεσαν διώσεσθαι 9.88}}: rarely with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, πέποιθα.. τὸν πυρφόρον ἥξειν κεραυνόν {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 444; εἴ τις πέποιθεν ἑαυτῷ Χριστοῦ εἶναι NT.2Cor.10.7}}; π. εἴς τινας ὅτι.. {{NT|NT.Gal.5.10}}; ἐπί τινας ὅτι.. {{NT|NT.2Cor.2.3; ἐπὶ χρήμασι NT.Mark.10.24: _absolutely_, ὄφρα πεποίθῃς}} that **you may feel confidence**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.524, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.344}}; πεποιθώς **in sure confidence**, {{LXX|LXX.Deu.33.28.}}
**B.IV** post-{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}_perfect_ _passive_ πέπεισμαι **believe, trust**, with _dative_, νεκροῖσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 599; ὀνείροις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1190, etc.: with _accusative_ et}}_infinitive_, συνοίσειν ταῦτα πέπ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.51, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 368a: _absolutely_, νῦν δὲ πέπεισμαι [prev. author] “Prt.” 328e}}; πεπεισμένος ἔκ τινων λογίων **persuaded** by.., {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 14; πεπείσμεθα περὶ ὑμῶν τὰ κρείττονα NT.Heb.6.9}}. (Cf. Latin _fido_, **fides.**)

3983:
** πειν-άω**,
forms in αε _contraction_ into η not ᾱ (as in διψάω,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 42)}}, πεινῇς, -ῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1270}}; _infinitive_ πεινῆν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Nu.” 441, “Pl.” 595, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 496c}}; _Epic dialect_ πεινήμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.137 (only here in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”)}}: _imperfect_ ἐπείνων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.2.15}}: _future_ πεινήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 539, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.17}}; later πεινάσω [ᾱ] {{LXX+NT|LXX.Sir.24.21, +others, NT.Rev.7.16}}: _aorist_ ἐπείνησα {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.39, ἐπείνᾱσα LXX.Gen.41.55, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “epigram” 50, 5th-4th c.BC: Aesopus Fabularum Scriptor 62, 3rd c.AD: Pseudo-Callisthenes Historicus 3.6}}: _perfect_ πεπείνηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 606a}}: later we find the _contraction_ of αε into ᾱ, ἐπείνας, πεινᾷ, -ᾶν, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Deu.25.18, NT.Rom.12.20, etc.}}: (πεῖνἀ):—**to be hungry**, πεινάων, of a lion, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.25; λέοντε.. ἄμφω πεινάοντε 16.758; λέοντα.. μέγα πεινάοντα 18.162}}; κακῶς π. **to be starved**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.13, 14; π. βάδην 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 535}}; thrice in Trag., πεινῶσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 199; πεινῶντι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 895, 5th c.BC: Achaeus Tragicus 25; πεινᾶντι δὲ μηδὲ ποτένθῃς 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.148: _metaphorically_, πεινῆν φασι τὴν γῆν 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.6.2.}}
**II** with _genitive_, **hunger after**, σίτου δ᾽ οὐκέτ᾽ ἔφη πεινήμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.137.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **hunger, crave after**, χρημάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.39, etc.; ἐπαίνου [prev. author] “Oec.” 13.9}}; simply, **to be in want of, lack**, πεινῶντες ἀγαθῶν {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 521a; μάλα π. συμμάχων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.50, etc.: later with _accusative_, οἱ π. καὶ διψῶντες τὴν δικαιοσύνην NT.Matt.5.6}}. [ᾱ in un _contraction_ forms in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus With this the _Doric dialect_ contractions δια-πεινᾶμες (see. διαπεινάω) and πεινᾶντι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus}} [same place], and the _Attic dialect_ contractions (-η- from -ηε-) agree; but πεινῶντι _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _present_ _indicative_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.23}} codices points to -ᾰοντι.]

3984:
** πεῖρα**, ας
(_Ionic dialect_ πεῖρα, _accusative_ πεῖραν, _genitive_ -ης), _Aeolic dialect_ **πέρρα** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus in “An. Ox.” 2.252}}: ἡ:— **trial, attempt**, π. τοι μαθήσιος ἀρχά {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 63}}; opposed to δόξα, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 571; πείρᾳ δ᾽ οὐ προσωμίλησά πω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 591; πικρὰν πεῖραν τολμήσειν [prev. author] “El.” 471; πείρᾳ σφαλῆναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70}}; ἢν μὲν ξυμβῇ ἡ π. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.3; πείρᾳ θην πάντα τελεῖται 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.62}}; πεῖραν ἔχοντες **being proved**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.76}}; but πεῖραν ἔχειν τινός to have **experience** of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.5}}; π. τινῶν ἔχειν ὅτι.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 3.2.16}}; π. ἔχει τῆς γνώμης involves **a trial** of your resolution, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.140}}; πεῖράν τινος λαμβάνειν or λαβεῖν to make **trial** or **proof** of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 691, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.236, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 448a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.6.33, etc.}}; also, gain **experience** of.., ἐν ἑαυτῷ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 5.8.15; π. λ. τινός, ὅπως ἔχει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 342a; π. λ. τινός, εἰ ἄρα τι λέγει [prev. author] “Thg.” 129d}}; πεῖράν τινος διδόναι (cf. Latin _specimen sui edere_) Darei Epist. in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 22.21, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.138, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.45; π. τῆς δόξης δοῦναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.11; π. ἔργῳ δεδωκέναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.107, compare 195; π. ἀλλήλων λαμβάνοντες καὶ διδόντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 348a; πεῖραν ποιήσασθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.53}}; π. ποιεῖσθαι εἰ.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.20; ταῖς π. βασανίζειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 747a3; πεῖραν καθεῖναι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 2.13, compare “NA” 1.39}}; π. δέξασθαι **undertake**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pyrrhus” 5.}}
**2** with Preps., ἀπὸ πείρης by **experiment**, opposed to αὐτόματον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.9. ; διὰ πείρας ἰέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 369a; διὰ π. ἔργων ἐλήλυθε 1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus “Praef.” 7}}; ἀποδοκιμασθῆναι διὰ τῆς π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1341a37; ἐς πεῖραν ἤλθομεν φίλων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 309, etc.}}; ἰέναι ἐς τὴν π. τοῦ ναυτικοῦ **try** an action by sea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.21}}; ἀκοῆς κρείσσων ἐς π. ἔρχεται turns out **on trial** greater than report, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.41; ἐκ τῆς π. δῆλον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 938b38}}; Κύρου ἐν πείρᾳ γενέσθαι to have been **acquainted** with Cyrus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.1; ἐν π. τέλος διαφαίνεται 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.70}}; ἐπὶ πείρᾳ by way of **test** or **trial**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 583}}; ἐπὶ π. δούς **on trial**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 118}}; π. θανάτου πέρι καὶ ζωᾶς a **contest** for.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.28.}}
**II** **attempt on** or **against** one, πεῖράν τιν᾽ ἐχθρῶν ἁρπάσαι **a means of attacking**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 2; τοιοῦδε φωτὸς π. εὖ φυλακτέον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 499}}; especially **attempt to seduce** a woman, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 26, “Cim.” 1}}: _absolutely_, **attempt, enterprise**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 719 (troch.), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.20}}; πεῖραν ἀφορμᾶν to go forth upon **an enterprise**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 290}}; compare πειρατής. (Cf. Latin _experior_, **peritus.**)

3985:
** πειράζω**,
(πεῖρα) used by early authors only in _present_ and _imperfect_, the other tenses being supplied by πειράω, -άομαι: Cretan dialect _future_ πειράξω {{Refs|“GDI” 5181}}: _aorist 1_ _imperative_ πείρᾰσον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.183 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus)}}: _aorist_ _passive_ ἐπειράσθην {{LXX+NT|LXX.Wis.11.9, NT.Matt.4.1}}: _perfect_ _participle_ πεπειρασμένος {{NT|NT.Heb.4.15}}:—**make proof** or **trial of**, with _genitive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.319.23.114: _absolutely_, 9.281.}}
**2** with _infinitive_, **attempt** to do, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.6.9, LXX.Judg.6.39, NT.Act.16.7, 24.6}}; π. τι **attempt** a thing, ἄλλος ἄλλην ἐπ᾽ ἐμὲ πειράζει τέχνην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Trag.” 149, compare 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.40; τόδε τόξον}}**make trial of,** {{Refs|Anacreont. 31.24}}: _absolutely_, **make an attempt**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus “Fragmenta” 195.}}
**3** _passive_, ἤθη.. ἐν χρόνῳ πειράζεται **are tried, proved**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 573}}; **to be experienced**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 32.}}
**II** with _accusative_ _person_, **try, tempt** a person, **put** him **to the test**, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.22.1, +others}}; ἑαυτοὺς πειράζετε, εἰ.. {{NT|NT.2Cor.13.5, +others}}; τί πειράζετε τὸν Κύριο; {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.17.2, cf. NT.Act.15.10, NT.1Cor.10.9, +others}}
**II.2** in bad sense, **seek to seduce, tempt**, Ἀθηναίην {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.10: _absolutely_, ὁ πειράζων}} **the Tempter**, {{NT|NT.1Thes.3.5, etc.}}:—_passive_, **to be sorely tried**, πειραζομένη βασανίζομαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“PLit.Lond.” 52.5; στομακάκκῃ 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 16.4.24}}; **to be attacked**, ὑποχύσει {{Refs 1st c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “Problemata - Physici et Medici Graeci Minores” 2.54; ἐπιληψίᾳ 1st c.AD: Cyranus 47}}; **to be tempted to sin,** {{NT|NT.Matt.4.1, +others}}

3986:
** πειρ-ασμός**, ὁ,
**trial**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Deu.4.34, +others, NT.1Pet.4.12}}; οἱ ἐπὶ τῶν παθῶν π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1 “Praef.” 5; πειρασμοὶ ἐν τῇ γῇ καὶ θαλάσσῃ 1st c.AD: Cyranus 40.}}
**2** **worry**, {{LXX|LXX.Eccl.5.2.}}
**II** **temptation**,{{LXX+NT|LXX.Sir.44.20, NT.Mark.14.38, etc.}}

3987:
** πειράω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.8, etc.}}: _imperfect_
**A ** ἐπείρων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.25:}}_future_ -άσω [ᾱ] {{Refs|[prev. work] 9, 43}}: _aorist_ ἐπείρᾱσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1276, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 517, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.54}}: _perfect_ πεπείρᾱκα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 26:}} —_passive_, _aorist_ ἐπειράθην [ᾱ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.54}}; compare πειράζω.
**B ** more frequently in _middle_ **πειράομαι**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.193, 24.390, etc.:}}_future_ -άσομαι [ᾱ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 959, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ _2nd pers._ _plural_ πειρασεῖσθε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 743, cf. Hippodamus Pythagoreus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.1.94; later πειρᾱθήσομαι “Geoponica” 12.13.12:}}_aorist_ ἐπειρᾱσάμην, _Ionic dialect_ ἐπειρησάμην, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.120, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.135, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.44, +others}}; but _aorist_ _passive_ ἐπειρήθην, _Attic dialect_ ἐπειράθην [ᾱ], found in _middle_ sense, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.384, +others, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.152, +others, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.5 (variant), 33, 6.92, and in later Prose:}}_perfect_ πεπείρᾱμαι, _Ionic dialect_ -ημαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.23, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 110, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.46, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 584, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.1, etc.}}: _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _pluperfect_ ἐπεπείραντο {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus “fragment” 24.3}}, _Ionic dialect_ ἐπεπειρέατο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.125. (From πεῖρα.)}}
**A** _active_, **attempt, endeavour, try**, with _infinitive_, μήτε τις.. πειράτω διακέρσαι ἐμὸν ἔπος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.8; πειρήσω (δύναμαι) φηλητέων ὄρχαμος εἶναι “hymnus ad Mercurium” 175; π. ἐς τὴν Μηδικὴν ἐσβάλλειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.84, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1025, +others: followed by ὡς.., 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.66, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.316, etc.; by ὅπως.., 4.545}}: with _adjective_ _neuter_, πολλὰ πειρῶντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.38; πάντα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1122b}}: with _infinitive_ understood, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.32.}}
**A.II** with _genitive_ _person_, **make trial of** one, μή μευ πειράτω, for the purpose of persuading, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.345, compare 24.433}}; of things, τευχέων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.1249}}: in hostile sense, **make an attempt on**, μήλων πειρήσοντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.301, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.134}}; οὐ πειρᾶν τῆς πόλιος, πρὶν.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.82; π. τοῦ χωρίου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.61; Νισαίας [prev. author] 4.70; ἀλλήλων [prev. author] 7.38; νυμφείας εὐνᾶς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.30.}}
**A.III** _absolutely_, ναυσὶ π. **make an attempt** by sea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.25; π. ἐπὶ τὴν κώμην [prev. work] 43.}}
**A.IV** with _accusative_ _of things_, **experience**, τύχης ἐπήρειαν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 46.}}
**A.IV.2** with _accusative_ _person_, **make an attempt on** a woman's honour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 517 (ubiv. Scholia), “Pl.” 150, 1067, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.12, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.28, etc.}}:—_passive_, πειραθεὶς ὁ Ἁρμόδιος ὑπὸ Ἱππάρχου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.54, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 227c}}; see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus IV, compare πεῖρα 11.}}
**B** more frequently in _middle_, with _infinitive_, **try to** do, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.5, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.71, 6.138, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 459, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 6.2, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.64, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.41, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 186b}}: with _future_ _infinitive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.8.4: the}}_infinitive_ is sometimes understood, πειρήσεται (i.e. ἀλύξαι) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.417: followed by εἰ, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.806, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 95b; πειρήσεται αἴ κε θέῃσιν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.601}}; by ἐάν or ἄν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 327, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 638e}}; by μὴ.., **lest**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.394}}; by ὅπως.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.3}}: with _participle_, frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, ἐπειρᾶτο ἐπιών 1.77}}; προσβαίνων{{Refs|[prev. work] 84; π. βιώμενοι 4.139; π. ἀποσχίζων 6.9, compare 5, 50, 7.139, +others}}; π. σκοπῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 190e}}?? with _neuter_ _adjective_, τὰ μεγάλα καὶ τὰ μικρὰ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.5.14.}}
**B.II** most frequently (V.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11) with _genitive_}}
**B.II.1** with _genitive_ _person_, **make trial of** one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.444, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.336, etc.}}; νῦν σεῦ, ξεῖνε, ὀΐω πειρήσεσθαι, εἰ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|.. 19.215; ἕο αὐτοῦ ἐν ἔντεσι.. εἰ οἷ ἐφαρμόσσειε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.384; with _genitive_ not expressed, ἔπεσιν πειρήσομαι 2.73}}; ἦ πρῶτ᾽ ἐξερέοιτο ἕκαστά τε πειρήσαιτο **test** him in each particular, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.119 (variant{μυθήσαιτο})}}]; π. θεοῦ **make trial of, tempt** a god, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.86. γ,compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1663 (troch.)}}: in hostile sense, πρὶν πειρήσαιτ᾽ Ἀχιλῆος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.580 (with _accusative_ _cognate_ added ἀέθλους.. ἐπειρήσαντ᾽ Ὀδυσῆος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.23): frequently in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, especially ἀλλήλων πειρᾶσθαι, as ἐπειρῶντο κατὰ τὸ ἰσχυρὸν ἀλλήλων 1.76; πειρᾶσθέ μου γυναικὸς ὡς ἀφράσμονος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1401, etc.}}; π. τῆς Πελοποννήσου **make an attempt** on it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.100; π. τοῦ τείχους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.81.}}
**B.II.2** with _genitive_ _of things_, **make proof** or **trial of**.., σθένεος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.359; ἥβης 23.432}}; χειρῶν καὶ σθένεος π., ἢ.. ἦ.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.282}}; **try one's chance at** or **in** a work or contest, ἔργου {{Refs|18.369}}; ἀέθλου, ἀέθλων, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.707, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.100, etc.; παλαιμοσύνης [prev. work] 126}}; **make proof of, try** a weapon, τόξου {{Refs|21.159, 180; νευρῆς [prev. work] 410 (but [ὀϊστοί,] τῶν τάχ᾽ ἔμελλον πειρήσεσθαι}} arrows whose force they were soon **to make trial of**, i. e.**feel**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 418)}}; also, **make proof of, have experience of**, especially in _perfect_ _passive_, first in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus, νηῶν “Op.” 660; οὐ πεπειρημένοι πρότερον [οἱ] Αἰγύπτιοι Ἑλλήνων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.159, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 118}}; πειρασάμενος ἀγαθῶν, δουλείας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.44, 5.69, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.1; κακῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.253}}; ὀρφανίας π., i.e. to be an orphan, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 49}}; but π. τινὸς μετρίου **find** him moderate **by experience** or **on trial**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 8, compare “Arat.” 43}}; also, πεπείρανται ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 27.2.}}
**B.II.3** _absolutely_, **try one's fortune, try the chances of war**, αἴ κε θεὸς πειρώμενος ἐνθάδ᾽ ἵκηται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.129}}; πειρώμενος ἢ ἐν ἀέθλῳ ἠὲ καὶ ἐν πολέμῳ **making trial of one's powers**, {{Refs|16.590}}; Ἕκτορι πειρηθῆναι ἀντιβίην, ἢ.. ἦ.. **to try one's fortune** against him, {{Refs|21.225}}; περὶ δ᾽ αὐτῆς πειρηθήτω (i.e. τῆς ἵππου) **let** him **try** for her, as a prize, {{Refs|23.553.}}
**B.III** **make a trial** or **put** a matter **to the test**, ἐν σοὶ πειρώμεθα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 21a: with _dative_ modi, ἐγχείῃ πειρήσομαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.279}}; ἐπειρήσαντο πόδεσσι **tried their luck** in the foot-race, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.120, compare 205; σφαίρῃ 8.377}}; also π. σὺν ἔντεσι, σὺν τεύχεσι π., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.220, 11.386: but in}}_perfect_, οὐδέ τί πω μύθοισι πεπείρημαι I **have** not **tried myself, have** not **found my skill**, in words, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.23: _absolutely_, ὁ πειραθεὶς πιστεύσει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 1.16}}; πεπειραμένος σαφῶς οἶδα **by experience**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hier.” 2.6.}}
**B.IV** with _accusative_ _person_, **make an attempt on**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(V.A.IV. 2), Διὸς ἄκοιτιν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.34.}}

3988:
** πεισμ-ονή**, ἡ,
**persuasion,** {{NT+6th c.AD+|NT.Gal.5.7, compare “PMag.Par.” 2.274, “PLond.” 5.1674.36 (6th c.AD).}}
**2** **confidence**, ἡ ἐξ ἀλλήλων πρὸς ἀλλήλους γινομένη π. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 299.17.}}
**II** **quality of a cable, pertinacity**, {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 28.24, 741.8, etc.}}

3989:
** πέλᾰγ-ος**, εος, τό,
_genitive_ _plural_ πελαγέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.85, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 702 (Lyric poetry), πελαγῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.24}}; _Epic dialect_ _dative_ πελάγεσσι (see. below):—**the sea**, especially **high sea, open sea**, π. μέγα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.16, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.179, etc.; ἐν πελάγεϊ ἀναπεπταμένῳ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.60}}.; διὰ πελάγους out at **sea**, opposed to παρὰ γῆν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.13}}: frequently coupled with other words denoting **sea**, ἁλὸς ἐν πελάγεσσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.335; π. θαλάσσης 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.608}}; π. πόντιον, πόντου π., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.56, “fragment” 235}}; ἅλιον π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 938 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** of parts of the sea (θάλασσα), frequently with geographical epithet, Αἰγαῖον π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 659, etc., compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.85 (π. Αἰγαίας ἁλός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 88, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 379); Ἰκαρίων ὑπὲρ πελαγέων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 702 (Lyric poetry), compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Icar.” 3; ἐκ μεγάλων πελαγῶν τοῦ τε Τυρσηνικοῦ καὶ τοῦ Σικελικοῦ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.24.}}
**3** **flooded plain**, γίνεται π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.97, compare 3.117.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, of any vast quantity, πλούτου π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 218}}; κακῶν π. a '**sea** of troubles', {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 433; π. ἀτηρᾶς δύης [prev. author] “Pr.” 746}}; ἄτης ἄβυσσον π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Supp.” 470; κακῶν π. εἰσορῶ τοσοῦτον ὥστε μήποτ᾽ ἐκνεῦσαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 822 (Lyric poetry); ἀληθινὸν εἰς π. αὑτὸν ἐμβαλεῖς.. πραγμάτων 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 65.6; φεύγειν εἰς τὸ π. τῶν λόγων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 338a; φανήσεται μακρὸν τὸ δεῦρο π. οὐδὲ πλώσιμον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 663}}; of great difficulties, μέγ᾽ ἄρα π. ἐλαχέτην τι{{Refs|[prev. work] 1746 (Lyric poetry).}}

3991:
** πέμπτος**, η, ον,
(πέντε) **fifth: with four others**, πέμπτος μετὰ τοῖσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.335; π. αὐτός 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.61, 3.19}}; π. σπιθαμή, i.e. four cubits and a span, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.106}}; ἐς π. μῆνα by **the fifth** month, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.77}}; τὸ πέμπτον μέρος **a fifth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36b, etc.}}; τὸ π., as _adverb_, **for the fifth time**, ὕπατοι, ὑπατεύων, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.77, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 19.}}
**II** ἡ πέμπτη (i.e. ἡμέρα) **the fifth day**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 802, 803, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1131.}}
**II.b** ἡ π. (i.e. ὥρα) **the fifth hour**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.1.29.}}
**II.2** ἡ π. (i.e. ὁδός), in the Roman camp, = **via quintana**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.30.6.}}
**II.3** ἡ π. tax **of one-fifth,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PLond. 3.1107.5, +others (3rd c.AD).}}
**III** τὸ π. σῶμα the **fifth** or celestial element, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 12, “Placita Philosophorum” 1.3.22; called π. στοιχεῖον [prev. work]2.6.2; π. οὐσία [prev. work]2.25.7, 6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 9.29.}}

3992:
** πέμπω**,
_Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ -έμεναι, -έμεν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.48, 10.18}}: _Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ πέμπεσκε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.106}}: _future_ πέμψω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.167, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ πεμψῶ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.141}}; _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ πεμψέμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.484:}}_aorist_ ἔπεμψα, _Epic dialect_ πέμψα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.442, 21.43, etc.}}: _perfect_ πέπομφα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.12, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.10, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.48}}: _pluperfect_ ἐπεμπόμφει, _Ionic dialect_ -εε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.9, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.85}}:—_middle_ (not in early Prose, except in compounds ἀπο-, μετα-, προ-πέμπομαι), _future_ πέμψομαι only _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 111}}: _aorist_ ἐπεμψάμην {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hec.” 977}}:—_passive_, _future_ πεμφθήσομαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.1.4, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 27}}: _aorist_ ἐπέμφθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.59, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1163, etc.}}: _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _perfect_ πέπεμπται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 473, (προ-) 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.77}}; _participle_ πεπεμμένος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.159, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 32, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 50.13}}: _pluperfect_ ἐπέπεμπτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 36.18, (προὐπ-) 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.79 (conjecture)}}:—**send**, frequently of persons, as messengers, spies, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.116, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 37, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.15, etc.}}; of troops, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 34 (_anapaest meter_), “Th.” 470: with double _accusative_, ὁδὸν π. τινά}} **send** one **on** a journey, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 739, compare “El.” 1163 (_passive_)}}; also of things, πέμψω δέ τοι οὖρον ὄπισθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.167, etc.}}; π. γράμματα, ἐπιστολήν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 310d, 323b}}; in letters, in the epistolary aorist, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.129, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.25, LXX.2Esd.4.14}}; π. κακόν τινι **send** one evil, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.109; π. παραβᾶσιν Ἐρινύν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 59 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ποινάς, ζημίαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Eu.” 203 (uncertain), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 506; ψόφον π. ἔσω [prev. author] “IT” 1308}}; ὕπνον, ὀνείρατα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 19, “El.” 460}}; frequently of omens, π.οἰωνόν, τέρατα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.310, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.15, compare “Smp.” 4.48; μαντείας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 149; also ἱκεσίους π. λιτάς [prev. author] “Ph.” 495}}; π. ἀρωγάς, ἀλκάν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 598, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 189 (Lyric poetry):}} —Constr.:
**1** with _accusative_ of place to which, π. τινὰ Θήβας, ἀγρούς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OC” 1770 (_anapaest meter_), “OT” 761: also with _dative_, Ἀΐδᾳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 159 (_anapaest meter_): but usually with Preps., ἐς Τροίην, φίλην ἐς πατρίδα γαῖαν, etc., 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.207, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.37, etc.; π. εἰς Ἀΐδαο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.48; δόμον Ἄϊδος εἴσω 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.524}}; π. εἰς διδασκάλων **send** to school, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325d (so πέμπειν alone, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 216)}}; π. ἐπ᾽ εὐρέα νῶτα θαλάσσης **over.**., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.560, etc.}}; π.ἐπὶ Θρῃκῶν ἵππους **to** them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.464}}; but πέμπειν ἐπί τι **send for** a purpose, ἐπ᾽ ὕδωρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.12; ἐπὶκατασκοπήν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.9 (π. εἰς κ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 45); π. ἀρωγὴν ἐπὶ νίκην 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 477 codices (_anapaest meter_)}}; π. ἐπί τινι **send to** him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.6}}; **against.**., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 61 (_anapaest meter_), etc.}}; **for** a purpose, ἐπὶ πολέμῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.17}}; περί τινος about something, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.91, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.10}}; ὑπέρ τινος Epist. Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 12.12}}; παρά or πρός τινα **to** some one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.81, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.6; ὥς τινα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.50.}}
**2** followed by _adverbs_, οἴκαδε, οἶκόνδε, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.281, 24.418; ὅνδε δόμονδε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.445; θύραζε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.461; πόλεμόνδε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.452, etc.}}; ἕταρον γὰρ.. πέμπ᾽ Ἄϊδόσδε **was conducting** or **convoying** Patroclus to Hades, {{Refs|23.137.}}
**3** followed by _infinitive_ of purpose, τὴν.. ἅρμασι π. νέεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.8; ἕπεσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.575; ἰέναι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.396; ἱκανέμεν 4.29; ἄγειν 24.419; φέρειν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.454}}; φέρεσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 681; μήτηρ με πέμπει πατρὶ τυμβεῦσαι χοάς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 406}}; **send word**, πέμπεις.. σῇ δάμαρτι, παῖδα σὴν δεῦρ᾽ ἀποστέλλειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 360}}; πέμπουσιν οἱ ἔφοροι.. στρατεύεσθαι **sent him orders** to march, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.7}}: also with _participle_, κήρυκας π. ἀγγέλλοντας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 12.76.22: the place from which is expressed by ἀπό or ἐκ, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.447, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.635, etc.}}
**4** _absolutely_, ἐπέμψαμεν πρὸς ὑμᾶς περὶ ἀποστάσεως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.13; πέμπει κελεύων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.91, 2.81; ἐκέλευε.. πέμπων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.1; ἔπεμπε πρὸς Κῦρον δεόμενος [prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.5.4; ἔπεμπον ἐρωτῶντες [prev. author] “An.” 6.6.4, etc.}}
**5** **send forward, nominate** a person for a post, ὀνόματα Wilcken {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Chr.” 28.20 (2nd c.AD):}} —_passive_ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work]392.7 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** **send forth** or **away, dismiss, send home**, τὸν ξεῖνον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.227, +others: less frequently in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”, as 24.780}}; χρὴ ξεῖνον παρεόντα φιλεῖν, ἐθέλοντα δὲ πέμπειν 'welcome the coming, **speed** the parting guest', {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.74; ὑπέδεκτο καὶ πέμπε 23.315}}; of the father who sends off his daughter to go to her husband, with _dative_,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.5; π. τινὰ ἄποικον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1518, etc.}}
**II.2** of missiles, **discharge, shoot**, πέτρας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 716: _metaphorically_, ὄμματος.. τόξευμα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1005: _absolutely_, οἱ πολλάκις πέμποντες ἔστιν ὅτε τυγχάνουσι τοῦ σκοποῦ 4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Vitae Sophistarum” p.495 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**II.3** of words, **send forth, utter**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 443, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 846 (Lyric poetry), 1445 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**III** **conduct, escort**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.390, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.336, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 571, etc.}}; frequently of Hermes and other gods, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.626, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 12, “Supp.” 219}}; ὁ πέμπων _absolutely_, of Hermes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 133 (compareπομπός, πομπαῖος, etc.)}}; of a ship, **convey, carry**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.556, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 136 (Lyric poetry); κραιπνοφόροι δέ μ᾽ ἔπεμψαν αὖραι [prev. author] “Pr.” 132 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.203 (_passive_).}}
**III.2** πομπὴν π. **conduct**, or **take part in**, a procession, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.56, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 757, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.56, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.80, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.26, etc.}}; π. χορούς **move in** dancing **procession**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 434 (Lyric poetry)}}; Παναθήναια π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 494, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 4.22:}} —_passive_, φαλλὸς Διονύσῳ πεμπόμενος **carried in procession** in his honour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.49, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 32, “Demetr.” 12; τῆς πομπῆς ὅπως ἂν ὡς κάλλισταπεμφθῇ “IG” 12.84.27; χορὸς ὁ εἰς Δῆλον πεμπόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.3.12.}}
**IV** **send** as a present, εἵματα, σῖτον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.83, 4.623}}; π. δῶρα, σκῦλα, ξένια, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.106 (_active_ and _passive_), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1429, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.42.}}
**V** **send up, produce**, ὅσα πέμπει βιόδωρος αἶα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1162 (Lyric poetry).}}
**B** _middle_, πέμπεσθαί τινα **send for** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 602}}, ubiv. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia; τί χρῆμ᾽ ἐπέμψω τὸν ἐμὸν ἐκ δόμων πόδ; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 977.}}
**B.II** **send in one's own service** or **cause to be sent**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 556, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 14.}}

3993:
** πένης**, ητος, ὁ,
(πένομαι) **one who works for his living, day-labourer, poor** man, opposed to πλούσιος, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 283}}; opposed to δυνάμενος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 3}}; πτωχοῦ μὲν γὰρ βίος.., ζῆν ἐστιν μηδὲν ἔχοντα· τοῦ δὲ πένητος ζῆν φει- δόμενον καὶ τοῖς ἔργοις προσέχοντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 553; οἱ π. αὐτῶν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.133, 2.47; πλούσιος ἐκ πένητος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.4; πένητες ἄνθρωποι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.51}}; **οἷ᾽** ἀνὴρ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 584; π. ἵππος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 11.5.}}
**II** as _adjective_, π. δόμοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1139: with _neuter_, ἐν πένητι σώματι [prev. work] 372: with _genitive_, χρημάτων πένητες}} **poor in** money,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 38; π. φίλων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 332c; π. ἀπολογίας 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Apol.” 11}}: _comparative_ πενέστερος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 1.13}}: _superlative_ πενέστατος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.123.}}

3995:
** πενθερός**, ὁ,
**father-in-law**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.170, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.582, Legal Draconisap. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.57 (_plural_), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.52, “PCair.Zen.” 369.2 (3rd c.BC)}}; λαβὼν Ἄδραστον π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1302}}: in _plural_, **parents-in-law**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 636.}}
**II** generally, **connexion by marriage**, e.g. **brother-in-law**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 1286}}; also, ={γαμβρός}, **son-in-law**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 305 (_plural_)}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _bándhus_ 'kinsman', Lithuanian _beñdras_ 'comrade', Gothic _bindan_ 'bind'.)

3996:
** πενθ-έω**,
_Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _dual_ πενθείετον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.283}}; _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ πενθήμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.174, 19.120}}: _future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 207}}: _aorist_ ἐπένθησα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ch.” 173, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.211}}: _perfect_ πεπένθηκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Demon.” 25, (συμ-) 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.33}}: (πένθος):— **bewail, lament**, especially for persons, νέκυν πενθῆσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.225, compare “Tragica Adespota” 331; πενθέειν τινα ὡς τεθνεῶτα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.95; π. γόοις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 545 (_anapaest meter_); π. τινὰς δημοσίᾳ 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.66; π. τινὰ τριχί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 173; ἐπί τινι π. καὶ κείρασθαι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.211}}: _absolutely_, **mourn, go into mourning**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 258b, etc.: with _accusative_ _cognate_, πενθεῖ νέον οἶκτον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 64}}:—_passive_, **to be mourned for**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.27; πένθος ἀμφί τινι πενθεῖται 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 33.4.}}
**2** of things, π. κακά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1320, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.2; πήματα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 739; τύχας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 268.}}

3997:
** πένθος**, εος, τό,
**grief, sorrow**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.658, etc.}}; τινος **for** one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.324, etc.; π. ἄλαστον ἔχουσα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.105; π. λαγχάνειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 659}}; Τρῶας λάβε π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.548, etc.; μέγα π. Ἀχαιίδα γαῖαν ἱκάνει 1.254, etc.; πένθεϊ δ᾽ ἀτλήτῳ βεβολήατο πάντες 9.3; θυμὸς ἐτείρετο π. λυγρῷ 22.242, etc.}}
**2** especially of grief for the dead, **mourning**, τοκεῦσι γόον καὶ π. ἔθηκας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|17.37; παιδὸς γάρ οἱ ἄλαστον ἐνὶ φρεσὶ π. ἔκειτο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.423; Σάρδεσι π. παρασχών 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 322; δμῳαῖς προθήσειν π. οἰκεῖον στένειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1249}}; π. ποιήσασθαι make **a public mourning**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.1; so π. προεθήκαντο [prev. author] 6.21; π. τίθεται [prev. author] 2.46; π. τινὸς κοινοῦσθαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 426}}; ἐν πένθει [εἶναι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 290, 847 (Lyric poetry); πολὺ π. ἦν κατὰ τὸ στράτευμα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.5.10; π. λιπεῖν “IG” 3.1311}}; π. λύεσθαι, ἀποθέσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 18, “Alex.” 75: in _plural_, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).6, “fragment” 154, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 333 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 395e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1370b25, etc.}}
**II** **unhappy event, misfortune**, π. τινός one's **illfortune**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.14; ἔτλαν π. οὐ φατόν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 7(6).37.}}
**III** of a person, **a misery**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 615 (Lyric poetry)}}; π. ἔδωκε φέρειν, i. e. the body, {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 228.6 (from Ephesus). (Cf. πείσομαι from *πένθ-σομαι}}_future_ of πάσχω, _perfect_ πείσονθα, Lithuanian _kenčiù_ 'suffer', **pa-kantà** 'patience'; πενθ- is reduced to πṇθ- (παθ-) in αἰνο-παθής, etc.)
